\label{remark2}
Following a demonstration in \cite{lam2024matrix}, using the notations in (\ref{eqn:combined_main_effects}), model (\ref{eqn:MMEFM}) can be written as a multilevel matrix FM as follows:
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)} &= \widetilde{\mathbf{Q}}_m\widetilde{\mathbf{W}}_t^{(m)}\widetilde{\mathbf{J}}_m' + \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}, \;\;\; \text{ where}\\
    \widetilde{\mathbf{Q}}_m &:= (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}, \mathbf{Q}_m, \mathbf{R}_m, \1_{p_m}), \;\;\; \widetilde{\mathbf{J}}_m := (\1_{q_m}, \mathbf{J}_m, \mathbf{C}_m, \mathbf{I}_{q_m}),  \;\;\;
    \widetilde{\mathbf{W}}_t^{(m)} :=  \left(
  \begin{array}{cccc}
    \mathbf{a}_t^{(m)} & \0 & \0 & \0 \\
    \0 & \mathbf{G}_t & \0 & \0 \\
    \0 & \0 & \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} & \0 \\
    c_t^{(m)} & \0 & \0 & \mathbf{b}_t^{(m)'} \\
  \end{array}
\right).
\end{align*}
Ignoring the above structure means that we are estimating $(p_m + k_r + k_r^{(m)} + 1)(q_m + k_c + k_c^{(m)} + 1)$ time series in $\widetilde{\mathbf{W}}_t^{(m)}$, which tends to overfit the data because $p_mq_m$ is usually too large relative to $T$.

On the other hand, consider a multilevel matrix FM given by $\Acute{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)} = \Acute{\mathbf{C}}_t + \Acute{\mathbf{E}}_t$ for $m\in[M]$,  where \mbox{$\Acute{\mathbf{C}}_t := \mathbf{Q}_m\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{J}_m' + \mathbf{R}_m\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{C}_m'$}. Defining $\mathbf{M}_n := \mathbf{I}_n - n^{-1}\1_n\1_n'$, we construct
\[c_t^{(m)} := (p_mq_m)^{-1}\1_{p_m}'\Acute{\mathbf{C}}_t\1_{q_m}, \;\; \mathbf{a}_t^{(m)} := q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\Acute{\mathbf{C}}_t\1_{q_m}, \;\;\; \mathbf{b}_t^{(m)} := p_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\Acute{\mathbf{C}}'_t\1_{p_m}.\]
Then $\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}$ can be expressed as
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)} &= c_t^{(m)}\1_{p_m}\1_{q_m}' + \mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}' + \1_{p_m}\mathbf{b}_t^{(m)'} + (\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{Q}_m)\mathbf{G}_t(\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\mathbf{J}_m)' +  (\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{R}_m)\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)}(\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\mathbf{C}_m)' + \Acute{\mathbf{E}}_t,
\end{align*}
which is a multilevel MEFM in (\ref{eqn:MMEFM}), with grand means and main effects given by
\begin{align*}
    \mu_t &= (p_mq_m)^{-1}\1_{p_m}'\mathbf{Q}_m\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{J}_m'\1_{q_m}, \; \mu_t^{(m)} = (p_mq_m)^{-1}\1_{p_m}'\mathbf{R}_m\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{C}_m'\1_{q_m}, \; \boldsymbol{\alpha}_t = \mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{J}_m'\1_{q_m}, \\ \boldsymbol{\beta}_t&=\mathbf{G}_t'\mathbf{Q}_m'\1_{p_m}, \;    \boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)} = \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{C}_m'\1_{q_m}, \; \boldsymbol{\beta}_t^{(m)} = \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{R}_m'\1_{p_m},
\end{align*}
and the associated main effect loading matrices for $\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t$, $\boldsymbol{\beta}_t$, $\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}$ and $\boldsymbol{\beta}_t^{(m)}$ given respectively by
\[\mathbf{A}_{1m} = q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{Q}_m, \;\; \mathbf{B}_{1m} = p_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\mathbf{J}_m, \;\;  \mathbf{A}_{2m} = q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{R}_m, \;\; \mathbf{B}_{2m} = p_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\mathbf{C}_m.\]
Two important observations are that all factor loading matrices are now one rank less because of the multiplication of $\mathbf{M}_{p_m}$ and $\mathbf{M}_{q_m}$, and $\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}$ and $\boldsymbol{\beta}_t^{(m)}$ are now of length $k_r^{(m)}$ and $k_c^{(m)}$ rather than being $r_2^{(m)}$ and $\ell_2^{(m)}$ respectively in model~\eqref{eqn:MMEFM}. Since $r_2^{(m)}$ and $\ell_2^{(m)}$ can be larger than $k_r^{(m)}$ and $k_c^{(m)}$, a multilevel MEFM is certainly more flexible than a multilevel matrix FM.

\label{remark3}
We stress that the grand mean time series $\{c_t^{(m)}\}$ and the main effects time series $\{\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\}$ and $\{\mathbf{b}_t^{(m)}\}$ themselves can be nonstationary, which is an important feature for MEFM in \cite{lam2024matrix}.
The global or local grand means and main effects can also be nonstationary, as long as Conditions (ME1) and (ME2) in Section \ref{subsec:assumption} are satisfied.

\label{remark:global_common_alignment}
The two-sided alignment step is needed because, for each $m$, $\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m'\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_{G,t}^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_m$ is expressed in group-specific row and column coordinates. In particular, if $\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m'\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_{G,t}^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_m\approx\mathbf{L}_m\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{S}_m'$, then direct averaging yields $M^{-1}\sum_{m=1}^M\mathbf{L}_m\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{S}_m'$, while $M^{-1}\sum_{m=1}^M(\mathbf{S}_m\otimes\mathbf{L}_m)$ need not be a Kronecker product. Hence, the average generally cannot be written as $\mathbf{L}\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{S}'$ for a common pair $(\mathbf{L},\mathbf{S})$, and the resulting coordinate change cannot in general be absorbed by the subsequent two-sided least-squares refitting.

At the population level, $\mathbf{G}_t$ is represented in the coordinate systems of group $m$ and $m_0$ as $\mathbf{L}_m\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{S}_m'$ and $\mathbf{L}_{m_0}\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{S}_{m_0}'$, respectively. The vectorized coordinate change from group $m$ to group $m_0$ is then $(\mathbf{S}_{m_0}\mathbf{S}_m^{-1})\otimes(\mathbf{L}_{m_0}\mathbf{L}_m^{-1})$, since applying this transformation to $\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{L}_m\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{S}_m')$ yields $\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{L}_{m_0}\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{S}_{m_0}')$. Under the rearrangement operator,
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{R}\left((\mathbf{S}_{m_0}\mathbf{S}_m^{-1})\otimes(\mathbf{L}_{m_0}\mathbf{L}_m^{-1})\right)
    =\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{L}_{m_0}\mathbf{L}_m^{-1})\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{S}_{m_0}\mathbf{S}_m^{-1})'.
\end{align*}
Hence, the leading left and right singular vectors of $\mathcal{R}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Xi}}_m)$ estimate the row and column alignment matrices, bringing the representation from group $m$ into the coordinate system of group $m_0$ before averaging.

\label{thm:main_effect}
    Let Assumptions~(IC1), (IC2), (E1) and (E2) hold. Then, for each $m\in[M]$ and $t\in[T]$, 
    \begin{align*}
        (\widehat{c}_t^{(m)} - c_t^{(m)})^2 = O_P\Big(\frac{1}{p_mq_m}\Big), \quad\;
        \frac{1}{p_m}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{a}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2 = O_P\Big(\frac{1}{q_m}\Big), \quad\;
        \frac{1}{q_m}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{b}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{b}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2 = O_P\Big(\frac{1}{p_m}\Big).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, for each $m\in[M]$ and $t\in[T]$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \big(\widehat{\mu}_t - \mu_t\big)^2 
        = O_P\Big(\max_{m\in[M]}\frac{1}{p_mq_m}\Big), \qquad
        \big(\widehat{\mu}_t^{(m)} - \mu_t^{(m)}\big)^2 
        = O_P\Big(\max_{m\in[M]}\frac{1}{p_mq_m}\Big).
    \end{align*}

\label{thm:initial_A1m}
    Let the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:main_effect} hold. Further assume Assumptions~(ME1), (ME2), (R1) and (S1). For each $m\in[M]$, define $\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}:=\mathbf{A}_{1m}(\mathbf{A}_{1m}'\mathbf{A}_{1m})^{-1/2}$ and $\widecheck{\mathbf{B}}_{1m}:=\mathbf{B}_{1m}(\mathbf{B}_{1m}'\mathbf{B}_{1m})^{-1/2}$. Then there exist asymptotically orthogonal matrices $\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{B_1,m}$ with $\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}'=\mathbf{I}+o_P(1)$ and $\mathbf{H}_{B_1,m}\mathbf{H}_{B_1,m}'=\mathbf{I}+o_P(1)$, such that
    \begin{align*}
        \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2
        &= O_P(\eta_{A_{1m}}), \; \eta_{A_{1m}} := R(T,d_{r,\star})\nu_{r,m}
        \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{r,n}
        +\frac{p_m^{1-\gamma_r}}{Tq_m}
        \big(1\vee \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{r,n}\big), \\
        \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{B}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{B}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{B_1,m} \big\|_F^2
        &= O_P(\eta_{B_{1m}}), \;  \eta_{B_{1m}} := R(T,d_{c,\star})\nu_{c,m}
        \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{c,n}
        +\frac{q_m^{1-\gamma_c}}{Tp_m}
        \big(1\vee \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{c,n}\big).
    \end{align*}

\label{thm:A2m_alphatm}
    Let the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m} hold. For each $m\in[M]$, define $\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}:=\mathbf{A}_{2m}(\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{A}_{2m})^{-1/2}$ and $\widecheck{\mathbf{B}}_{2m}:=\mathbf{B}_{2m}(\mathbf{B}_{2m}'\mathbf{B}_{2m})^{-1/2}$. Then there exist asymptotically orthogonal matrices $\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{B_2,m}$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
        =O_P\Big(&R(T,d_{r,\star})\frac{\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{r,n}}{\nu_{r,m}}
        +\frac{p_m^{1-\gamma_r}}{Tq_m}
        \frac{\big(1\vee \max_{n\in[M]}\nu_{r,n}\big)}{\nu_{r,m}^2}
        +\frac{p_m^{1-2\gamma_r^{(m)}}}{q_m^2}\Big), \\
        \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{B}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{B}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{B_2,m} \big\|_F^2
        =O_P\Big(&R(T,d_{c,\star})\frac{\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{c,n}}{\nu_{c,m}}
        +\frac{q_m^{1-\gamma_c}}{Tp_m}
        \frac{\big(1\vee \max_{n\in[M]}\nu_{c,n}\big)}{\nu_{c,m}^2}
        +\frac{q_m^{1-2\gamma_c^{(m)}}}{p_m^2}\Big).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, define $\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}:=(\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{A}_{2m})^{1/2}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}$ and $\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_t^{(m)}:=(\mathbf{B}_{2m}'\mathbf{B}_{2m})^{1/2}\boldsymbol{\beta}_t^{(m)}$. Then,
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{1}{p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}}
        \big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2
        =O_P\Big(&\frac{1}{p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m}
        +R(T,d_{r,\star})\frac{\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{r,n}}{1\wedge \nu_{r,m}}
        +\frac{p_m^{1-\gamma_r^{(m)}}}{Tq_m}
        \frac{1\vee \max_{n\in[M]}\nu_{r,n}}{1\wedge \nu_{r,m}} \Big), \\
        \frac{1}{q_m^{\gamma_c^{(m)}}}
        \big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{H}_{B_2,m}^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2
        =O_P\Big(&\frac{1}{q_m^{\gamma_c^{(m)}}p_m}
        +R(T,d_{c,\star})\frac{\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{c,n}}{1\wedge \nu_{c,m}}
        +\frac{q_m^{1-\gamma_c^{(m)}}}{Tp_m}
        \frac{1\vee \max_{n\in[M]}\nu_{c,n}}{1\wedge \nu_{c,m}} \Big).
    \end{align*}

\label{thm:A1m_alphat}
    Let the assumptions in Theorem~\ref{thm:A2m_alphatm} hold. Define $\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t:=(T^{-1}\sum_{s=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_s\boldsymbol{\alpha}_s')^{-1/2}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t$ and $\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_t:=(T^{-1}\sum_{s=1}^T\boldsymbol{\beta}_s\boldsymbol{\beta}_s')^{-1/2}\boldsymbol{\beta}_t$. Then there exist asymptotically orthogonal matrices $\mathbf{H}_\alpha$ and $\mathbf{H}_\beta$ such that, for each $t\in[T]$,
    \begin{align*}
        \big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t-\mathbf{H}_\alpha^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t \big\|^2
        =O_P\Big(\max_{m\in[M]}\Big\{
        &\frac{1}{p_m^{\gamma_r}q_m}
        +R(T,d_{r,\star})(1\vee\nu_{r,m})\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{r,n}
        +\frac{p_m^{1-\gamma_r}}{Tq_m}
        \frac{1\vee\max_{n\in[M]}\nu_{r,n}}{1\wedge\nu_{r,m}} \Big\}\Big), \\
        \big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_t-\mathbf{H}_\beta^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\beta}}_t \big\|^2
        =O_P\Big(\max_{m\in[M]}\Big\{
        &\frac{1}{q_m^{\gamma_c}p_m}
        +R(T,d_{c,\star})(1\vee\nu_{c,m})\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{c,n}
        +\frac{q_m^{1-\gamma_c}}{Tp_m}
        \frac{1\vee\max_{n\in[M]}\nu_{c,n}}{1\wedge\nu_{c,m}}\Big\}\Big).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, let $\eta_{A_{1m}}$ and $\eta_{B_{1m}}$ denote the rates in Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m}, and $\eta_\alpha$ and $\eta_\beta$ denote the rates in the two factor bounds above. Define $\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}:=\mathbf{A}_{1m}(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t')^{1/2}$ and $\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{B}}}_{1m}:=\mathbf{B}_{1m}(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\beta}_t\boldsymbol{\beta}_t')^{1/2}$. Then,
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{1}{p_m^{\gamma_r}}
        \big\| \widetilde{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_\alpha \big\|_F^2
        =O_P\Big(\eta_{A_{1m}}
        +\Big\{\frac{p_m^{1-\gamma_r}}{q_m}
        +R(T,d_{r,\star})(1\vee\nu_{r,m})
        \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{r,n}\Big\}\eta_\alpha\Big), \\
        \frac{1}{q_m^{\gamma_c}}
        \big\| \widetilde{\mathbf{B}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{B}}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_\beta \big\|_F^2
        =O_P\Big(\eta_{B_{1m}}
        +\Big\{\frac{q_m^{1-\gamma_c}}{p_m}
        +R(T,d_{c,\star})(1\vee\nu_{c,m})
        \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{c,n}\Big\}\eta_\beta\Big).
    \end{align*}

\label{thm:common_comp_global_local_init}
Let Assumptions~(IC1), (E1), (E2), (F1), (L1), (L2), and (R2) hold.
Define $\widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m := \mathbf{R}_m (\mathbf{R}_m'\mathbf{R}_m)^{-1/2}$, $\widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m := \mathbf{C}_m (\mathbf{C}_m'\mathbf{C}_m)^{-1/2}$,
and
\begin{align*}
    \eta_{Q,m} &:= 
    R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}) \;
    p_m^{2 (\delta_{q,1}^{(m)} -\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)})}
    \Bigg( \frac{  \max_{n\in[M] \setminus \{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}}{\max_{n\in[M] \setminus\{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}} \Bigg)^2 , \\
    \eta_{J,m} &:=
    R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}) \;
    q_m^{2 (\delta_{j,1}^{(m)} -\delta_{j,k_c}^{(m)})}
    \Bigg( \frac{  \max_{n\in[M] \setminus \{m\}} s_{Q,n}^2 q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}}{\max_{n\in[M] \setminus\{m\}} s_{Q,n}^2 q_n^{\delta_{j,k_c}^{(n)}}} \Bigg)^2 .
\end{align*}
Further assume $\eta_{Q,m}, \eta_{J,m} = o(1)$.
Then there exist asymptotically orthogonal matrices $\mathbf{H}_{R,m}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{C,m}$ with $\mathbf{H}_{R,m} \mathbf{H}_{R,m}'=\mathbf{I}+o_P(1)$ and $\mathbf{H}_{C,m} \mathbf{H}_{C,m}'=\mathbf{I}+o_P(1)$,
such that
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\quad\quad
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m - \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m \mathbf{H}_{R,m} \big\|_F^2 = O_P(\Delta_{R,m}) , \;\quad
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m - \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big\|_F^2 = O_P(\Delta_{C,m}) ,
    \quad
    \text{where}  \\
    \Delta_{R,m} &:=
    p_m^{-2 \delta_{r,k_r^{(m)}}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{-2 \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} 
    \Bigg\{
    \big(p_m^{2 \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} q_m^{2 \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} 
    + p_m^{1 + \delta_{q,1}^{(m)}} q_m^{1 + \delta_{j,1}^{(m)}} \big) \eta_{J,m}
    + \frac{p_m^{1 + \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} q_m^{1 + \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}}{T} 
    + p_m^{2(1 - \delta_{r,1}^{(m)})}
    + \frac{p_m^2 q_m}{T} 
    \Bigg\} , 
    \\
    \Delta_{C,m} &:=
    q_m^{-2 \delta_{c,k_c^{(m)}}^{(m)}}
    p_m^{-2 \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} 
    \Bigg\{
    \big(q_m^{2 \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} p_m^{2 \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} 
    + q_m^{1 + \delta_{j,1}^{(m)}} p_m^{1 + \delta_{q,1}^{(m)}} \big) \eta_{Q,m}
    + \frac{q_m^{1 + \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} p_m^{1 + \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}}{T} 
    + q_m^{2(1 - \delta_{c,1}^{(m)})}
    + \frac{q_m^2 p_m}{T} 
    \Bigg\} .
\end{align*}
Further let Assumption~(S1) hold,
and define $\widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} := \big( \mathbf{R}_m'\mathbf{R}_m \big)^{1/2} \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \big( \mathbf{C}_m'\mathbf{C}_m \big)^{1/2}$.
Then
\begin{align*}
    &\quad
    \frac{1}{
    p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} 
    q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    }
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big\|_F^2
    = 
    O_P( \Delta_{R,m} + \Delta_{C,m} 
    + \Delta_{F,m} ) ,
    \quad \text{where} 
    \\
    \Delta_{F,m} &:=
    \min\Big( 
    q_m^{1 - 2\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    p_m^{1 - \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    \Delta_{R,m}
    + 
    q_m^{1 - 2\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    p_m^{ - \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} 
    ,
    p_m^{1 - 2\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{1 - \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \Delta_{C,m}
    +
    p_m^{1 - 2\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{ - \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \Big) .
\end{align*}

\label{thm:common_comp_global}
Let all assumptions in Theorem~\ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init} hold.
Let $m_0\in[M]$ be a fixed reference group, and define $\widecheck{\mathbf{G}}_t := \big( \mathbf{Q}_{m_0}'\mathbf{Q}_{m_0} \big)^{1/2} \mathbf{G}_t \big( \mathbf{J}_{m_0}'\mathbf{J}_{m_0} \big)^{1/2}$.
For any $m\in[M]$, define
\begin{align*}
    \Delta_{G,m} &:= 
    \min(p_m,q_m)
    + p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \big( \Delta_{R,m} + \Delta_{C,m} + \Delta_{F,m} \big)
    + \big( \eta_{Q,m} + \eta_{J,m} \big) p_m q_m , \\
    S_m &:= \lambda_{\min}\big( \mathbf{Q}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m \big) \lambda_{\min}\big( \mathbf{J}_m'\mathbf{J}_m \big) 
    \asymp 
    p_m^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)}} q_m^{\delta_{j,k_c}^{(m)}} .
\end{align*}
and assume $\Delta_{G,m} = o\big\{ \lambda_{\min}\big( \mathbf{Q}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m \big) \lambda_{\min}\big( \mathbf{J}_m'\mathbf{J}_m \big) \big\}$.
Further assume that the reference group $m_0$ is chosen such that for any $m\in[M]$,
$\lambda_{\min}\big( \mathbf{Q}_{m_0}'\mathbf{Q}_{m_0} \big) \lambda_{\min}\big( \mathbf{J}_{m_0}'\mathbf{J}_{m_0} \big)
= O\big\{ \lambda_{\min}\big( \mathbf{Q}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m \big) \lambda_{\min}\big( \mathbf{J}_m'\mathbf{J}_m \big) \big\}$.
Then there exist asymptotically orthogonal matrices $\mathbf{H}_{Q,m_0}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{J,m_0}$ such that
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{G}}_t - \mathbf{H}_{Q,m_0}' \widecheck{\mathbf{G}}_t \mathbf{H}_{J,m_0} \big\|_F^2
    = O_P(\Delta_G) ,
    \quad \text{where} 
    \quad
    \Delta_G :=
    \max_{m\in[M]} \Delta_{G,m}
    + \Big( \max_{m'\in[M]} \frac{\Delta_{G,m'}}{S_{m'}} \Big) \max_{m\in[M]} S_m .
\end{align*}
Also, we have
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widetilde{\mathbf{Q}}_m - \mathbf{Q}_m \big( \mathbf{Q}_{m_0}' \mathbf{Q}_{m_0} \big)^{-1/2} \mathbf{H}_{Q,m_0} \big\|_F^2
    =  O_P(\Delta_{K,m}),
    \quad \text{where} 
    \quad
    \Delta_{K,m} := 
    \Delta_G \frac{ p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}} }{ p_{m_0}^{2 \delta_{q,k_r}^{(m_0)}}
    q_{m_0}^{2 \delta_{j,k_c}^{(m_0)}} }
    + \frac{ p_m q_m}{T S_{m_0}} ,
\end{align*}
and similarly $\big\| \widetilde{\mathbf{J}}_m - \mathbf{J}_m \big( \mathbf{J}_{m_0}' \mathbf{J}_{m_0} \big)^{-1/2} \mathbf{H}_{J,m_0} \big\|_F^2 = O_P(\Delta_{K,m})$.

\label{thm:global_factor_detection}
Let all the conditions in Theorem \ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init} hold. Define the global active set as 
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{A}_G
    :=\left\{m\in[M]:\text{there exists } n\neq m\in[M] \text{ such that } \{\mathbf{Q}_m\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{J}_m'\}_{t\in[T]} \text{ and } \{\mathbf{Q}_n\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{J}_n'\}_{t\in[T]} \text{ are nonzero}\right\},
    \label{eqn:active_set}
\end{align}
and the statistics
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:S_G}
S_{G,m} := \frac{\sum_{n=1;n\neq m}^M \max_{c_m, c_n\in[k_r], d_m,d_n\in[k_c]}\Big\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(n)'}\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}\Big\}^2}{\max_{c_m\in[k_r+k_r^{(m)}], d_m\in[k_c + k_c^{(m)}]}\Big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{V}}_{m,\cdot d_m}\widehat{\mathbf{V}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)'}\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_{m,\cdot c_m}\Big)^2}, \;\;\; m\in[M].
\end{equation}
Then for any $m \in \mathcal{A}_G$ and $k\notin \mathcal{A}_G$, assuming 
\[\max_{n\neq m,n\in\mathcal{A}_G}p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,k_c}^{(n)}}\gtrsim \max(\max_{n\neq m,n\in\mathcal{A}_G}p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}, \max_{n\notin \mathcal{A}_G}p_n^{\delta_{r,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{c,1}^{(n)}})R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}),\]
we have
\begin{align*}
S_{G,m} &\gtrsim_P \frac{\max_{n\neq m,n\in\mathcal{A}_G}p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,k_c}^{(n)}}}{p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}}, \;\text{ and }\;\\
S_{G,k} &\lesssim_P \frac{\max\big(\max_{n\in\mathcal{A}_G}p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}, \max_{n\neq k,n\notin\mathcal{A}_G}p_n^{\delta_{r,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{c,1}^{(n)}}\big)}{p_k^{\delta_{r,1}^{(k)}}q_k^{\delta_{c,1}^{(k)}}}R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}),\end{align*}
where $R(\cdot,\cdot)$ is defined in (\ref{eqn:R(T,d)}) and $d_{\text{fac},\star}$ in Assumption {\em (R2)}. In particular, if $p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}} \asymp p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1} ^{(n)}} \asymp p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,k_c}^{(n)}}$ for any $n, m \in \mathcal{A}_G$, and $\max_{n\in[M]}(p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}, p_n^{\delta_{r,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{c,1}^{(n)}})R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}) = o(\min_{k\notin\mathcal{A}_G}p_k^{\delta_{r,1}^{(k)}}q_k^{\delta_{c,1}^{(k)}})$, then
\[S_{G,m} \asymp_P 1, \;\;\; S_{G,k} = o_P(1).\]

\label{thm:rank_estimation}
Let all the conditions in Theorem \ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init} hold. Then for each $m\in[M]$, as $\min(T,p_m,q_m) \rightarrow \infty$,
\[P(\widehat{r}_1 \geq r_1) \rightarrow 1, \;\; P(\widehat{\ell}_1 \geq \ell_1) \rightarrow 1, \;\; P(\widehat{r}_2^{(m)} \geq r_2^{(m)}) \rightarrow 1, \;\; P(\widehat{\ell}_2^{(m)} \geq \ell_2^{(m)}) \rightarrow 1,\]
if $\eta_{A_{1m}} = o(\nu_{r,m}^2)$ and $\eta_{B_{1m}} = o(\nu_{c,m}^2)$, where $\eta_{A_{1m}}$ and $\eta_{B_{1m}}$ are the rates defined in Theorem \ref{thm:initial_A1m}.
Moreover, we also have $P(\widehat{k}_r \geq k_r) \rightarrow 1$ and $P(\widehat{k}_c \geq k_c) \rightarrow 1$ if
\begin{align*}
&\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}\cdot\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}\big[T^{-1}p_mq_mp_nq_n + p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star})\big] \\
&=o\Big(\min\big(\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m} p_m^{2\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)}}q_m^{2\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}p_n^{2\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}q_n^{2\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}, 
\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m} p_m^{2\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{2\delta_{j,k_c}^{(m)}}p_n^{2\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{2\delta_{j,k_c}^{(n)}}\big)\Big).
\end{align*}
Finally, we also have $P(\widehat{k}_r^{(m)} \geq k_r^{(m)}) \rightarrow 1$ and $P(\widehat{k}_c^{(m)} \geq k_c^{(m)}) \rightarrow 1$ if
\begin{align*}
    q_m^{2(1-\delta_{c,1}^{(m)})} + T^{-1}q_m^2p_m + q_m^{1+\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}p_m^{1+\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}\eta_{Q,m} &= o(q_m^{4\delta_{c,k_c^{(m)}}^{(m)}-2\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}p_m^{2\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}),\\
    p_m^{2(1-\delta_{r,1}^{(m)})} + T^{-1}p_m^2q_m + p_m^{1+\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{1+\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}\eta_{J,m} &= o(p_m^{4\delta_{r,k_r^{(m)}}^{(m)}-2\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{2\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}).
\end{align*}
If all factors are strong and all processes have short memory, the greater than or equal sign in all asymptotic probabilities above will become the equal sign, if we add the following terms to the numerator and denominator of the eigenvalue ratios defining $\widehat{r}_1$, $\widehat{r}_2^{(m)}$, $\widehat{\ell}_1$, $\widehat{\ell}_2^{(m)}$, $\widehat{k}_r$, $\widehat{k}_c$, $\widehat{k}_r^{(m)}$ and $\widehat{k}_c^{(m)}$ respectively ($C$ is a generic constant):
\begin{align*}
&\max_{m\neq n\in[M]}CT^{-1}p_mp_n, \; CT^{-1}p_m^2, \; \max_{m\neq n\in[M]}CT^{-1}q_mq_n, \;  CT^{-1}q_m^2\;\\
&\max_{m\neq n\in[M]} CT^{-1}p_mq_mp_nq_n, \; \max_{m\neq n\in[M]} CT^{-1}p_mq_mp_nq_n, \;  CT^{-1/2}p_mq_m, \; CT^{-1/2}p_mq_m.
\end{align*}

We first derive the convergence rate for grand mean and its corresponding global and local components. By left- and right-multiplying $\1_{p_m}'$ and $\1_{q_m}$ on \eqref{eqn:MMEFM}, we have
\begin{align}\label{eqn:left_right_multiply_ones_MMEFM}
    \1_{p_m}'\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} = p_mq_mc_t^{(m)} + \1_{p_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}.
\end{align}
Thus, 
\begin{align}
    \mathbbm{E}(\widehat{c}_t^{(m)} - c_t^{(m)})^2 &= (p_mq_m)^{-2}\mathbbm{E}(\1_{p_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m})^2 = (p_mq_m)^{-2}\mathbbm{E}\big(\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{j=1}^{q_m}E_{t,ij}^{(m)}\big)^2 \nonumber \\
    &= (p_mq_m)^{-2}\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{p_m}\sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{h=1}^{q_m}\mathbbm{E}(E_{t,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,lh}^{(m)}) = O(p_m^{-1}q_m^{-1}), \label{eqn:sum_of_error_rate}
\end{align}
where we apply Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm} in the last equality. Hence $|\widehat{c}_t^{(m)} - c_t^{(m)}| = O_P\big((p_mq_m)^{-1/2}\big)$. Taking sum on both sides of \eqref{eqn:left_right_multiply_ones_MMEFM} \text{w.r.t.} $m$, we then have $\mu_t = M^{-1}\sum_{m=1}^M(p_mq_m)^{-1}\1_{p_m}'(\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)})\1_{q_m}$. Therefore, since $\widehat{\mu}_t = M^{-1}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\widehat{c}_t^{(m)}$, we have
\begin{align*}
    (\widehat{\mu}_t - \mu_t)^2 
    &\lesssim \big(\sum_{m=1}^{M}(p_mq_m)^{-1}\1_{p_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\big)^2 
    \lesssim \sum_{m=1}^{M}\big((p_mq_m)^{-1}\1_{p_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\big)^2 \\
    &= \sum_{m=1}^M\big(\widehat{c}_t^{(m)} - c_t^{(m)}\big)^2 
    = \sum_{m=1}^{M}O_P\big((p_mq_m)^{-1}\big) = O_P\Big(\max_{m\in[M]}(p_mq_m)^{-1}\Big).
\end{align*}
Furthermore, as $\widehat{\mu}_t^{(m)} = \widehat{c}_t^{(m)} - \widehat{\mu}_t$ and $\mu_t^{(m)} = c_t^{(m)} - \mu_t$, for each $m\in[M]$, 
\begin{align*}
    \big(\widehat{\mu}_t^{(m)}-\mu_t^{(m)}\big)^2
    &\lesssim \big(\widehat{c}_t^{(m)}-c_t^{(m)}\big)^2
    +\big(\widehat{\mu}_t-\mu_t\big)^2
    =O_P\Big(\max_{n\in[M]}(p_nq_n)^{-1}\Big),
\end{align*}
which is the stated rate.



By \eqref{eqn:main_effects}, the definitions of $\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{b}}_t^{(m)}$, and (IC1), we have
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{a}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2 
    = q_m^{-2}\big\| \mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} \big\|^2 
    = q_m^{-2}\big\| \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} - p_m^{-1}\1_{p_m}\1_{p_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} \big\|^2.
\end{align*}
For the first part, by Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm},
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\big\| \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} \big\|^2
    =\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{h=1}^{q_m}
    \mathbbm{E}(E_{t,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,ih}^{(m)})
    \le\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{j=1}^{q_m}
    \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{h=1}^{q_m}
    |\mathbbm{E}(E_{t,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,kh}^{(m)})|
    =O(p_mq_m).
\end{align*}
And for the second part, write
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\big\| p_m^{-1}\1_{p_m}\1_{p_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} \big\|^2 &= p_m^{-2}\mathbbm{E}(\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'}\1_{p_m}\1_{p_m}'\1_{p_m}\1_{p_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}) \\
    &= p_m^{-1}\mathbbm{E}(\1_{p_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m})^2 = O(q_m),
\end{align*}
where the last equality follows by \eqref{eqn:sum_of_error_rate}. By triangle inequality, combining the two bounds, we obtain $\mathbbm{E}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2=O(p_mq_m^{-1})$. Therefore, by Markov's inequality, we have
\begin{align}
    p_m^{-1}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{a}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2 &= O_P(q_m^{-1}), \label{eqn:row_main_effect_rate} \\
    q_m^{-1}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{b}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{b}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2 &= O_P(p_m^{-1}), \label{eqn:column_main_effect_rate}
\end{align}
where column effect $\widehat{\mathbf{b}}_t^{(m)}$ follows similarly. This ends the proof. $\square$

For $m\ne n\in[M]$, define 
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}
    &:=T^{-1}q_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}
    \sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}\left(\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{Y}_t^{(n)'}\right)\mathbf{M}_{p_n}, \label{eqn:S_A1} \\
    \widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_1,mn}
    &:=T^{-1}q_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}
    \sum_{t=1}^T\left(\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{Y}_t^{(n)'}\right)\mathbf{M}_{p_n}. \label{eqn:S_A1_hat}
\end{align}
The corresponding population matrix and its estimator are
\begin{align*}
    \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_1,m}
    = (M-1)^{-1}\sum_{n=1,n\neq m}^{M}\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}^{\otimes 2}, \qquad 
    \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m}
    = (M-1)^{-1}\sum_{n=1,n\neq m}^{M}\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_1,mn}^{\otimes 2}.
\end{align*}
By \eqref{eqn: Omega_population}, we have $\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}=T^{-1}\mathbf{A}_{1m}\left(\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t')\right)\mathbf{A}_{1n}'$. Hence, 
\begin{align}
    \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_1,m}
    =T^{-2}(M-1)^{-1}\mathbf{A}_{1m}\left(\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t')\right)
    \left(\sum_{n\ne m}\mathbf{A}_{1n}'\mathbf{A}_{1n}\right)
    \left(\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t')\right)\mathbf{A}_{1m}'.
    \label{eqn:Omega_A1}
\end{align}

Define $\omega_{A_1,m}:=p_m^{\gamma_r}\sum_{n\ne m}p_n^{\gamma_r}$. Since \eqref{eqn:Omega_A1} implies that the column space of $\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_1,m}$ is contained in that of $\mathbf{A}_{1m}$, with $\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}:=\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'$, we have
\begin{align}
    (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_1,m}=\0.
    \label{eqn:projected_Omega_A1_zero}
\end{align}
Since $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}$ consists of the leading $r_1$ eigenvectors of $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m}$,
\begin{align}
    \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}
    =\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_1,m},\qquad
    \widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_1,m}:=\textnormal{diag}\big(\lambda_1(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m}),\ldots,
    \lambda_{r_1}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m})\big).
    \label{eqn:Omega_A1_A1_D1}
\end{align}
Define $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}:=\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_1,mn}-\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}$. Then,
\begin{align}
    \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_1,m}
    =(M-1)^{-1}\sum_{n\ne m}
    \left(\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}'+\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}'+\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}'\right).
    \label{eqn:Omega_A1_full_decomposition}
\end{align}
Left-multiplying \eqref{eqn:Omega_A1_full_decomposition} by $\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}$ and using $(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}=\0$ gives
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_1,m}) \\
    &=(M-1)^{-1}\sum_{n\ne m}\left\{(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}'+(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}'\right\}.
\end{align*} 
By \eqref{eqn:S_A1_bound} and \eqref{eqn:Delta_A1_oP} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_S_A1_Delta_A1}, $\big\| \mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2+\big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2=O_P(p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r})$. Hence, \eqref{eqn:Delta_A1_projected_bound} gives
\begin{align}
    &\big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_1,m}) \big\|_F^2 \nonumber\\
    &=O_P\Big(R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}+\gamma_r}
    \sum_{n\ne m}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}+\gamma_r}
    +T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_r+1}q_m^{-1}
    \sum_{n\ne m}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}+\gamma_r}\Big) \nonumber\\
    &=O_P\Bigg(\omega_{A_1,m}^2\Big(
    R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}-\gamma_r}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    +T^{-1}p_m^{1-\gamma_r}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}
    p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big)\Bigg).
    \label{eqn:Omega_A1_projected_bound}
\end{align}

Define $\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}:=\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}$.
Since $\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}$ both have orthonormal columns, Lemma~\ref{lemma:rotation_orthogonality} gives
\begin{align}
    \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}
    =(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}.
    \label{eqn:A1_projected_rotation}
\end{align}
Left-multiplying \eqref{eqn:Omega_A1_A1_D1} by $\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}$, and using \eqref{eqn:projected_Omega_A1_zero} and \eqref{eqn:A1_projected_rotation}, gives
\begin{align}
    \left(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}\right)\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_1,m}
    =(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_1,m})\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}.
    \label{eqn:A1_minus_A1H_times_D}
\end{align}
Combining \eqref{eqn:Omega_A1_projected_bound}, \eqref{eqn:A1_minus_A1H_times_D}, and Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_D_A1}, together with $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m} \big\|=1$, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2 
    \leq \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_1,m}) \big\|_F^2
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m} \big\|^2\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_1,m}^{-1} \big\|_F^2 \\
    &=O_P\Big(R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}-\gamma_r}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    +T^{-1}p_m^{1-\gamma_r}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big).
\end{align*}
Using $p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}=1\vee\nu_{r,n}$, this is exactly the row-side rate stated in Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m}. Both terms above are $o(1)$ under (R1), so that $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F=o_P(1)$. Hence, Lemma~\ref{lemma:rotation_orthogonality} gives $\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}'\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}=\mathbf{I}_{r_1}+o_P(1)$, $\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}'=\mathbf{I}_{r_1}+o_P(1)$, and $\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}$ is invertible with probability tending to one. Moreover, defining $\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}:=\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'$, we have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2
    =2\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2.
    \label{eqn:A1_projector_rate}
\end{align}
The proof for the global column-loading estimator $\widehat{\mathbf{B}}_{1m}$ follows similarly, with $\mathbf{H}_{B_1,m}:=\widecheck{\mathbf{B}}_{1m}'\widehat{\mathbf{B}}_{1m}$. This ends the proof. $\square$

Recall that
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}:=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp},\qquad
    \widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_2,m}:=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)'}\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp},
    \label{eqn:S_A2}
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}:=\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}:=\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}$. Since $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}^{\perp}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}^{\perp'}=\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}$, the sample matrix in the estimation procedure satisfies
\begin{align}
    \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}
    &=\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)'}\right)
    \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}
    \left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)'}\right)
    =\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_2,m}\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_2,m}'.
    \label{eqn:Omega_A2_sample}
\end{align}
Define its population counterpart as
\begin{align}
    \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m}:=\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)'}\right)
    \mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)'}\right)
    =\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}'.
    \label{eqn:Omega_A2_population}
\end{align}
By (IC2),
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}=\mathbf{A}_{2m}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)'}\right)\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}.
\end{align*}
Hence, with $\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m}:=\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'$, 
\begin{align}
    (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}=\0,\qquad
    (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m}=\0.
    \label{eqn:projected_Omega_A2_zero}
\end{align}
Since $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}$ consists of the leading $r_2^{(m)}$ eigenvectors of $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}$,
\begin{align}
    \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}=\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_2,m},\qquad
    \widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_2,m}:=\textnormal{diag}\big(\lambda_1(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}),\ldots,
    \lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m})\big).
    \label{eqn:Omega_A2_A2_D2}
\end{align}
Define $\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}:=\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}$. Since $\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}$ both have orthonormal columns, Lemma~\ref{lemma:rotation_orthogonality} gives
\begin{align}
    \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}
    =(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}.
    \label{eqn:A2_projected_rotation}
\end{align}
Left-multiplying \eqref{eqn:Omega_A2_A2_D2} by $\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m}$, and using \eqref{eqn:projected_Omega_A2_zero} and \eqref{eqn:A2_projected_rotation}, gives
\begin{align}
    \left(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}\right)\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_2,m}
    =(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m})\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}.
    \label{eqn:A2_minus_A2H_times_D}
\end{align}

Let $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}:=\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_2,m}-\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}$. Since
\begin{align*}
    \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m}
    =\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}'
    +\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}'
    +\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}',
\end{align*}
using $(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}=\0$ gives
\begin{align}
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})
    (\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m}) \big\|_F^2
    \lesssim
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
    \left(\big\| \mathbf{S}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2+\big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2\right).
    \label{eqn:bound_Omega_A2_hat_minus_Omega}
\end{align}
By \eqref{eqn:S_A2_bound} and \eqref{eqn:Delta_A2_oP} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_S_A2_Delta_A2}, $\big\| \mathbf{S}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2+\big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2=O_P(p_m^{2\gamma_r^{(m)}})$. Combining \eqref{eqn:bound_Omega_A2_hat_minus_Omega}, \eqref{eqn:Delta_A2_projected_bound}, \eqref{eqn:A2_minus_A2H_times_D}, and Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_D_A2}, together with $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m} \big\|=1$, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
    \leq\big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})
    (\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m}) \big\|_F^2
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m} \big\|^2\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_2,m}^{-1} \big\|_F^2 \\
    &=O_P\Big(R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r-\gamma_r^{(m)}}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    +T^{-1}p_m^{1+\gamma_r-2\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    +p_m^{1-2\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-2}\Big).
\end{align*}
Together with the definition of $\nu_{r,m}$, this proves the row-side loading rate in Theorem~\ref{thm:A2m_alphatm}. All three terms above are $o(1)$ under (R1), so that $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F=o_P(1)$. Hence, Lemma~\ref{lemma:rotation_orthogonality} gives $\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}=\mathbf{I}_{r_2^{(m)}}+o_P(1)$, $\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'=\mathbf{I}_{r_2^{(m)}}+o_P(1)$, and $\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}$ is invertible with probability tending to one. Moreover, defining $\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_2,m}:=\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'$, we have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_2,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
    =2\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2.
    \label{eqn:A2_projector_rate}
\end{align}
The proof for the local column-loading estimator $\widehat{\mathbf{B}}_{2m}$ follows similarly, with $\mathbf{H}_{B_2,m}:=\widecheck{\mathbf{B}}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{B}}_{2m}$.

We next prove the local main effect factor series. For $m\in[M]$, define
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m}
    :=\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1}
    (\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m})^{-1}
    \widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp},\qquad
    \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}
    :=(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m})^{-1}
    \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}.
\end{align*}
By (S1) and the asymptotic orthogonality of $\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}$, we have $\big\| \mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m} \big\|=O_P(1)$. Moreover, $\mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m}\mathbf{A}_{1m}=\0$, $\mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}=\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1}$, $\widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}=\mathbf{I}_{r_2^{(m)}}$, and $\widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}=\0$.

We first control $\widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}-\mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m}$. Note that
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}
    -\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}
    \widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \\
    &=(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m})'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}
    +\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'(\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp})\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m} \\
    &\quad+\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}
    (\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}).
\end{align*}
Since $\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}$ have orthonormal columns, $\big\| \mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|=O_P(1)$, and $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp} \big\|=\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|$, it follows that
\begin{align}
    &\quad\;\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}
    -\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}
    \widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\| \nonumber \\
    &=O_P\Big(\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F
    +\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|\Big).
    \label{eqn:local_gram_perturbation}
\end{align}
By (S1), the eigenvalues of $\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}$ are bounded away from zero. Together with the asymptotic orthogonality of $\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}$, this implies $\lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}\big(\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}\big)\gtrsim_P 1$.
Since the right-hand side of \eqref{eqn:local_gram_perturbation} is $o_P(1)$, Weyl's inequality further gives
\begin{align*}
    \big\| (\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m})^{-1} \big\|
    =O_P(1), \qquad
    \big\| (\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m})^{-1} \big\|
    =O_P(1).
\end{align*}
Therefore, using $\mathbf{A}^{-1}-\mathbf{B}^{-1}=\mathbf{A}^{-1}(\mathbf{B}-\mathbf{A})\mathbf{B}^{-1}$,
\begin{align}
    &\quad\;\big\| (\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m})^{-1}
    -(\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}
    \widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m})^{-1} \big\| \nonumber \\
    &=O_P\Big(\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F
    +\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|\Big).
    \label{eqn:local_gram_inverse_perturbation}
\end{align}
Moreover,
\begin{align*}
    \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}
    -\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}
    =(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m})'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}
    +\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'(\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}),
\end{align*}
and hence
\begin{align}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}
    -\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp} \big\|
    =O_P\Big(\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F
    +\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|\Big).
    \label{eqn:local_numerator_perturbation}
\end{align}
Since $\mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m}=(\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m})^{-1}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}$, combining \eqref{eqn:local_gram_inverse_perturbation} and \eqref{eqn:local_numerator_perturbation} yields
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}-\mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m} \big\|
    =O_P\Big(\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F
    +\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|\Big).
\end{align*}
Thus, by Lemma~\ref{lemma:rotation_orthogonality},
\begin{align}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}-\mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m} \big\|^2
    =O_P\Big(\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2
    +\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2\Big).
    \label{eqn:L_alpha_perturbation}
\end{align}
In particular, $\big\| \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m} \big\|=O_P(1)$.

By the definition of $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}=q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}$,
\begin{align}
    \widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}
    &=q_m^{-1}\mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}
    +q_m^{-1}(\widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}-\mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m})\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} \nonumber \\
    &\quad+\widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}(\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}-\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m})\mathbf{A}_{1m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t
    -\widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m})\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)} \nonumber \\
    &=:\mathcal{I}_{\alpha,1}^{(m)}+\mathcal{I}_{\alpha,2}^{(m)}+\mathcal{I}_{\alpha,3}^{(m)}+\mathcal{I}_{\alpha,4}^{(m)},
    \label{eqn:local_alpha_decomposition}
\end{align}
where the first equality uses $\mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m}\mathbf{A}_{1m}=\0$, $\widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}=\0$, $\mathcal{L}_{\alpha,m}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}=\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1}$ and $\widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{\alpha,m}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}=\mathbf{I}_{r_2^{(m)}}$.

For the first term in \eqref{eqn:local_alpha_decomposition}, note that the matrix $(\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m})^{-1}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}$ has bounded Frobenius norm by (S1). Since $\big\| \mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1} \big\|=O_P(1)$, Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm} gives $\big\| \mathcal{I}_{\alpha,1}^{(m)} \big\|^2=O_P(q_m^{-1})$. Moreover, Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm}, \eqref{eqn:L_alpha_perturbation}, (ME1) and (ME2) yield respectively
\begin{align*}
    &\big\| \mathcal{I}_{\alpha,2}^{(m)} \big\|^2
    =O_P\left(p_mq_m^{-1}\left(\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2
    +\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2\right)\right),\\
    &\big\| \mathcal{I}_{\alpha,3}^{(m)} \big\|^2
    =O_P\Big(p_m^{\gamma_r}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2\Big),\qquad
    \big\| \mathcal{I}_{\alpha,4}^{(m)} \big\|^2
    =O_P\Big(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2\Big).
\end{align*}
Consequently,
\begin{align*}
    p_m^{-\gamma_r^{(m)}}
    \big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2
    =O_P\Big(&p_m^{-\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    +(p_m^{1-\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    +p_m^{\gamma_r-\gamma_r^{(m)}})
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2 \\
    &+(p_m^{1-\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}+1)
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2\Big).
\end{align*}
Substituting the rates from Theorems~\ref{thm:initial_A1m} and \ref{thm:A2m_alphatm} obtained above, and using $1+p_m^{\gamma_r-\gamma_r^{(m)}}\lesssim p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_r^{(m)}}$, and noting that $p_m^{1-2\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-2}=o(p_m^{-\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1})$ under (R1), gives
\begin{align}
    p_m^{-\gamma_r^{(m)}}\big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2
    =O_P\Big(&p_m^{-\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_r^{(m)}}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r} \nonumber \\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{1+\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-2\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big).
    \label{eqn:local_alpha_explicit_rate}
\end{align}
Since $p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_r^{(m)}}=(1\wedge\nu_{r,m})^{-1}$ and
$p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}=1\vee\nu_{r,n}$, this is the row-side factor rate stated in Theorem~\ref{thm:A2m_alphatm}. The column-side rate follows by symmetry. This ends the proof. $\square$

We prove the row-side results; the column-side results follow by symmetry. Let $\mathbf{A}_\alpha:=(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_1,\ldots,\boldsymbol{\alpha}_T)$,
$\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\alpha,T}:=T^{-1}\mathbf{A}_\alpha\mathbf{A}_\alpha'$, 
$\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha:=\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\alpha,T}^{-1/2}\mathbf{A}_\alpha=(\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_1,\ldots,\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_T)$.
Also write $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha:=(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_1,\ldots,\widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_T)$ and define
$\mathbf{P}_\alpha:=T^{-1}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha$.
By Lemma~\ref{lemma:prop_piecewise}, (S1), and Weyl's inequality, $\lambda_{r_1}(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\alpha,T})\asymp_P \lambda_1(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\alpha,T})\asymp_P1$. Hence $\big\| \boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\alpha,T}^{-1/2} \big\|=O_P(1)$ and $\big\| \widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t \big\|=O_P(1)$ for each $t\in[T]$.

For each $m\in[M]$, define $\mathbf{Z}_{\alpha,m}:=\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'\mathbf{A}_{1m}\mathbf{A}_\alpha$ and $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\alpha,m}:=\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}-\mathbf{Z}_{\alpha,m}$, and let $\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\alpha,m,t}$ denote the $t$th column of $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\alpha,m}$. Since $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'\mathbf{A}_{1m}=\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}'(\mathbf{A}_{1m}'\mathbf{A}_{1m})^{1/2}$, Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m} and (ME1) imply that $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'\mathbf{A}_{1m}$ is invertible with probability tending to one and has squared singular values of order $p_m^{\gamma_r}$. Thus, 
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{P}_{\mathbf{Z}_{\alpha,m}}
    =\mathbf{A}_\alpha'(\mathbf{A}_\alpha\mathbf{A}_\alpha')^{-1}\mathbf{A}_\alpha
    =\mathbf{P}_\alpha,\qquad
    \sigma_{r_1}^2(\mathbf{Z}_{\alpha,m})\asymp_P Tp_m^{\gamma_r}.
    \label{eqn:Z_alpham}
\end{align}

Write 
\begin{align*}
    \widehat{\mathbf{y}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{A}_{1m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t&=q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}-(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{A}_{2m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}), \\
    \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\alpha,m,t}&=\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'\left(q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}-(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{A}_{2m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)})\right).
\end{align*}
Using $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{A}_{2m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}=\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\big(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}\big)+(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m})\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}$, the loading and series rates in Theorem~\ref{thm:A2m_alphatm}, the asymptotic orthogonality of $\mathbf{H}_{A_2,m}$, and $\big\| \widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2=O_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}})$ give
\begin{align}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{A}_{2m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2
    =O_P\Big(&q_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    \nonumber\\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{1+\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big).
    \label{eqn:global_alpha_local_reconstruction}
\end{align}
The same rate holds for $T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{A}_{2m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2$,
Indeed, the same argument for $\mathcal{I}_{\alpha,1}^{(m)}$ gives $T^{-1}\sum_t\big\| \mathcal{I}_{\alpha,1}^{(m)} \big\|^2=O_P(q_m^{-1})$. Applying the remaining bounds in \eqref{eqn:local_alpha_decomposition}, together with
\begin{align*}
    &T^{-1}\sum_t\big\| q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} \big\|^2=O_P(p_mq_m^{-1}), \\
    T^{-1}\sum_t\big\| &\mathbf{A}_{1m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t \big\|^2=O_P(p_m^{\gamma_r}), \qquad
    T^{-1}\sum_t\big\| \widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)} \big\|^2=O_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}),
\end{align*}
gives the stated time-average reconstruction rate.

Next, since 
\begin{align*}
    q_m^{-1}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}
    =q_m^{-1}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m}'\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}
    +q_m^{-1}(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m})'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m},
\end{align*}
Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm} gives $\big\| q_m^{-1}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} \big\|^2=O_P(q_m^{-1})$, with the same rate after averaging over $t$. Using Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m} and (R1), this gives
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\big\| q_m^{-1}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} \big\|^2,\quad
    T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\big\| q_m^{-1}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} \big\|^2 \\
    &=O_P\Big(q_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    +T^{-1}p_mq_m^{-1}\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big),
\end{align*}
which is bounded by the right-hand side of \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_local_reconstruction}. Since $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m} \big\|=1$, we therefore obtain
\begin{align}
    \big\| \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\alpha,m,t} \big\|^2,\quad
    T^{-1}\big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\alpha,m} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(&q_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    \nonumber\\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{1+\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big).
    \label{eqn:global_alpha_score_bound}
\end{align}

Define $\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{\alpha,m}:=\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}'(\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}')^{-1}\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}$. By \eqref{eqn:Z_alpham}, \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_score_bound}, and (R1), $\big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\alpha,m} \big\|_F^2=o_P(Tp_m^{\gamma_r})$.
Hence Weyl's inequality gives $\sigma_{r_1}^2(\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m})\asymp_P Tp_m^{\gamma_r}$, so that $\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}'$ is invertible with probability tending to one. Since $\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\mathbf{Z}_{\alpha,m}'=\0$,
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{\alpha,m}
    =\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\alpha,m}'
    (\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}')^{-1}\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}.
    \label{eqn:projected_group_temporal}
\end{align}
Using
$\big\| (\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m}')^{-1}\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m} \big\| =\sigma_{r_1}^{-1}(\widehat{\mathbf{Z}}_{\alpha,m})$ and $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{\alpha,m}-\mathbf{P}_\alpha \big\|_F^2=2\big\| \mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{\alpha,m} \big\|_F^2$, we obtain
\begin{align}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{\alpha,m}-\mathbf{P}_\alpha \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(&p_m^{-\gamma_r}q_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})
    p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_r}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    \nonumber\\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{1-\gamma_{r,\min}^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big).
    \label{eqn:global_alpha_group_projector}
\end{align}
All terms on the right-hand side are $o(1)$ under (R1).

For the rowwise bound, let $\mathbf{U}_\alpha:=T^{-1/2}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'$, so that $\mathbf{P}_\alpha=\mathbf{U}_\alpha\mathbf{U}_\alpha'$ and $\big\| \mathbf{e}_t'\mathbf{U}_\alpha \big\|^2=T^{-1}\big\| \widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t \big\|^2=O_P(T^{-1})$. Hence
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \mathbf{e}_t'\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\alpha,m}' \big\|^2
    \lesssim
    \big\| \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\alpha,m,t} \big\|^2
    +T^{-1}\big\| \widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t \big\|^2\big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\alpha,m} \big\|_F^2.
\end{align*}
Combining this with \eqref{eqn:projected_group_temporal} and \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_score_bound} yields
\begin{align}
    T\big\| \mathbf{e}_t'\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp
    (\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{\alpha,m}-\mathbf{P}_\alpha) \big\|^2
    =O_P\Big(&p_m^{-\gamma_r}q_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})
    p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_r}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    \nonumber\\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{1-\gamma_{r,\min}^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big).
    \label{eqn:global_alpha_group_projector_row}
\end{align}

Now let $\widehat{\mathbf{K}}_\alpha:=M^{-1}\sum_{m=1}^M\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{\alpha,m}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha:=T^{-1/2}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'$, and define $\mathbf{H}_\alpha:=\mathbf{U}_\alpha'\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha=T^{-1}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'$. By construction,
$\widehat{\mathbf{K}}_\alpha\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha=\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_\alpha$, where $\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_\alpha$ contains the leading $r_1$ eigenvalues of
$\widehat{\mathbf{K}}_\alpha$. Since $M$ is fixed, \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_group_projector} gives $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{K}}_\alpha-\mathbf{P}_\alpha \big\|_F=o_P(1)$. The nonzero eigenvalues of $\mathbf{P}_\alpha$ are all equal to one, so $\lambda_{r_1}(\widehat{\mathbf{K}}_\alpha)\asymp_P1$ and $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{D}}_\alpha^{-1} \big\|=O_P(1)$.

By Lemma~\ref{lemma:rotation_orthogonality}, $\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha-\mathbf{U}_\alpha\mathbf{H}_\alpha
=\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha$. Left-multiplying the preceding eigenequation by $\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp$ therefore gives
\begin{align}
    (\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha-\mathbf{U}_\alpha\mathbf{H}_\alpha)\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_\alpha
    =\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp(\widehat{\mathbf{K}}_\alpha-\mathbf{P}_\alpha)\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha.
    \label{eqn:global_alpha_projected_eigen}
\end{align}
Equations \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_group_projector} and \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_projected_eigen} imply $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha-\mathbf{U}_\alpha\mathbf{H}_\alpha \big\|_F=o_P(1)$. Hence Lemma~\ref{lemma:rotation_orthogonality} shows that $\mathbf{H}_\alpha$ is asymptotically orthogonal.

For each $t\in[T]$, left-multiplying \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_projected_eigen} by $\mathbf{e}_t'$ and using \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_group_projector_row} gives
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t-\mathbf{H}_\alpha'\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t \big\|^2
    =O_P\Bigg(\max_{m\in[M]}\Big\{
    &p_m^{-\gamma_r}q_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})
    p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_r}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{1-\gamma_{r,\min}^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    \Big\}\Bigg).
\end{align*}
Moreover, $\mathbf{H}_\alpha'\mathbf{H}_\alpha=\mathbf{I}-(\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha-\mathbf{U}_\alpha\mathbf{H}_\alpha)'(\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha-\mathbf{U}_\alpha\mathbf{H}_\alpha)$. Since $\mathbf{H}_\alpha^{-1}=(\mathbf{H}_\alpha'\mathbf{H}_\alpha)^{-1}\mathbf{H}_\alpha'$ and $\mathbf{H}_\alpha$ is asymptotically orthogonal, $\big\| \mathbf{H}_\alpha^{-1}-\mathbf{H}_\alpha' \big\|=O_P(\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha-\mathbf{U}_\alpha\mathbf{H}_\alpha \big\|_F^2)$. Together with $\big\| \widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t \big\|=O_P(1)$, replacing $\mathbf{H}_\alpha'$ by $\mathbf{H}_\alpha^{-1}$ contributes only the square of the preceding bound and is therefore negligible under (R1). Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t-\mathbf{H}_\alpha^{-1}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t \big\|^2
    =O_P\Bigg(\max_{m\in[M]}\Big\{
    &p_m^{-\gamma_r}q_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})
    p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_r}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{1-\gamma_{r,\min}^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    \Big\}\Bigg),
\end{align*}
which proves the stated rate for the global row main effect series.

We next consider the refitted global loading $\widetilde{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}$. Let $\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_m:=(\widehat{\mathbf{y}}_1^{(m)},\ldots,\widehat{\mathbf{y}}_T^{(m)})$ and $\mathbf{Y}_m:=\mathbf{A}_{1m}\mathbf{A}_\alpha$. By \eqref{eqn:A1mtilde} and the definitions of $\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}$ and $\mathbf{H}_\alpha$,
\begin{align}
    \widetilde{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_\alpha
    =T^{-1}(\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_m-\mathbf{Y}_m)\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'.
    \label{eqn:final_A1_exact}
\end{align}

Define $\boldsymbol{\Pi}_{2\mid1,m}:=\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}(\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^\perp\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m})^{-1}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^\perp$, and define $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Pi}}_{2\mid1,m}$ analogously using $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^\perp$. By (S1) and Theorem~\ref{thm:A2m_alphatm}, $\big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Pi}}_{2\mid1,m} \big\|=O_P(1)$. Since $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\widehat{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t^{(m)}=\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Pi}}_{2\mid1,m}\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}$ and $\mathbf{A}_{2m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}=\boldsymbol{\Pi}_{2\mid1,m}\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \widehat{\mathbf{y}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{A}_{1m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t
    =q_m^{-1}(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Pi}}_{2\mid1,m})\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}
    -(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Pi}}_{2\mid1,m}-\boldsymbol{\Pi}_{2\mid1,m})\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}.
\end{align*}
By (IC2), $T^{-1}\sum_t\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t'=\mathbf{A}_{1m}\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\alpha,T}^{1/2}=\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}$ and $\boldsymbol{\Pi}_{2\mid1,m}\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}=\0$.
Consequently,
\begin{align*}
    T^{-1}(\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_m-\mathbf{Y}_m)\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'
    =(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Pi}}_{2\mid1,m})
    \Big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^Tq_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t'\Big)
    -\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Pi}}_{2\mid1,m}\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}.
\end{align*}
Since $T^{-1}\sum_tq_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\widecheck{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}_t'=q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}(T^{-1}\sum_t\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t')\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\alpha,T}^{-1/2}$, \eqref{eqn:E1u} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_Delta} gives its squared Frobenius norm as $O_P(T^{-1}p_mq_m^{-1})$. Also $\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^\perp\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}=\0$, and hence
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Pi}}_{2\mid1,m}\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m} \big\|_F^2
    \lesssim_P\big\| (\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(p_m^{\gamma_r}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2\Big).
\end{align*}
Substituting Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m} gives
\begin{align}
    \big\| T^{-1}(\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_m-\mathbf{Y}_m)\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha' \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(&R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r} \nonumber \\
    &+T^{-1}p_mq_m^{-1}\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big).
    \label{eqn:final_A1_cross_moment}
\end{align}

Furthermore, \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_local_reconstruction} and $T^{-1}\sum_t
\big\| q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m} \big\|^2=O_P(p_mq_m^{-1})$ give
\begin{align}
    T^{-1}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_m-\mathbf{Y}_m \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(p_mq_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big),
    \label{eqn:final_A1_residual_energy}
\end{align}
where the remaining term in \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_local_reconstruction} is absorbed into $p_mq_m^{-1}$ under (R1).

Finally, since $\mathbf{P}_\alpha\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'=\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'\mathbf{H}_\alpha$, \eqref{eqn:final_A1_exact} gives
\begin{align*}
    \widetilde{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_\alpha
    =\big(T^{-1}(\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_m-\mathbf{Y}_m)\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'\big)\mathbf{H}_\alpha
    +T^{-1}(\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_m-\mathbf{Y}_m)\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'.
\end{align*}
The first term is controlled by \eqref{eqn:final_A1_cross_moment}. For the second term, since $\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha'=\sqrt{T}\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha$,
\begin{align*}
    \big\| T^{-1}(\widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_m-\mathbf{Y}_m)\mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_\alpha' \big\|_F^2
    \le\big\{T^{-1}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{Y}}_m-\mathbf{Y}_m \big\|_F^2\big\}
    \big\| \mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha \big\|_F^2.
\end{align*}
By \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_group_projector} and \eqref{eqn:global_alpha_projected_eigen}, $\big\| \mathbf{P}_\alpha^\perp\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_\alpha \big\|_F^2$ satisfies the same bound obtained above for the global row main effect series. Combining this with \eqref{eqn:final_A1_cross_moment} and \eqref{eqn:final_A1_residual_energy}, and dividing the resulting bound by $p_m^{\gamma_r}$, gives 
\begin{align*}
    p_m^{-\gamma_r}
    \big\| \widetilde{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}-\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_\alpha \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(\eta_{A_1,m}
    +\Big\{\frac{p_m^{1-\gamma_r}}{q_m}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})(1\vee\nu_{r,m})
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{r,n}\Big\}\eta_\alpha\Big),
\end{align*}
which proves Theorem~\ref{thm:A1m_alphat}. The column-side result follows by symmetry.

Expanding $\eta_{A_1,m}$ and $\eta_\alpha$ gives the equivalent explicit rate:
\begin{align*}
    p_m^{-\gamma_r}\big\| \widetilde{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}&-\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_\alpha \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}-\gamma_r}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r} \\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{1-\gamma_r}q_m^{-1}\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r} 
    +\Big\{p_m^{1-\gamma_r}q_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_r}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big\} \\
    &\cdot\max_{n\in[M]}\Big\{
    p_n^{-\gamma_r}q_n^{-1} 
    +R(T,d_{r,\star})p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    \max_{s\in[M]\setminus\{n\}}p_s^{\gamma_r^{(s)}-\gamma_r}
    +T^{-1}p_n^{1-\gamma_{r,\min}^{(n)}}q_n^{-1}
    \max_{s\in[M]}p_s^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(s)}-\gamma_r}
    \Big\}\Big), \\
    q_m^{-\gamma_c}\big\| \widetilde{\mathbf{B}}_{1m}&-\widecheck{\widecheck{\mathbf{B}}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_\beta \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(R(T,d_{c,\star})q_m^{\gamma_c^{(m)}-\gamma_c}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}q_n^{\gamma_c^{(n)}-\gamma_c} \\ 
    &+T^{-1}q_m^{1-\gamma_c}p_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}q_n^{\gamma_{c,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_c}
    +\Big\{q_m^{1-\gamma_c}p_m^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{c,\star})q_m^{\gamma_{c,\max}^{(m)}-\gamma_c}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}q_n^{\gamma_c^{(n)}-\gamma_c}\Big\} \\
    &\cdot\max_{n\in[M]}\Big\{
    q_n^{-\gamma_c}p_n^{-1}
    +R(T,d_{c,\star})q_n^{\gamma_{c,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_c}
    \max_{s\in[M]\setminus\{n\}}q_s^{\gamma_c^{(s)}-\gamma_c}
    +T^{-1}q_n^{1-\gamma_{c,\min}^{(n)}}p_n^{-1}
    \max_{s\in[M]}q_s^{\gamma_{c,\max}^{(s)}-\gamma_c}
    \Big\}\Big),
\end{align*}
where the column-side results follow by symmetry. This completes the proof. $\square$

For the local factor loading estimators, it suffices to show the results for $\widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m$, and analogous arguments follow for $\widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m$.
To this end, we adapt the decomposition and arguments used in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma: common_comp_global_local_init}, with the roles of rows and columns interchanged appropriately. 
Recall from \eqref{eqn: check_Yt_m_simplify} that $\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)} = 
\mathbf{Q}_m\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{J}_m' + \mathbf{R}_m\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{C}_m' + \mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}$.
Hence
\begin{align}
    \widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)\prime} \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp}
    &=
    \mathbf{J}_m\mathbf{G}_t'\mathbf{Q}_m'\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp}
    + \mathbf{C}_m\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)\prime}\mathbf{R}_m'\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp}
    + \mathbf{M}_{q_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)\prime} \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp}
    \notag \\
    &=:
    \mathcal{A}_t + \mathcal{B}_t + \mathcal{C}_t .
    \label{eqn: local_C_decomp_components}
\end{align}
With \eqref{eqn: local_C_decomp_components}, we may expand $\mathbf{X}_{C,m}$ as
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{X}_{C,m} &= \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \big( \widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)\prime} \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp} \big) \big( \widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)\prime} \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp} \big)'  \notag\\
    &=
    \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{B}_t \mathcal{B}_t'
    + \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{B}_t \mathcal{C}_t' 
    + \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{B}_t \mathcal{A}_t' 
    + \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{C}_t \mathcal{B}_t' 
    + \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{C}_t \mathcal{C}_t' \notag\\
    &\;\quad
    + \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{C}_t \mathcal{A}_t' 
    + \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{A}_t \mathcal{B}_t' 
    + \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{A}_t \mathcal{C}_t' 
    + \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{A}_t \mathcal{A}_t'  
    = :
    \sum_{i=1}^9 \mathcal{J}_i . \label{eqn:XCm-decomposition}
\end{align}



For convenience, define the matrices $\mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp} := \mathbf{Q}_m^{\perp} \mathbf{Q}_m^{\perp \prime}$ and 
$\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} := \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp} \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp \prime}$.
Then we can write $\mathcal{B}_t\mathcal{B}_t' = \mathbf{C}_m\mathbf{F}_t^{(m) \prime}\mathbf{R}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \mathbf{R}_m\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{C}_m'$.
Further define
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{B}_t^0 := \mathbf{C}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m) \prime} \mathbf{R}_m' \mathbf{Q}_m^{\perp},
    \quad
    \mathcal{J}_1^0 := \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{B}_t^0 \mathcal{B}_t^{0 \prime}.
    \label{eqn: local_C_J1_pop}
\end{align}
Then by Lemmas~\ref{lemma:rotation_orthogonality},
\ref{lemma: common_comp_global_local_init}, 
and following Lemma~S5 in \cite{Cen2026_multiorder},
we have
\begin{align}
    &\;\quad
    \Big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp}  - \mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \Big\|_F^2
    = 
    \Big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m} - \mathbf{P}_{Q,m} \Big\|_F^2 \notag \\
    &=
    O_P\left\{
    R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}) \;
    p_m^{2 (\delta_{q,1}^{(m)} -\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)})}
    \left( \frac{  \max_{n\in[M] \setminus \{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}}{\max_{n\in[M] \setminus\{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}} \right)^2
    \right\} .
    \label{eqn: local_C_projection_perturbation}
\end{align}

Consider $\mathcal{J}_1$.
Note that $\mathcal{J}_1 - \mathcal{J}_1^0 = T^{-1} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathbf{C}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)\prime} \mathbf{R}_m' \big( \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} - \mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \big) \mathbf{R}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m'$,
we have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{J}_1 - \mathcal{J}_1^0 \big\|_F^2
    &\leq
    \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \Big\| \mathbf{C}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)\prime} \mathbf{R}_m' \big( \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp}  - \mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \big)  \mathbf{R}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m' \Big\|_F^2 \notag \\
    &\lesssim
    \big\| \mathbf{C}_m \big\|^4 
    \big\| \mathbf{R}_m \big\|^4
    \Big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} - \mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \Big\|_F^2
    \cdot \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \big\| \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \big\|_F^4 ,
    \label{eqn: local_C_J1_perturbation}
\end{align}
where the last inequality used the sub-multiplicativity and the fact that the Frobenius norm of a product is bounded by products of spectral and Frobenius norms. 
By Assumption~(F1), $\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)}$ has uniformly bounded fourth moments, so $T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\|\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)}\|_F^4=O_P(1)$. 
Combining \eqref{eqn: local_C_J1_perturbation},
\eqref{eqn: local_C_projection_perturbation},
and Assumption~(L1), we thus have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{J}_1 - \mathcal{J}_1^0 \big\|_F^2
    \lesssim_P
    q_m^{2 \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    p_m^{2 \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    \left\{
    R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}) \;
    p_m^{2 (\delta_{q,1}^{(m)} -\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)})}
    \left( \frac{  \max_{n\in[M] \setminus \{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}}{\max_{n\in[M] \setminus\{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}} \right)^2
    \right\} .
    \label{eqn: local_C_J1_perturbation_rate}
\end{align}


Next, by Assumptions~(L1) and (S1), the nonzero eigenvalues of $\mathcal{J}_1^0$ satisfy that, for any $i\in [k_c^{(m)}]$,
\begin{align}
    \lambda_i\big( \mathcal{J}_1^0 \big)
    \asymp
    q_m^{\delta_{c,i}^{(m)}}
    p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} ,
    \label{eqn: local_C_J1_eigenvalues}
\end{align}
which follows analogous arguments as for \eqref{eqn: Omega_Q_m_krth_eigenvalue_lower} in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma: common_comp_global_local_init}.

It remains to bound $\mathcal{J}_i$, $i=2,\dots, 9$.
Note that these terms all involve at least one of $\mathcal{A}_t$, $\mathcal{C}_t$, or the projection perturbation (cf.\ \eqref{eqn: local_C_projection_perturbation}). 
We bound their squared Frobenius norms separately.
To this end, for $\mathcal{J}_2$ and $\mathcal{J}_4$, 
by Assumptions~(E2), (F1), and the same calculation as in \eqref{eqn: cIII_Q_mn}, we have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{J}_2 \big\|_F^2, \;
    \big\| \mathcal{J}_4 \big\|_F^2
    &=
    \Bigg\| \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{B}_t \mathcal{C}_t' \Bigg\|_F^2
    \lesssim_P
    \frac{1}{T} q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} q_m p_m
    =
    \frac{1}{T}  q_m^{1 + \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} p_m^{1 + \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} .
    \label{eqn: local_C_J2}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{J}_5$, using Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_Delta} and the proof of \eqref{eqn:Em11Em}, it holds that
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{J}_5 \big\|_F^2
    \lesssim_P
    q_m^{2 - 2 \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    + \frac{1}{T} q_m^2 p_m .
    \label{eqn: local_C_J5}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{J}_6$ and $\mathcal{J}_8$, it suffices to consider the former and the latter follows in similar arguments.
Recall that $\mathcal{A}_t = \mathbf{J}_m\mathbf{G}_t'\mathbf{Q}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp}$ from \eqref{eqn: local_C_decomp_components}.
Note that
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathbf{Q}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp} \big\|_F
    &=
    \big\| \mathbf{Q}_m'
    (\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp} \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp \prime} - \mathbf{Q}_m^{\perp} \mathbf{Q}_m^{\perp \prime})
    \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp}
    \big\| \notag \\
    &\leq
    \big\| \mathbf{Q}_m \big\| \cdot
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} - \mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \big\|_F
    \cdot \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m^{\perp} \big\| 
    =
    \big\| \mathbf{Q}_m \big\| 
    \cdot \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} - \mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \big\|_F ,
    \label{eqn: local_C_Q_perp_bound}
\end{align}
Also, with $f(\mathbf{X}) = \|\mathbf{X}\|_F^2$ being convex, we have $\big\| T^{-1} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathbf{X}_t \big\|_F^2 = f\big( T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathbf{X}_t \big) \leq T^{-1} \sum_{t=1}^{T} f(\mathbf{X}_t) = T^{-1} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \|\mathbf{X}_t\|_F^2$.
Altogether, by Assumption~(F1), and the sub-multiplicativity of the Frobenius norm, we arrive at
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{J}_6 \big\|_F^2 &=
    \Bigg\| \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathcal{C}_t \mathcal{A}_t' \Bigg\|_F^2
    \leq
    \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T}
    \big\| \mathcal{C}_t \big\|_F^2
    \big\| \mathcal{A}_t \big\|_F^2 \notag \\
    &\leq
    \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T}
    \big\| \mathcal{C}_t \big\|_F^2 \cdot
    \big\| \mathbf{J}_m \big\|^2 \cdot
    \big\| \mathbf{G}_t \big\|_F^2 \cdot
    \big\| \mathbf{Q}_m \big\|_F^2 \cdot
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} - \mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \big\|_F^2  \notag \\
    &\lesssim_P
    q_m^{1 + \delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}
    p_m^{1 + \delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}
    \left\{
    R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}) \;
    p_m^{2 (\delta_{q,1}^{(m)} -\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)})}
    \left( \frac{  \max_{n\in[M] \setminus \{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}}{\max_{n\in[M] \setminus\{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}} \right)^2
    \right\} ,
    \label{eqn: local_C_J6}
\end{align}
where the second inequality used
\eqref{eqn: local_C_projection_perturbation},
\eqref{eqn: local_C_Q_perp_bound},
and $\| \mathcal{C}_t \|_F^2 = O_P(p_m q_m)$ which follows from similar arguments for \eqref{eqn: unit_vector_error_bound}.
Next, $\mathcal{J}_3$ and $\mathcal{J}_7$ can be bounded analogously by the above arguments, with $\mathcal{C}_t$ replaced by $\mathcal{B}_t$ where $\| \mathcal{B}_t \|_F^2 = O_P(q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}})$, thus giving
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{J}_3 \big\|_F^2, \;
    \big\| \mathcal{J}_7 \big\|_F^2
    &\lesssim_P
    q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)} + \delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}
    p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)} + \delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}
    \left\{
    R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}) \;
    p_m^{2 (\delta_{q,1}^{(m)} -\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)})}
    \left( \frac{  \max_{n\in[M] \setminus \{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}}{\max_{n\in[M] \setminus\{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}} \right)^2
    \right\} .
    \label{eqn: local_C_J3}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{J}_9$, using Assumption~(F1),
the bound of $\| \mathcal{A}_t \|_F^2$ as in \eqref{eqn: local_C_Q_perp_bound} and \eqref{eqn: local_C_J6}, we have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{J}_9 \big\|_F^2
    &\leq
    \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^T \big\| \mathcal{A}_t \big\|_F^4
    &\lesssim_P
    q_m^{2 \delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}
    p_m^{2 \delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}
    \left\{
    R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}) \;
    p_m^{2 (\delta_{q,1}^{(m)} -\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)})}
    \left( \frac{  \max_{n\in[M] \setminus \{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}}{\max_{n\in[M] \setminus\{m\}} s_{J,n}^2 p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}} \right)^2
    \right\}^2 .
    \label{eqn: local_C_J9}
\end{align}


Finally, recall \eqref{eqn: local_C_J1_pop}.
Then combining \eqref{eqn: local_C_J1_perturbation_rate},
\eqref{eqn: local_C_J2},
\eqref{eqn: local_C_J5},
\eqref{eqn: local_C_J6},
\eqref{eqn: local_C_J3},
and \eqref{eqn: local_C_J9}
give the rate on $\big\| \mathbf{X}_{C,m} - \mathcal{J}_1^0 \big\|_F^2$,
which, together with \eqref{eqn: local_C_J1_eigenvalues} and applying the Davis--Kahan theorem \citep[Theorem~2]{yu2015useful}, complete the proof for $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m - \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big\|_F^2$.

It remains to show the results for the local factor estimators.
To this end, define
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{L}_{R,m}
    &:=
    \big( \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m' \mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m \big)^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m' \mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp}
    ,  \quad
    \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m}
    := \big( \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big)^{-1} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} .
\end{align*}
By Assumptions~(L1) and (S1), the matrix $\widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m' \mathbf{P}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m$ is positive definite with eigenvalues bounded away from zero, and it holds that
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{L}_{R,m} \big\|, \;
    \big\| \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \big\| =
    O_P\Big(
    p_m^{-\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}/2} \Big),
    \quad
    \mathcal{L}_{R,m} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m = \mathbf{I},
    \quad
    \mathcal{L}_{R,m} \mathbf{Q}_m = \0 .
    \label{eqn: local_F_L_properties}
\end{align}
Then by the definition of $\widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)}$ in Step~3 of Section~\ref{subsec:estimation} and \eqref{eqn: check_Yt_m_simplify}, we may decompose
\begin{align}
    \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} &= \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)} \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m \notag \\
    &= 
    \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \mathbf{Q}_m \mathbf{G}_t \mathbf{J}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m
    + \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m
    + \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{M}_{q_m} \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m 
    =:
    \mathcal{K}_1 + \mathcal{K}_2 + \mathcal{K}_3 .
    \label{eqn: local_F_decomp}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{K}_1$, by \eqref{eqn: local_F_L_properties}, \eqref{eqn: local_C_Q_perp_bound}, and Assumption~(F1), we have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{K}_1 \big\|_F^2
    &\leq
    \big\| \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \big\|^2 \big\| \mathbf{Q}_m'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \big\|_F^2 \big\| \mathbf{G}_t \big\|_F^2 \big\| \mathbf{J}_m \big\|^2 \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m \big\|^2 \notag \\
    &\lesssim_P
    p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)} -\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}
    \eta_{Q,m}
    \big\| \mathbf{G}_t \big\|_F^2 
    \lesssim_P
    p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)} -\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}
    \eta_{Q,m} ,
    \label{eqn: local_F_K1_rate}
\end{align}
with $\eta_{Q,m}$ defined in the statement of Theorem~\ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init}.


Consider $\mathcal{K}_2$ next.
Note that we can write
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{K}_2 &=
    \big( \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} + \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \big)
    \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)}
    \big( \mathbf{H}_{C,m} + \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m - \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big) \notag \\
    &=
    \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{H}_{C,m}
    + (\widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1}) \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \notag \\
    &\;\quad
    + \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} (\widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m - \mathbf{H}_{C,m})
    + (\widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1}) \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} (\widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m - \mathbf{H}_{C,m}) .
    \label{eqn: local_F_K2_expansion}
\end{align}
First, by the rates of convergence of the local loading matrices, we have
\begin{align}
    &\;\quad
    \big\| \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \big\|_F^2 
    =
    \big\| \big( \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big)^{-1} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m
    - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \big\|_F^2 \notag \\
    &=
    \big\| \big( \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big)^{-1} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m \mathbf{H}_{R,m}
    - \mathbf{I} \big\|_F^2 \notag \\
    &=
    \big\| \big( \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big)^{-1} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} (\widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m \mathbf{H}_{R,m} -\widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m)
    + \big( \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big)^{-1} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m
    - \mathbf{I} \big\|_F^2 \notag \\
    &\leq
    \big\| \big( \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big)^{-1} \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{Q,m}^{\perp} \big\|^2
    \cdot \big\| \widecheck{\mathbf{R}}_m \mathbf{H}_{R,m} -\widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big\|_F^2 
    \lesssim_P
    \Delta_{R,m} ,
    \label{eqn: local_F_E_rate_row}
\end{align}
where $\Delta_{R,m}$ is defined in the statement of Theorem~\ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init}.
Similarly, we have
\begin{align}
    &\;\quad
    \big\| \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m' \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m - \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big\|_F^2 
    = 
    \big\| \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m' (\widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m - \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m \mathbf{H}_{C,m}) + \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m'\widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m \mathbf{H}_{C,m} - \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big\|_F^2 \notag \\
    &=
    \big\| \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m' (\widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m - \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m \mathbf{H}_{C,m}) + (\mathbf{C}_m'\mathbf{C}_m)^{-1/2} \mathbf{C}_m'\mathbf{C}_m (\mathbf{C}_m'\mathbf{C}_m)^{-1/2} \mathbf{H}_{C,m} - \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big\|_F^2 
    \lesssim_P
    \Delta_{C,m} ,
    \label{eqn: local_F_E_rate_col} .
\end{align}
Also, recall the definition $\widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} = \big( \mathbf{R}_m'\mathbf{R}_m \big)^{1/2} \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \big( \mathbf{C}_m'\mathbf{C}_m \big)^{1/2}$, and it is straightforward from Assumptions~(F1) and (L1) that $\big\| \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \big\|_F^2 = O_P\big( p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} \big)$,
which, combined with 
\eqref{eqn: local_F_K2_expansion},
\eqref{eqn: local_F_E_rate_row},
\eqref{eqn: local_F_E_rate_col},
give
\begin{align}
    \Big\| \mathcal{K}_2 - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \Big\|_F^2
    &\lesssim_P
    p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \big( \Delta_{R,m} +
    \Delta_{C,m} \big) .
    \label{eqn: local_F_K2_rate}
\end{align}


Finally, for $\mathcal{K}_3$, by \eqref{eqn: local_F_L_properties} and the same argument as for \eqref{eqn: unit_vector_error_bound},
\begin{align}
    &\;\quad
    \big\| \mathcal{K}_3 \big\|_F^2 =
    \Big\| \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m}\mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{M}_{q_m} \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m \Big\|_F^2  \notag \\
    &\leq
    \big\| \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \big\|^2
    \cdot \Big\| \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{M}_{q_m} 
    \big\{ \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m \mathbf{H}_{C,m} +
    (\widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m - \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m \mathbf{H}_{C,m})
    \big\} 
    \Big\|_F^2 \notag \\
    &\lesssim
    \big\| \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \big\|^2
    \cdot \Big\| \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{M}_{q_m} \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \Big\|_F^2 
    + \big\| \widehat{\mathcal{L}}_{R,m} \big\|^2
    \cdot \Big\| \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}
    \mathbf{M}_{q_m} (\widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m - \widecheck{\mathbf{C}}_m \mathbf{H}_{C,m}) \Big\|_F^2 \notag \\
    &\lesssim_P
    p_m^{1 -\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} + p_m^{1 -\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} q_m \Delta_{C,m} .
    \label{eqn: local_F_K3}
\end{align}

By combining 
\eqref{eqn: local_F_decomp},
\eqref{eqn: local_F_K1_rate},
\eqref{eqn: local_F_K2_rate}, 
and \eqref{eqn: local_F_K3},
we conclude that
\begin{align*}
    \frac{1}{
    p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} 
    q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    }
    \Big\| \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \Big\|_F^2 
    &\lesssim_P
    \Delta_{R,m}
    +
    \Delta_{C,m}
    +
    p_m^{1 - 2\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{1 - \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \Delta_{C,m}
    +
    p_m^{1 - 2\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{ - \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} ,
\end{align*}
where the rate in \eqref{eqn: local_F_K1_rate}, after scaled by $p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}$, is omitted as it is dominated by $\Delta_{C,m}$, particularly:
\[
q_m^{-2 \delta_{c,k_c^{(m)}}^{(m)}}
    p_m^{-2 \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} 
    \big(q_m^{2 \delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} p_m^{2 \delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} 
    + q_m^{1 + \delta_{j,1}^{(m)}} p_m^{1 + \delta_{q,1}^{(m)}} \big) \eta_{Q,m} .
\]
Noting that all these arguments hold if we alternatively work on $\widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m) \prime}$, with the resulting rates of convergence symmetric (i.e., with row and column dimensions swapped),
and taking the minimum of these two completes the proof of this theorem.
$\square$

For each $m\in[M]$ and any $c_m\in[k_r]$ and $d_m\in[k_c]$, we have 
\begin{align}
    \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\widecheck\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m} &= (\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\mathbf{J}_m \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{Q}_m)\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t) + (\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\mathbf{C}_m \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{R}_m)\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)})\notag\\ 
    &\;\;\;\;+ \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}. \label{eqn:Thm9-1}
\end{align}
From the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init}, for some invertible constant order matrices $\mathbf{H}_q$ and $\mathbf{H}_j$, we have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m - \mathbf{Q}_m(\mathbf{Q}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{q,m} \big\| = O_P(\eta_{Q,m}) = o_P(1), \;\;\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{J}}_m - \mathbf{J}_m(\mathbf{J}_m'\mathbf{J}_m)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{j,m} \big\| = O_P(\eta_{J,m}) = o_P(1). \label{eqn:Thm9-eta_QJ}
\end{align}
Hence, defining $\mathbf{e}_{p,k}$ as the real unit vector of length $p$ with 1 at its $k$th position, we have
\begin{align}
    \max_{c_m \in[k_r]}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{Q}_m \big\| &= \max_{c_m\in[k_r]} \big\| \mathbf{e}_{k_r, c_m}'\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m \big\| \asymp \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m \big\|\notag\\ 
    &\geq \big\| (\mathbf{Q}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m)^{1/2}\mathbf{H}_{q,m} \big\| - \big\| \mathbf{Q}_m'(\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m - \mathbf{Q}_m(\mathbf{Q}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{q,m}) \big\| \asymp_P p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}/2}. \label{eqn:Thm9-2}
\end{align}
Similarly,
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:Thm9-3}
    \max_{d_m \in[k_c]}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\mathbf{J}_m \big\| \asymp_P q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}/2}. 
\end{equation}
Consider $m\in\mathcal{A}_G$. Then (\ref{eqn:Thm9-1}) implies that
\begin{align*}
    &T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\widecheck\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\widecheck\mathbf{Y}_t^{(n)'}\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_n} = \sum_{j=0}^{9}I_j, \text{ where}\\
    I_0 &:=  (\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\mathbf{J}_m \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{Q}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}(\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t))^{\otimes 2}(\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\mathbf{J}_n \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}'\mathbf{Q}_n)',\\
    I_1 &:=  (\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\mathbf{J}_m \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{Q}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T(\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)^{\otimes 2} - \mathbbm{E}(\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t))^{\otimes 2})(\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\mathbf{J}_n \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}'\mathbf{Q}_n)',\\
    I_2 &:= (\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\mathbf{J}_m \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{Q}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(n)})'(\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\mathbf{C}_n \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}'\mathbf{R}_n)',\\
    I_3 &:= (\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\mathbf{J}_m \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{Q}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\mathbf{M}_{q_n}\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n},\\
    I_4 &:= (\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\mathbf{C}_m \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{R}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)})\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)'(\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\mathbf{J}_n \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}'\mathbf{Q}_n)',\\
    I_5 &:= (\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\mathbf{C}_m \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{R}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)})\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(n)})'(\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\mathbf{C}_n \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}'\mathbf{R}_n)',\\
    I_6 &:= (\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}'\mathbf{C}_m \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{R}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)})\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\mathbf{M}_{q_n}\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n},\\
    I_7 &:= \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)'(\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\mathbf{J}_n \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}'\mathbf{Q}_n)',\\
    I_8 &:= \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(n)})'(\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\mathbf{C}_n \otimes \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}'\mathbf{R}_n)',\\
    I_9 &:= \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\mathbf{M}_{q_n}\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n},
\end{align*}
with $I_0 = I_1=I_4=I_7=0$ if $n\notin \mathcal{A}_G$. By Assumption (S1) on $T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)^{\otimes 2}$, and that the set of vectors
$\{\mathbf{e}_{k_c,d_n}'\mathbf{H}_{j,n}'(\mathbf{J}_n'\mathbf{J}_n)^{1/2}\otimes \mathbf{e}_{k_r,c_n}'\mathbf{H}_{q,n}'(\mathbf{Q}_n'\mathbf{Q}_n)^{1/2}\}_{c_n\in[k_r],d_n\in[k_c]}$ is linearly independent by Assumption (L1),
for each $n\neq m$ with $n\in\mathcal{A}_G$, there must exist $c_n\in[k_r], d_n\in[k_c]$ (through using (\ref{eqn:Thm9-eta_QJ}), so that we can replace $\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}$ by $\mathbf{Q}_n(\mathbf{Q}_n'\mathbf{Q}_n)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{q,n}\mathbf{e}_{k_r,c_n}$  and similarly $\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}$ by $\mathbf{J}_n(\mathbf{J}_n'\mathbf{J}_n)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{j,n}\mathbf{e}_{k_c,d_n}$) such that 
\begin{align}
    \max_{c_m\in[k_r], d_m\in[k_c]}I_0^2 &\asymp_P \max_{c_m\in[k_r], d_m\in[k_c]} \big\| \mathbf{e}_{k_c,d_m}'\mathbf{H}_{j,m}'(\mathbf{J}_m'\mathbf{J}_m)^{1/2}\otimes \mathbf{e}_{k_r,c_m}'\mathbf{H}_{q,m}'(\mathbf{Q}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m)^{1/2} \big\|^2\notag\\ &\cdot\big\| \mathbf{e}_{k_c,d_n}'\mathbf{H}_{j,n}'(\mathbf{J}_n'\mathbf{J}_n)^{1/2}\otimes \mathbf{e}_{k_r,c_n}'\mathbf{H}_{q,n}'(\mathbf{Q}_n'\mathbf{Q}_n)^{1/2} \big\|^2\notag\\
    &\gtrsim_P p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}\cdot p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,k_c}^{(n)}}, \label{eqn:Thm9-I0}
\end{align}
where the last line used (\ref{eqn:Thm9-2}), (\ref{eqn:Thm9-3}) and Assumption (L1). 
By Lemma \ref{lemma:prop_piecewise}-2 and \ref{lemma:prop_piecewise}-3, we also have
\begin{align*}
    \max_{c_m\in[k_r], d_m\in[k_c]}I_1^2 &\lesssim_P  p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}\cdot p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}),\;\; \max_{c_m\in[k_r], d_m\in[k_c]}I_2^2 \lesssim_P  p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}\cdot p_n^{\delta_{r,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{c,1}^{(n)}}R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}),\\
    \max_{c_m\in[k_r], d_m\in[k_c]}I_4^2 &\lesssim_P p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}\cdot p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}), \;\;
    \max_{c_m\in[k_r], d_m\in[k_c]}I_5^2
    \lesssim_P p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}\cdot p_n^{\delta_{r,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{c,1}^{(n)}}R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}).
\end{align*}
Using similar proof for Lemma \ref{lemma:bound_Delta}, and the fact that 
\[\max_{i\in[p_m],j\in[k_r]}|(\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m} - \mathbf{Q}_m(\mathbf{Q}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{q,m})_{ij}| = O_P(\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m} - \mathbf{Q}_m(\mathbf{Q}_m'\mathbf{Q}_m)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{q,m} \big\|) = o_P(\eta_{Q,m}) = o_P(1),\]
and similarly 
\[\max_{i\in[q_m],j\in[k_c]}|(\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m} - \mathbf{J}_m(\mathbf{J}_m'\mathbf{J}_m)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{j,m})_{ij}| = O_P(\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m} - \mathbf{J}_m(\mathbf{J}_m'\mathbf{J}_m)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{j,m} \big\|) = o_P(\eta_{J,m}) = o_P(1),\]
we can prove the following in parallel to (\ref{eqn:E1u}), (\ref{eqn:Em11En}) and (\ref{eqn:Em11Em})(details omitted):
\begin{align}
    \big\| T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{v}}_m\mathbf{u}_t' \big\|_F^2 &= O_P(T^{-1}p_m),\label{eqn:Thm9-d33}\\
    \big\| T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{v}}_m\widehat{\mathbf{v}}_n'\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'} \big\|_F^2 &= O_P(T^{-1}p_mp_n),\label{eqn:Thm9-d34}\\
    \big\| T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{v}}_m\widehat{\mathbf{v}}_m'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'} \big\|_F^2 &= O_P(p_m + T^{-1}p_m^2),\label{eqn:Thm9-d35}
\end{align}
where $\{\mathbf{u}_t\}$ is independent of $\{\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\}$ for each $m\in[M]$, and $\widehat{\mathbf{v}}_m,\widehat{\mathbf{v}}_n$ are unit vectors potentially dependent on $\{\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\}$ for some $m\in[M]$, but are consistent to some non-random unit vectors $\mathbf{v}_m$ and $\mathbf{v}_n$ respectively. 
With (\ref{eqn:Thm9-d33}) and (\ref{eqn:Thm9-d34}), we can show that
\begin{align*}
    \max_{c_m\in[k_r],d_m\in[k_c]} I_3^2 &\asymp_P p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}\cdot T^{-1}p_n, \;\;  \max_{c_m\in[k_r],d_m\in[k_c]} I_6^2 \asymp_P p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}\cdot T^{-1}p_n,\\
    \max_{c_m\in[k_r],d_m\in[k_c]} I_7^2 &\asymp_P T^{-1}p_m\cdot p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}, \;\; \max_{c_m\in[k_r],d_m\in[k_c]} I_8^2 \asymp_P T^{-1}p_m\cdot p_n^{\delta_{r,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{c,1}^{(n)}},\\
    \max_{c_m\in[k_r],d_m\in[k_c]} I_9^2 &\asymp_P T^{-1}p_mp_n.
\end{align*}
Using the rate assumption in the statement of the theorem, together with Assumption (R2), (\ref{eqn:Thm9-I0}) and the  rates for $I_1^2$ to $I_9^2$ result in
\begin{align*}
    &\sum_{n\neq m}\max_{c_m,c_m\in[k_r]}\max_{d_m,d_n\in[k_c]}\big\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\widecheck\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{m,\cdot d_m}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\widecheck\mathbf{Y}_t^{(n)'}\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}\big\}^2 \gtrsim_P p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}\max_{n\neq m,n\in\mathcal{A}_G}p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,k_c}^{(n)}}.
\end{align*}
On the other hand, if $m=k\notin\mathcal{A}_G$, then $I_0=I_1=I_2=I_3=0$, and the rates for $I_4^2$ to $I_9^2$ lead to 
\begin{align*}
    &\sum_{n\neq k}\max_{c_k,c_n\in[k_r]}\max_{d_k,d_n\in[k_c]}\big\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{k,\cdot c_k}'\widecheck\mathbf{Y}_t^{(k)}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{k,\cdot d_k}\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_{n,\cdot d_n}'\widecheck\mathbf{Y}_t^{(n)'}\widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_{n,\cdot c_n}\big\}^2\\ &\lesssim_P p_k^{\delta_{r,1}^{(k)}}q_k^{\delta_{c,1}^{(k)}}\max(\max_{n\in\mathcal{A}_G}p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}, \max_{n\neq k, n\notin \mathcal{A}_G} p_n^{\delta_{r,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{c,1}^{(n)}})R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star}).
\end{align*}
Now we rewrite $\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}$ as
\[\widecheck\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)} = \mathbf{U}_m\mathbf{H}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{V}_m' + \mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m},\]
where $\mathbf{U}_m := (\mathbf{Q}_m,\mathbf{R}_m)$, $\mathbf{V}_m := (\mathbf{J}_m,\mathbf{C}_m)$ and $\mathbf{H}_t^{(m)} := \textnormal{diag}(\mathbf{G}_t,\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)})$. We then have $\text{rank}(\mathbf{U}_m) \leq k_r+k_r^{(m)}$ and $\text{rank}(\mathbf{V}_m) \leq k_c + k_c^{(m)}$. Repeating our estimation methodology for just the local common components for individual groups using data $\{\widecheck\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}\}$, we can use the same proof as Theorem \ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init} to arrive at $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{U}}_m - \mathbf{U}_m(\mathbf{U}_m'\mathbf{U}_m)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{u,m} \big\|_F^2 = o_P(1)$ and $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{V}}_m - \mathbf{V}_m(\mathbf{V}_m'\mathbf{V}_m)^{-1/2}\mathbf{H}_{v,m} \big\|_F^2 = o_P(1)$, where $\mathbf{H}_{u,m}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{v,m}$ are two constant order invertible matrices. Then using (\ref{eqn:Thm9-d35}) and Assumption (R2) and (S1), similar to the treatment before, we have for $m\in\mathcal{A}_G$,
\begin{align*}
    &\max_{c_m\in[k_r+k_r^{(m)}], d_m\in[k_c+k_c^{(m)}]} \big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T(\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{Q}_m\mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{J}_m'\widehat{\mathbf{V}}_{m,\cdot d_m} + \widehat{\mathbf{U}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{R}_m\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{C}_m'\widehat{\mathbf{V}}_{m,\cdot d_m}\\ &\hspace{1.3in}+ \widehat{\mathbf{U}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\widehat{\mathbf{V}}_{m,\cdot d_m})^{2}\big)^2\\
    &\gtrsim_P p_m^{2\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{2\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}} - p_m^{2\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{2\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} - p_m - T^{-1}p_m^2 \asymp_P p_m^{2\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{2\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}.
\end{align*}
At the same time, if $m\notin\mathcal{A}_G$, 
\begin{align*}
    &\max_{c_m\in[k_r+k_r^{(m)}], d_m\in[k_c+k_c^{(m)}]} \big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T(\widehat{\mathbf{U}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{R}_m\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{C}_m'\widehat{\mathbf{V}}_{m,\cdot d_m} + \widehat{\mathbf{U}}_{m,\cdot c_m}'\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\widehat{\mathbf{V}}_{m,\cdot d_m})^{2}\big)^2\\
    &\gtrsim_P  p_m^{2\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{2\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} - p_m - T^{-1}p_m^2 \asymp_P p_m^{2\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{2\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}.
\end{align*}
This completes the proof of the theorem. $\square$

Consider $\widehat{r}_1$. We omit the details for $\widehat{\ell}_1$ since its proof is parallel to that for $\widehat{r}_1$. It is not difficult to see that, through the finiteness of $M$,
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:Thm10-1}
\max_{n\in[M]}\max_{n\neq m}\sigma_{r_1}^2\Big\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(n)'}\Big\} \lesssim \max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_{i}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{\mathbf{A}_1,m}) \lesssim \max_{m\in[M]}\max_{n\neq m}\sigma_{1}^2\Big\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(n)'}\Big\}, \;\; i\in[r_1],
\end{equation}
where $\sigma_i(\mathbf{A})$ denotes the $i$th largest singular value of the matrix $\mathbf{A}$. At the same time, for $i\in[r_1]$,
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:Thm10-2}
\sigma_{i}\Big\{\mathbf{A}_{1m}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{\otimes 2}\big)\mathbf{A}_{1n}'\Big\} - \sum_{j=2}^5I_j\leq \sigma_{i}\Big\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(n)'}\Big\} \leq
\sigma_{1}\Big\{\mathbf{A}_{1m}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{\otimes 2}\big)\mathbf{A}_{1n}'\Big\} + \sum_{j=2}^5I_j,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align*}
    I_2 &:= \sigma_1\Big\{\mathbf{A}_{2m}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(n)'}\big)\mathbf{A}_{2n}'\Big\}, \;\; I_3 := \sigma_1\Big\{ T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\big(\mathbf{A}_{1n}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t + \mathbf{A}_{2n}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(n)}\big)' \Big\},\\
    I_4 &:= \sigma_1\Big\{ T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T \big(\mathbf{A}_{1m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t + \mathbf{A}_{2m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\big)q_n^{-1}\1_{q_n}' \mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n} \Big\},\\
    I_5 &:= \sigma_1\big\{T^{-1}q_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}\big\}.
\end{align*}
Firstly, for $i\in[r_1]$, using (\ref{eqn:sample_covariance_G}) and Assumption (S1),
\begin{align*}
\sigma_i\Big\{\mathbf{A}_{1m}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{\otimes 2}\big)\mathbf{A}_{1n}'\Big\} &\geq \lambda_i^{1/2}(\mathbf{A}_{1m}'\mathbf{A}_{1m})\lambda_{r_1}^{1/2}(\mathbf{A}_{1n}'\mathbf{A}_{1n})\lambda_{r_1}(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{\otimes 2})\\&\gtrsim_P p_m^{\gamma_r/2}p_n^{\gamma_r/2}(1 + R(T,d_{r,\star})^{1/2}).
\end{align*}
Using (ME1), and  (\ref{eqn:sample_cross_moment_G}) on $I_2$, (\ref{eqn:E1u}) on $I_3$ and $I_4$ and (\ref{eqn:Em11En}) on $I_5$, 
\begin{align*}
    I_2 &:= O_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}/2}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}/2}R(T,d_{r,\star})^{1/2}) = o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r/2}p_n^{\gamma_r/2}),\\
    I_3 &:= O_P(T^{-1/2}p_m^{1/2}q_m^{-1/2}\cdot (p_n^{\gamma_r/2} + p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}/2})) = o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r/2}p_n^{\gamma_r/2}),\\
    I_4 &:= O_P(T^{-1/2}p_n^{1/2}q_n^{-1/2}\cdot(p_m^{\gamma_r/2} + p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}/2})) = o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r/2}p_n^{\gamma_r/2}),\\
    I_5 &:= O_P(T^{-1/2}p_m^{1/2}p_n^{1/2}q_m^{-1/2}q_n^{-1/2}) = o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r/2}p_n^{\gamma_r/2}),
\end{align*}
where we use (R1) as well for concluding that all the rates above are $o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r/2}p_n^{\gamma_r/2})$.
Hence (\ref{eqn:Thm10-2}) implies that
for $i\in[r_1]$,
\[
\max_{m,n\in[M], n\neq m}\sigma_i^2\Big\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(n)'}\Big\} \asymp_P \max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r},
\]
so that by (\ref{eqn:Thm10-1}), for $i\in[r_1]$,
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:Thm10-3}
\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_i(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{\mathbf{A}_1,m}) \asymp_P \max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}.
\end{equation}
On the other hand, we can write
\begin{align*}
    \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{\mathbf{A}_1,m} &=: (M-1)^{-1}\sum_{n\neq m} \Big(\mathbf{A}_{1m}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{\otimes 2}\big)\mathbf{A}_{1n}' + R_{m,n}\Big)^{\otimes 2}\\
    &= (M-1)^{-1}\sum_{n\neq m}\Big(\mathbf{A}_{1m}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{\otimes 2}\big)\mathbf{A}_{1n}'\mathbf{A}_{1n}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{\otimes 2}\big)\mathbf{A}_{1m}' + \mathbf{A}_{1m}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{\otimes 2}\big)\mathbf{A}_{1n}'R_{m,n}'\\ 
    &+ R_{m,n}\mathbf{A}_{1n}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{\otimes 2}\big)\mathbf{A}_{1m}' + R_{m,n}R_{m,n}'\Big), 
\end{align*}
so that for $i\in\{r_1+1,\ldots,p_m\}$,
\begin{align}
    \lambda_i(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{\mathbf{A}_1,m}) &\leq \lambda_1\Big\{(M-1)^{-1}\sum_{n\neq m}R_{m,n}R_{m,n}'\Big\} \leq \max_{n\neq m}\sigma_1^2(R_{m,n})\notag\\
    &\lesssim \max_{n\neq m}\sum_{j=2}^5I_j^2 = O_P(\max_{n\neq m}p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}R(T,d_{r,\star})), \label{eqn:Thm10-4}
\end{align}
where the last equality used Assumption (R1). Hence combining (\ref{eqn:Thm10-3}) and (\ref{eqn:Thm10-4}), we have
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_{i+1}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{\mathbf{A}_1,m})}{\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_i(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{\mathbf{A}_1,m})} \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
    \asymp_P 1, & \hbox{$i\in[r_1-1]$;} \\
    \lesssim_P \frac{\max_{m\in[M],n\neq m}p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}R(T,d_{r,\star})}{\max_{m\in[M],n\neq m}p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}}  = o_P(1), & \hbox{$i=r_1$,}
  \end{array}
\right. 
\end{align*}
where the equation for the case $i=r_1$ used Assumption (R1). This implies $P(\widehat{r}_1 \geq r_1) \rightarrow 1$. It is also clear that using (\ref{eqn:Thm10-3}) and (\ref{eqn:Thm10-4}), the ratio
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_{i+1}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{\mathbf{A}_1,m}) + \max_{m\neq n\in[M]}Cp_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}R(T,d_{r,\star})}{\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_i(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{\mathbf{A}_1,m}) + \max_{m\neq n\in[M]}Cp_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}R(T,d_{r,\star})} \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
    \asymp_P 1, & \hbox{$i\in[r_1-1]$;} \\
     = o_P(1), & \hbox{$i=r_1$,} \\
    \asymp_P 1, & \hbox{$i=r_1+1,\ldots,p_m$,}
  \end{array}
\right.  
\end{align*}
so that $P(\widehat{r}_1 = r_1) \rightarrow 1$, which aligns with the statement of adding $\max_{m\neq n\in[M]}CT^{-1}p_mp_n$ to the ratio when all factors are strong and all processes have short memory.

For $\widehat{r}_2^{(m)}$ (We omit $\widehat{\ell}_2^{(m)}$ since the proof is parallel), given $i\in[r_2^{(m)}]$, consider 
\begin{align}
   &J_1 - \sum_{i=2}^6J_i  \lesssim \sigma_i\Big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T(\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)})^{\otimes 2}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}^{\perp}\Big) \lesssim J_1 + \sum_{i=2}^6J_i, \; \text{ where } \label{eqn:Thm10-5}\\
       J_1 &:= \sigma_i\Big(\mathbf{A}_{2m}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)})^{\otimes 2}\big)\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{A}_{1m}^{\perp}\Big), \notag\\
       J_2 &:= \sigma_1\Big(\mathbf{A}_{1m}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{\otimes 2}\big)(\mathbf{A}_{1m}'\mathbf{A}_{1m})^{1/2}(\mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{A}_1,m}^{-1})'(\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} - \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m})'\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}^{\perp}\Big),\notag\\
       J_3 &:= \sigma_i\Big(\mathbf{A}_{2m}\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)})^{\otimes 2}\big)\mathbf{A}_{2m}'(\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}^{\perp} - \mathbf{A}_{1m}^{\perp})\Big), \notag\\
       J_4 &:= \sigma_1\Big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^Tq_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}(\mathbf{A}_{1m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t + \mathbf{A}_{2m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)})'\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}^{\perp}\Big),\notag\\
       J_5 &:= \sigma_1\Big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T (\mathbf{A}_{1m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t + \mathbf{A}_{2m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)})\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}q_m^{-1}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}^{\perp}\Big), \notag\\
       J_6 &:= \sigma_1\Big(T^{-1}q_m^{-2}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}^{\perp}\Big). \notag
\end{align}
For $i\in[r_2^{(m)}]$, using (\ref{eqn:sample_covariance_G}) and Assumption (S1) and (L1),
\begin{align*}
    J_1 &= \sigma_i\Big(\big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)})^{\otimes 2}\big)(\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{A}_{1m}^{\perp})^{\otimes 2}\Big) \geq \sigma_i^2(\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{A}_{1m}^{\perp})\sigma_{r_2^{(m)}}\Big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)})^{\otimes 2}\Big) 
    \asymp_P p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}},
\end{align*}
Also,
\begin{align*}
    J_2 &= O_P(p_m^{\gamma_r}\eta_{A_{1m}}^{1/2}) = o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}),\\
    J_3 &= O_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}^{\perp} - \mathbf{A}_{1m}^{\perp} \big\|) = O_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m} - \widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\mathbf{H}_{A_1,m} \big\|) = o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}),\\
    J_4, J_5 &= O_P(T^{-1/2}p_m^{1/2}q_m^{-1/2}\cdot(p_m^{\gamma_r/2} + p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}/2})) = o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}),\\
    J_6 &= O_P(p_m^{1/2}q_m^{-1} + T^{-1/2}p_mq_m^{-1}) = o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}),
\end{align*}
where $\eta_{A_{1m}}$ is the rate defined in Theorem \ref{thm:initial_A1m}, and the equation for $J_2$ used the assumption in the statement of the theorem. We use (\ref{eqn:E1u}) for $J_4$ and $J_5$ and that $\eta_{A_{1m}} = o(\nu_{r,m}^2)$ and (R1) to conclude that they are $o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}})$, while $J_6$ uses (\ref{eqn:Em11Em}).

On the other hand, for $i\in\{r_2^{(m)+1},\ldots,p_m\}$,
\begin{align}
    \sigma_i\Big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T(\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)})^{\otimes 2}\widehat{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}^{\perp}\Big) \lesssim_P\sum_{i=2}^6 J_i = o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}). \label{eqn:Thm10-6}
\end{align}
The rates for $J_1$ to $J_6$, combined with (\ref{eqn:Thm10-5}) and (\ref{eqn:Thm10-6}), allow us to conclude that
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\lambda_{j+1}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m})}{\lambda_j(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m})} \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
    \asymp_P 1, & \hbox{$j\in[r_2^{(m)}-1]$;} \\
    = o_P(1), & \hbox{$j=r_2^{(m)}$,}
  \end{array}
\right.
\end{align*}
so that $P(\widehat{r}_2^{(m)} \geq r_2^{(m)}) \rightarrow 1$. It is also clear that when all factors are strong and all processes have short memory, $\eta_{A_{1m}} = T^{-1}$, so that the rate in (\ref{eqn:Thm10-6}) is $O_P(T^{-1/2}p_m)$, and 
the ratio
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\lambda_{j+1}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}) + T^{-1}p_m^2}{\lambda_j(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}) + T^{-1}p_m^2} \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
    \asymp_P 1, & \hbox{$j\in[r_2^{(m)}-1]$;} \\
    = o_P(1), & \hbox{$j=r_2^{(m)}$;}\\
    \asymp_P 1 & \hbox{$j=r_2^{(m)}+1,\ldots,p_m$,}
  \end{array}
\right.
\end{align*}
so that $P(\widehat{r}_2^{(m)} = r_2^{(m)}) \rightarrow 1$.

Now for $\widehat{k}_r$ (we omit the details for $\widehat{k}_c$ since it involves parallel arguments), consider for $i\in[k_r]$,
\begin{align}
  \max_{m\in[M]}\max_{n\neq m}\sigma_i^2\Big\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)}\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(n)'}\Big\} &\lesssim \max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{Q,m}) \notag\\&\lesssim \max_{m\in[M]}\max_{n\neq m}\sigma_1^2\Big\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)}\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(n)'}\Big\}.\label{eqn:Thm10-7}
\end{align}
At the same time, for $i\in[k_r]$, 
\begin{align*}
    K_{1,i} - \sum_{j=2}^9K_j &\leq \sigma_i\Big\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)}\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\widecheck{\mathbf{Y}}_t^{(n)'}\Big\} \leq K_{1,i} + \sum_{j=2}^9K_j, \; \text{ where}\\
    K_{1,i} &:= \sigma_i\Big( (\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)'}\mathbf{J}_m \otimes \mathbf{Q}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)^{\otimes 2}(\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\mathbf{J}_n \otimes \mathbf{Q}_n)'\Big),\\
    K_2 &:= \sigma_1\Big( (\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)'}\mathbf{J}_m \otimes \mathbf{Q}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(n)})'(\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\mathbf{C}_n \otimes \mathbf{R}_n)'\Big),\\
    K_3 &:= \sigma_1\Big( (\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)'}\mathbf{J}_m \otimes \mathbf{Q}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{q_n}\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}\Big),\\
    K_4 &:= \sigma_1\Big( (\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)'}\mathbf{C}_m \otimes \mathbf{R}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)})\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)'(\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\mathbf{J}_n \otimes \mathbf{Q}_n)'\Big),\\
    K_5 &:= \sigma_1\Big( (\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)'}\mathbf{C}_m \otimes \mathbf{R}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)})\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(n)})'(\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\mathbf{C}_n \otimes \mathbf{R}_n)'\Big),\\
    K_6 &:=\sigma_1\Big( (\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)'}\mathbf{C}_m \otimes \mathbf{R}_m)T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)})\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{q_n}\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}\Big),\\
    K_7 &:= \sigma_1\Big( \mathbf{M}_{p_m}\cdot T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)}\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)'(\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\mathbf{J}_n \otimes \mathbf{Q}_n)' \Big),\\
    K_8 &:= \sigma_1\Big( \mathbf{M}_{p_m}\cdot T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)}\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)})'(\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\mathbf{C}_n \otimes \mathbf{R}_n)' \Big),\\
    K_9 &:= \sigma_1\Big( \mathbf{M}_{p_m}\cdot T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{M}_{q_m}\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)}\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{q_n}\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\cdot \mathbf{M}_{p_n}\Big).
\end{align*}
To bound the above terms, firstly, we have $\|\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)'}\mathbf{J}_m\| \asymp q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}/2}$ and $\|\mathbf{v}_a^{(m)'}\mathbf{Q}_m\| \asymp p_m^{\delta_{q,1}/2}$ by construction, and hence using (\ref{eqn:sample_covariance_G}) and Assumption (S1), for $i\in[k_r]$,
\begin{align*}
    K_{1,i} &\gtrsim_P \lambda_1(\mathbf{J}_n'\mathbf{v}_b^{(n)}\mathbf{v}_b^{(m)}\mathbf{J}_m')\lambda_i(\mathbf{Q}_n'\mathbf{Q}_m)\lambda_{k_r+k_c}\Big(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{G}_t)^{\otimes 2}\Big)\\
    &\gtrsim_P q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}/2}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}/2}p_m^{\delta_{q,i}^{(m)}/2}p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)}/2}.
\end{align*}
Also, using (\ref{eqn:sample_cross_moment_G}), (\ref{eqn:Thm9-d33}) and (\ref{eqn:Thm9-d34}),
\begin{align*}
    K_2 &= O_P(R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star})^{1/2}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}/2}q_n^{\delta_{c,1}^{(n)}/2}p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}/2}p_n^{\delta_{r,1}^{(n)}/2}), &\; K_3 &= O_P(T^{-1/2}p_n^{1/2}q_n^{1/2}\cdot q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}/2}p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}/2}),\\
    K_4 &= O_P(R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star})^{1/2}q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}/2}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}/2}p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}/2}p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}/2}), & K_5 &= O_P(R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star})^{1/2}q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}/2}q_n^{\delta_{c,1}^{(n)}/2}p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}/2}p_n^{\delta_{r,1}^{(n)}/2}),\\
    K_6 &= O_P(T^{-1/2}p_n^{1/2}q_n^{1/2}\cdot q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}/2}p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}/2}), & K_7 &= O_P(T^{-1/2}p_m^{1/2}q_m^{1/2}\cdot q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}/2}p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}/2}),\\
    K_8 &= O_P(T^{-1/2}p_m^{1/2}q_m^{1/2}\cdot q_n^{\delta_{c,1}^{(n)}/2}p_n^{\delta_{r,1}^{(n)}/2}), & K_9 &= O_P(T^{-1/2}p_m^{1/2}p_n^{1/2}q_m^{1/2}q_n^{1/2}).
\end{align*}
By the assumption in the statement of the theorem, we also have for $i\in[k_r]$,
\[\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m,j=2,\ldots,9}K_j^2 = o_P(\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}p_m^{\delta_{q,i}^{(m)}}p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)}}) \lesssim_P \max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}K_{1,i}^2\asymp_P \max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{Q,m}),\]
after using (\ref{eqn:Thm10-7}).
On the other hand, for $i\in\{k_r+1,\ldots,p_m\}$,
\begin{align*}
    \max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{Q,m}) &\lesssim_P \max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m,j=2,\ldots,9}K_j^2\\
    &= O_P\Big(\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}\big(T^{-1}p_mp_nq_mq_n + R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star})p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}} p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}\big)\Big).
\end{align*}
With these intermediate results, we can conclude that for $i\in[k_r-1]$,
\begin{align}
    &\frac{\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_{i+1}(\mathbf{X}_{Q,m})}{\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{Q,m})} 
    \gtrsim_P \frac{\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}p_m^{\delta_{q,i+1}^{(m)}}p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)}}}{\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}}\notag\\
    &\succ_P \frac{\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}\big(T^{-1}p_mp_nq_mq_n + R(T,d_{\text{fac},\star})p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}} p_n^{\delta_{q,1}^{(n)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}\big)}{\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}q_n^{\delta_{j,1}^{(n)}}p_m^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)}}p_n^{\delta_{q,k_r}^{(m)}}} \;\; (=o_P(1))\notag\\
    &\gtrsim_P \frac{\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_{k_r+1}(\mathbf{X}_{Q,m})}{\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_{k_r}(\mathbf{X}_{Q,m})}, \label{eqn:Thm10-8}
\end{align}
where the second line is by the rate assumption in the statement of the theorem. The above implies \linebreak $P(\widehat{k}_r \geq k_r) \rightarrow 1$. When all factors are strong and all processes have short memory, then for $i\in\{k_r+1,\ldots,p_m\}$, 
\[\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{Q,m}) = O_P(\max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}T^{-1}p_mp_nq_mq_n),\]
so we can easily see that 
\[\frac{\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_{i+1}(\mathbf{X}_{Q,m}) + \max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}CT^{-1}p_mp_nq_mq_n}{\max_{m\in[M]}\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{Q,m}) + \max_{m,n\in[M],n\neq m}CT^{-1}p_mp_nq_mq_n} \asymp_P 1 \]
for $i\in\{k_r+1,\ldots,p_m\}$. This, together with (\ref{eqn:Thm10-8}), proves $P(\widehat{k}_r = k_r) \rightarrow 1$.


For $\widehat{k}_c^{(m)}$ (the proof for $\widehat{k}_r^{(m)}$ runs parallel and is omitted), consider the decomposition of $\mathbf{X}_{C,m}$ (\ref{eqn:XCm-decomposition}), where we can see from the rate in (\ref{eqn: local_C_J1_eigenvalues}) that for $i\in[k_c^{(m)}]$, 
\[\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{C,m}) \asymp_P q_m^{\delta_{c,i}^{(m)}}p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}},\]
while for $i\in\{k_c^{(m)}+1,\ldots,q_m\}$, the rates $\big\| \mathcal{J}_5 \big\|_F^2$ and $\big\| \mathcal{J}_6 \big\|_F^2$ from (\ref{eqn: local_C_J5}) and (\ref{eqn: local_C_J6}) respectively dominated, and hence we have
\[\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{C,m}) = O_P(q_m^{1-\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} + T^{-1/2}q_mp_m^{1/2} + q_m^{(1+\delta_{j,1}^{(m)})/2}p_m^{(1+\delta_{q,1}^{(m)})/2}\eta_{Q,m}^{1/2}).\]
With these two intermediate results, we can conclude that for $i\in[k_c^{(m)}-1]$,
\begin{align}
    \frac{\lambda_{i+1}(\mathbf{X}_{C,m})}{\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{C,m})} \gtrsim_P \frac{q_m^{\delta_{c,i+1}^{(m)}}p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}}{q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}} &\succ_P \frac{q_m^{1-\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}} + T^{-1/2}q_mp_m^{1/2} + q_m^{(1+\delta_{j,1}^{(m)})/2}p_m^{(1+\delta_{q,1}^{(m)})/2}\eta_{Q,m}^{1/2}}{q_m^{\delta_{c,k_c^{(m)}}^{(m)}}p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}}  \;\;(=o_P(1))\notag\\
    &\gtrsim_P \frac{\lambda_{k_c^{(m)}+1}(\mathbf{X}_{C,m})}{\lambda_{k_c^{(m)}}(\mathbf{X}_{C,m})}, \label{eqn:Thm10-9}
\end{align}
where the second line is by the rate assumption in the statement of the theorem. The above implies $P(\hat{k}_c^{(m)}\geq k_c^{(m)}) \rightarrow 1$. When all factors are strong and all processes have short memory, $\eta_{Q,m} = T^{-1}$, and hence for $i\in\{k_c^{(m)}+1,\ldots,q_m\}$,
\[\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{C,m}) = O_P(T^{-1/2}p_mq_m).\]
Hence, we can see that for $i\in\{k_c^{(m)}+1,\ldots,q_m\}$,
\[\frac{\lambda_{i+1}(\mathbf{X}_{C,m}) + CT^{-1/2}p_mq_m}{\lambda_i(\mathbf{X}_{C,m}) + CT^{-1/2}p_mq_m} \asymp_P 1.\]
This, together with (\ref{eqn:Thm10-9}), proves $P(\hat{k}_c^{(m)} = k_c^{(m)}) \rightarrow 1$. $\square$

{(Contraction properties of orthogonal projectors)}\label{prop:projector_prop}
    Let $\mathbf{A}\in\mathbbm{R}^{a\times b}$ be any matrix, and let $\mathbf{P}\in\mathbbm{R}^{a\times a}$ and $\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbbm{R}^{b\times b}$ be orthogonal projectors. Then,
    \begin{align}
        \big\| \mathbf{P}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{Q} \big\| \leq \big\| \mathbf{A} \big\|, \qquad
        \big\| \mathbf{P}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{Q} \big\|_F \leq \big\| \mathbf{A} \big\|_F.
        \label{eqn:projector_contraction}
    \end{align}
    In particular, for any positive integer $n$, define $\mathbf{M}_n:=\mathbf{I}_n-n^{-1}\1_n\1_n'$. Then, $\mathbf{M}_n$ is the orthogonal projector onto $\1_n^\perp$, and
    \begin{align}
        \mathbf{M}_n'=\mathbf{M}_n,\qquad
        \mathbf{M}_n^2=\mathbf{M}_n,\qquad
        \mathbf{M}_n\1_n=\0,\qquad
        \big\| \mathbf{M}_n \big\|\leq 1.
        \label{eqn:Mn_properties}
    \end{align}
    Moreover, 
    \begin{align}
        \big\| \mathbf{M}_n \big\|_{\max} \leq1, \qquad
        \big\| \mathbf{M}_n \big\|_1=\big\| \mathbf{M}_n \big\|_{\infty}=2(1-n^{-1})\leq 2.
        \label{eqn:Mn_l1_lmax_norms}
    \end{align}

Since $\mathbf{P}$ and $\mathbf{Q}$ are orthogonal projectors, $\big\| \mathbf{P} \big\|\leq1$ and $\big\| \mathbf{Q} \big\|\leq1$. Hence,
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \mathbf{P}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{Q} \big\|
    &\leq \big\| \mathbf{P} \big\|\big\| \mathbf{A} \big\|\big\| \mathbf{Q} \big\|
    \leq \big\| \mathbf{A} \big\|,\\
    \big\| \mathbf{P}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{Q} \big\|_F
    &\leq \big\| \mathbf{P} \big\|\big\| \mathbf{A} \big\|_F\big\| \mathbf{Q} \big\|
    \leq \big\| \mathbf{A} \big\|_F.
\end{align*}

By direct calculation, $\mathbf{M}_n'=\mathbf{M}_n$, $\mathbf{M}_n^2=\mathbf{M}_n$, and $\mathbf{M}_n\1_n=\0$. Thus, $\mathbf{M}_n$ is the orthogonal projector onto $\1_n^\perp$. Since the eigenvalues of an orthogonal projector belong to $\{0,1\}$, we have $\big\| \mathbf{M}_n \big\|\leq1$. Finally, its diagonal entries are $1-1/n$ and its off-diagonal entries are $-1/n$. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \mathbf{M}_n \big\|_{\max}\leq 1,\qquad
    \big\| \mathbf{M}_n \big\|_1=\big\| \mathbf{M}_n \big\|_{\infty}
    =(1-n^{-1})+(n-1)n^{-1}
    =2(1-n^{-1})\leq2.
\end{align*}
This completes the proof. $\square$

{(Covariance bounds for $\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}$)}\label{lemma:correlation_Etm}
    Let Assumptions~(E1) and (E2) hold. Then there exist a nonnegative sequence $\{\rho(h):h\in\mathbbm{Z}\}$ and a constant $C<\infty$, such that $\sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(h)<\infty$, $\sup_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(h)\le 1$, and, uniformly over $h\in\mathbbm{Z}$ and all $t$ such that $t,t+h\in[T]$, 
    \begin{align}
        \max_{m\in[M]}\sup_{i\in[p_m]}\sup_{j\in[q_m]}\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
        \Big| \mathbbm{E}\big(E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,kl}^{(m)}\big) \Big|
        \le C\rho(h).
        \label{eqn:error_cov_summable}
    \end{align}
    In particular, uniformly over all $t$ such that $t,t+h\in[T]$,
    \begin{align}
        \max_{m\in[M]}\sup_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\sup_{i\in[p_m]}\sup_{j\in[q_m]}\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
        \Big| \mathbbm{E}\big(E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,kl}^{(m)}\big) \Big|
        \le C.
        \label{eqn:error_cov_uniform}
    \end{align}
    Moreover, uniformly over $h\in\mathbbm{Z}$, all $t$ such that $t,t+h\in[T]$, the covariance of the error products satisfies
    \begin{align}
        \max_{m,n\in[M]}\sup_{i\in[p_m]}\sup_{j\in[q_m]}\sup_{k\in[p_n]}\sup_{l\in[q_n]}
        \sum_{a=1}^{q_m}\sum_{b=1}^{q_n}
        \left|\textnormal{Cov}\left(
        E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t+h,kl}^{(n)},
        E_{t,ia}^{(m)}E_{t,kb}^{(n)}
        \right)\right|
        \le C\rho(h).
        \label{eqn:error_product_cov}
    \end{align}

For $h\in\mathbbm{Z}$, let $\gamma_e(h):=\sum_{w\geq 0}|a_{e,w}a_{e,w+|h|}|$, and for each $m\in[M]$, define analogously $\gamma_e^{(m)}(h):=\sum_{w\geq 0}|a_{e,w}^{(m)}a_{e,w+|h|}^{(m)}|$ and $\gamma_\epsilon(h)^{(m)}:=\sum_{w\geq 0}|a_{\epsilon,w}^{(m)}a_{\epsilon,w+|h|}^{(m)}|$. By (E2),
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\gamma_e(h)
    &= \sum_{w\geq0}|a_{e,w}|^2
    +2\sum_{h=1}^\infty\sum_{w\geq0}|a_{e,w}||a_{e,w+h}| \\
    &= \sum_{w\geq0}|a_{e,w}|^2
    +2\sum_{0\leq w<r<\infty}|a_{e,w}||a_{e,r}| 
    = \bigg(\sum_{w\geq0}|a_{e,w}|\bigg)^2
    \leq c^2<\infty,
\end{align*}
and analogously, for every $m\in[M]$, $\sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\gamma_e^{(m)}(h)\leq c^2$ and $\sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h)\leq c^2$, where $c$ is the constant in (E2). Moreover, by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality and (E2),
\begin{align}
    \gamma_e(h)
    \leq \big(\sum_{w\geq 0} a_{e,w}^2\big)^{1/2}
    \big(\sum_{w\geq 0} a_{e,w+|h|}^2\big)^{1/2}
    \leq 1,
    \label{eqn:lag_kernel_bound}
\end{align}
so that $\sup_h \gamma_e(h)\leq 1$. The same argument gives $\sup_h\gamma_e^{(m)}(h)\leq 1$ and $\sup_h\gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h)\leq 1$. Since $M$ is fixed, define
\begin{align*}
    \rho(h):=\frac{1}{3}\left\{\gamma_e(h)+\max_{m\in[M]}\gamma_e^{(m)}(h)+\max_{m\in[M]}\gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h)\right\}.
\end{align*}
Then $\rho(h)\geq0$, $\sup_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(h)\leq1$, and
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(h)
    \leq \frac{1}{3}\left\{
    \sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\gamma_e(h)
    +\sum_{m=1}^M\sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\gamma_e^{(m)}(h)
    +\sum_{m=1}^M\sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h)
    \right\}
    \leq \frac{1+2M}{3}c^2<\infty.
\end{align*}

We next prove \eqref{eqn:error_cov_summable}. Fix $m\in[M]$. By (E1), write
\begin{align}
    E_{t,ij}^{(m)} 
    = (\mathbf{Q}_{e,m}\mathbf{G}_{e,t}\mathbf{J}_{e,m}')_{ij} + (\mathbf{R}_{e,m}\mathbf{F}_{e,t}^{(m)}\mathbf{C}_{e,m}')_{ij} + (\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\epsilon,m}\circ \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t^{(m)})_{ij} =: U_{t,ij}^{(m)} + V_{t,ij}^{(m)} + W_{t,ij}^{(m)}.
    \label{eqn:Et_decomposition}
\end{align}
Since the three component processes are mutually independent and have mean zero under (E1) and (E2), we have
\begin{align}\label{eqn:EtEt}
    \mathbbm{E}(E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)} E_{t,kl}^{(m)}) 
    = \mathbbm{E}(U_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}U_{t,kl}^{(m)}) + \mathbbm{E}(V_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}V_{t,kl}^{(m)}) + \mathbbm{E}(W_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}W_{t,kl}^{(m)}).
\end{align}
We bound the three terms on the r.h.s.\ separately. First, 
\begin{align*}
    U_{t,ij}^{(m)} = \sum_{a=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{b=1}^{k_{e,c}}(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ia}(\mathbf{G}_{e,t})_{ab}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jb}.
\end{align*}
Hence 
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}(U_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}U_{t,kl}^{(m)}) = \sum_{a=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{b=1}^{k_{e,c}}\sum_{c=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ia}(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{kc}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jb}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{ld}\mathbbm{E}\{(\mathbf{G}_{e,t+h})_{ab}(\mathbf{G}_{e,t})_{cd}\}.
\end{align*}
By (E2), we have $\mathbbm{E}\{(\mathbf{X}_{e,r})_{ab}(\mathbf{X}_{e,s})_{cd}\}= \mathbbm{1}\{r=s\}\mathbbm{1}\{a=c,b=d\}$, so that
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\{(\mathbf{G}_{e,t+h})_{ab}(\mathbf{G}_{e,t})_{cd}\}
    &= \sum_{u\geq0}\sum_{v\geq0}
    a_{e,u}a_{e,v}
    \mathbbm{E}\{(\mathbf{X}_{e,t+h-u})_{ab}(\mathbf{X}_{e,t-v})_{cd}\} \\
    &= \mathbbm{1}\{a=c,b=d\}
    \sum_{u\geq0}\sum_{v\geq0}
    a_{e,u}a_{e,v}\mathbbm{1}\{t+h-u=t-v\} \\
    &= \mathbbm{1}\{a=c,b=d\}\sum_{w\geq0}a_{e,w+|h|}a_{e,w},
\end{align*}
where the last equality follows by setting $w=v$ when $h\geq 0$ and
$w=u$ when $h<0$.
Hence,
\begin{align*}
    \big|\mathbbm{E}\{(\mathbf{G}_{e,t+h})_{ab}(\mathbf{G}_{e,t})_{cd}\}\big|
    \leq \gamma_e(h)\mathbbm{1}\{a=c,b=d\}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
    \big|\mathbbm{E}(U_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}U_{t,kl}^{(m)})\big|
    \leq \gamma_e(h)\sum_{a=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{b=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    |(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ia}||(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ka}||(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jb}||(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{lb}|.
\end{align*}
Taking absolute values and summing over $k$ and $l$, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}\big|\mathbbm{E}(U_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}U_{t,kl}^{(m)})\big| \\ 
    &\leq \gamma_e(h)\sum_{a=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{b=1}^{k_{e,c}} |(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ia}||(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jb}|\Big(\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}|(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ka}|\Big)\Big(\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}|(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{lb}|\Big) \\
    &\leq \gamma_e(h)\Big(\sum_{a=1}^{k_{e,r}} |(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ia}|\Big)\Big(\max_{a}\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}|(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ka}|\Big)\Big(\sum_{b=1}^{k_{e,c}} |(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jb}|\Big)\Big(\max_{b}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m} |(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{lb}|\Big) \\
    &\leq \gamma_e(h)\big(k_{e,r}\|\mathbf{Q}_{e,m}\|_1\big)\|\mathbf{Q}_{e,m}\|_1\big(k_{e,c}\|\mathbf{J}_{e,m}\|_1\big)\|\mathbf{J}_{e,m}\|_1 
    = O(\gamma_e(h)),
\end{align*}
where we use $\sum_{k}|(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ka}| \leq \big\| \mathbf{Q}_{e,m} \big\|_1$ and $\sum_{l}|(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{lb}| \leq \big\| \mathbf{J}_{e,m} \big\|_1$ in the first inequality, $\sum_a|(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ia}|\leq k_{e,r}\big\| \mathbf{Q}_{e,m} \big\|_1$ and $\sum_b|(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jb}|\leq k_{e,c}\big\| \mathbf{J}_{e,m} \big\|_1$ in the second inequality, and Assumption~(E1) in the last equality. By the same argument, 
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}|\mathbbm{E}(V_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}V_{t,kl}^{(m)})|=O(\gamma_e^{(m)}(h)).
\end{align*}
Finally, (E2) gives
\begin{align*}
    |\mathbbm{E}(\epsilon_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}\epsilon_{t,kl}^{(m)})| \leq \gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h)\mathbbm{1}\{(i,j)=(k,l)\}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}|\mathbbm{E}(W_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}W_{t,kl}^{(m)})| 
    &\leq \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}|(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\epsilon,m})_{ij}(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\epsilon,m})_{kl}|\gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h)\mathbbm{1}\{(i,j)=(k,l)\} \\
    &= \gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h)(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\epsilon,m})_{ij}^2 = O(\gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h)),
\end{align*}
by the uniform boundedness of $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\epsilon,m}$ in (E1). Combining the above bounds, there exists an absolute constant $C$ such that
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
    |\mathbbm{E}(U_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}U_{t,kl}^{(m)})|
    &\leq \frac{C}{3}\gamma_e(h), \\
    \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
    |\mathbbm{E}(V_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}V_{t,kl}^{(m)})|
    &\leq \frac{C}{3}\gamma_e^{(m)}(h), \\
    \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
    |\mathbbm{E}(W_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}W_{t,kl}^{(m)})|
    &\leq \frac{C}{3}\gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h),
\end{align*}
uniformly over $m\in[M]$, $h\in\mathbbm{Z}$, $i\in[p_m]$, and $j\in[q_m]$. By \eqref{eqn:EtEt} and the triangle inequality,
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
    |\mathbbm{E}(E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,kl}^{(m)})|
    &\leq 
    \frac{C}{3}\left(\gamma_e(h)+\gamma_e^{(m)}(h)+\gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h)\right) \\
    &\leq 
    \frac{C}{3}\left(\gamma_e(h)+\max_{m\in[M]}\gamma_e^{(m)}(h)+\max_{m\in[M]}\gamma^{(m)}_\epsilon(h)\right) 
    = C\rho(h) ,
\end{align*}
which concludes \eqref{eqn:error_cov_summable}. Since $\sup_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(h)\leq1$, \eqref{eqn:error_cov_uniform} follows immediately. 

We next show \eqref{eqn:error_product_cov}. For $h\in\mathbbm{Z}$, define $\kappa_e(h):=\sum_{w\ge0}a_{e,w}^2a_{e,w+|h|}^2$, and for each $m\in[M]$, define $\kappa_e^{(m)}(h):=\sum_{w\ge0}\left(a_{e,w}^{(m)}\right)^2\left(a_{e,w+|h|}^{(m)}\right)^2$ and $\kappa_\epsilon^{(m)}(h):=\sum_{w\ge0}\left(a_{\epsilon,w}^{(m)}\right)^2\left(a_{\epsilon,w+|h|}^{(m)}\right)^2$. Since 
\begin{align*}
    \kappa_e(h)\le\gamma_e(h)^2\le\gamma_e(h)\le 3\rho(h),
\end{align*}
and analogously $\kappa_e^{(m)}(h)\le 3\rho(h)$ and $\kappa_\epsilon^{(m)}(h)\le 3\rho(h)$ for every $m\in[M]$, we have 
\begin{align*}
    \kappa_e(h)+\max_{m\in[M]}\kappa_e^{(m)}(h)+\max_{m\in[M]}\kappa_\epsilon^{(m)}(h)
    \le 9\rho(h).
\end{align*}
Moreover, for any two sequences $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$ chosen from
$\{\gamma_e, \gamma_e^{(m)}, \gamma_\epsilon^{(m)}:m\in[M]\}$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \gamma_1(h)\gamma_2(h)\le 9\rho(h)^2\le 9\rho(h),
\end{align*}
where the second inequality used $\rho(h)\le 1$.

Recall the decomposition in \eqref{eqn:Et_decomposition}. Expanding each of the four error entries in the covariance yields finitely many terms. We first consider
\begin{align*}
    S_U(h):=\sum_{a=1}^{q_m}\sum_{b=1}^{q_n}
    \left|\textnormal{Cov}\left(U_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}U_{t+h,kl}^{(n)},U_{t,ia}^{(m)}U_{t,kb}^{(n)}\right)\right|.
\end{align*}
By the definition of $U_{t,ij}^{(m)}$,
\begin{align*}
    U_{t,ij}^{(m)}
    =\sum_{c=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ic}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jd}(\mathbf{G}_{e,t})_{cd}.
\end{align*}
It follows that
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\textnormal{Cov}\left(
    U_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}U_{t+h,kl}^{(n)},
    U_{t,ia}^{(m)}U_{t,kb}^{(n)}
    \right) \\
    &=\sum_{c_1=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d_1=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    \sum_{c_2=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d_2=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    \sum_{c_3=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d_3=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    \sum_{c_4=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d_4=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ic_1}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jd_1}
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,n})_{kc_2}(\mathbf{J}_{e,n})_{ld_2} \\
    &\qquad \cdot
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ic_3}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{ad_3}
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,n})_{kc_4}(\mathbf{J}_{e,n})_{bd_4} \\
    &\qquad \cdot
    \textnormal{Cov}\left(
    (\mathbf{G}_{e,t+h})_{c_1d_1}(\mathbf{G}_{e,t+h})_{c_2d_2},
    (\mathbf{G}_{e,t})_{c_3d_3}(\mathbf{G}_{e,t})_{c_4d_4}
    \right).
\end{align*}
By (E2), we have
\begin{align*}
    &\left|
    \textnormal{Cov}\left(
    (\mathbf{G}_{e,t+h})_{c_1d_1}(\mathbf{G}_{e,t+h})_{c_2d_2},
    (\mathbf{G}_{e,t})_{c_3d_3}(\mathbf{G}_{e,t})_{c_4d_4}
    \right)
    \right| \\
    &\leq C\gamma_e(h)^2\mathbbm{1}\{(c_1,d_1)=(c_3,d_3),\,(c_2,d_2)=(c_4,d_4)\} \\
    &\quad + C\gamma_e(h)^2\mathbbm{1}\{(c_1,d_1)=(c_4,d_4),\,(c_2,d_2)=(c_3,d_3)\} \\
    &\quad + C\kappa_e(h)\mathbbm{1}\{(c_1,d_1)=(c_2,d_2)=(c_3,d_3)=(c_4,d_4)\}.
\end{align*}
Substituting the above into the preceding expansion and using the triangle inequality, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    S_U(h)
    &\leq C\gamma_e(h)^2
    \sum_{c_1=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d_1=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    \sum_{c_2=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d_2=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    |(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ic_1}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jd_1}
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,n})_{kc_2}(\mathbf{J}_{e,n})_{ld_2}| \\
    &\qquad \cdot
    \sum_{a=1}^{q_m}|(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ic_1}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{ad_1}|
    \sum_{b=1}^{q_n}|(\mathbf{Q}_{e,n})_{kc_2}(\mathbf{J}_{e,n})_{bd_2}| \\
    &\quad + C\gamma_e(h)^2
    \sum_{c_1=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d_1=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    \sum_{c_2=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d_2=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    |(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ic_1}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jd_1}
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,n})_{kc_2}(\mathbf{J}_{e,n})_{ld_2}| \\
    &\qquad \cdot
    \sum_{a=1}^{q_m}|(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ic_2}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{ad_2}|
    \sum_{b=1}^{q_n}|(\mathbf{Q}_{e,n})_{kc_1}(\mathbf{J}_{e,n})_{bd_1}| \\
    &\quad + C\kappa_e(h)
    \sum_{c=1}^{k_{e,r}}\sum_{d=1}^{k_{e,c}}
    |(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ic}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{jd}
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,n})_{kc}(\mathbf{J}_{e,n})_{ld}| \\
    &\qquad \cdot
    \sum_{a=1}^{q_m}|(\mathbf{Q}_{e,m})_{ic}(\mathbf{J}_{e,m})_{ad}|
    \sum_{b=1}^{q_n}|(\mathbf{Q}_{e,n})_{kc}(\mathbf{J}_{e,n})_{bd}|.
\end{align*}
Since $k_{e,r}$ and $k_{e,c}$ are fixed, and since $\|\mathbf{Q}_{e,m}\|_1=\|\mathbf{J}_{e,m}\|_1=O(1)$ and $\|\mathbf{Q}_{e,n}\|_1=\|\mathbf{J}_{e,n}\|_1=O(1)$ for all $m,n\in[M]$, we have, uniformly over $i\in[p_m]$, $j\in[q_m]$, $k\in[p_n]$, and $l\in[q_n]$,
\begin{align}
    S_U(h)\leq C\gamma_e(h)^2+C\kappa_e(h)\leq C\rho(h).
    \label{eqn:four_global_error_bound}
\end{align}
Next, define
\begin{align*}
    S_V(h):=\sum_{a=1}^{q_m}\sum_{b=1}^{q_n}
    \left|\textnormal{Cov}\left(
    V_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}V_{t+h,kl}^{(n)},
    V_{t,ia}^{(m)}V_{t,kb}^{(n)}
    \right)\right|.
\end{align*}
When $m=n$, the above arguments apply to the local error process. When $m\neq n$, the cross-group independence of the local error innovation processes in (E2) yields, uniformly over $i\in[p_m]$, $j\in[q_m]$, $k\in[p_n]$, and $l\in[q_n]$,
\begin{align}
    S_V(h)\le C\gamma_e^{(m)}(h)\gamma_e^{(n)}(h)
    +C\mathbbm{1}\{m=n\}\kappa_e^{(m)}(h)
    \le C\rho(h).
    \label{eqn:four_local_error_bound}
\end{align}
Similarly, define
\begin{align*}
    S_W(h):=\sum_{a=1}^{q_m}\sum_{b=1}^{q_n}
    \left|\textnormal{Cov}\left(
    W_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}W_{t+h,kl}^{(n)},
    W_{t,ia}^{(m)}W_{t,kb}^{(n)}
    \right)\right|.
\end{align*}
Using the independence and uniformly bounded fourth moments of the idiosyncratic innovations in (E2), together with the uniform boundedness of the entries of $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\epsilon,m}$ in (E1), we obtain
\begin{align}
    S_W(h)\le C\gamma_\epsilon^{(m)}(h)\gamma_\epsilon^{(n)}(h)
    +C\mathbbm{1}\{m=n\}\kappa_\epsilon^{(m)}(h)
    \le C\rho(h).
    \label{eqn:four_idiosyncratic_error_bound}
\end{align}



It remains to bound the terms involving at least two components among $U_{t,ij}^{(m)}$, $V_{t,ij}^{(m)}$, and $W_{t,ij}^{(m)}$. These remaining terms fall into the following two cases.

{(Basic properties of $\mathcal{G}_T$ processes)}\label{lemma:prop_piecewise}
    Let $\{\mathbf{u}_t\}_{t\in[T]}\in \mathcal{G}_T(\{d_{u,1},\ldots,d_{u,m_u}\}|\{\mathbf{x}_{u,i},i\in\mathbb{Z}\})$ be a $p_u$-dimensional process, where $p_u$ and $m_u$ are bounded. Define $d_u:=\max_{1\le j\le m_u} d_{u,j}$. Then:  
    \begin{itemize}
        \item [1.] There exists a constant $C<\infty$ such that the second moments
        are uniformly bounded as
        \begin{align}
            \sup_{t\in[T]}\mathbbm{E}\|\mathbf{u}_t\|^2\le C.
            \label{eqn:uniform_second_moment_G}
        \end{align}
        \item [2.] For any $h\in\{0,\dots,T-1\}$, the centered sample autocovariance satisfies
        \begin{align}
            \left\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T-h}\big(\mathbf{u}_t\mathbf{u}_{t+h}'-\mathbbm{E}(\mathbf{u}_t\mathbf{u}_{t+h}')\big)\right\|_F^2 = O_P(R(T,d_u)).
            \label{eqn:sample_covariance_G}
        \end{align}
        \item [3.] Let $\{\mathbf{v}_t\}_{t\in[T]}\in \mathcal{G}_T(\{d_{v,1},\ldots,d_{v,m_v}\}|\{\mathbf{x}_{v,i},i\in\mathbb{Z}\})$ be another $p_v$-dimensional process, independent of $\{\mathbf{u}_t\}_{t\in[T]}$, where $p_v$ and $m_v$ are bounded. Define $d_v:=\max_{1\le j\le m_v} d_{v,j}$. For $d_{uv}:=(d_u+d_v)/2$, the sample cross-moment satisfies
        \begin{align}
            \left\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{u}_t\mathbf{v}_t'\right\|_F^2 = O_P(R(T,d_{uv})).
            \label{eqn:sample_cross_moment_G}
        \end{align}
    \end{itemize}

We first show \eqref{eqn:uniform_second_moment_G}. Fix $t\in[T]$, and suppose that $t$ belongs to segment $a\in[m_u]$ of $\{\mathbf{u}_t\}$. By the definition of $\mathcal{G}_T(\cdot)$, 
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{u}_t=\sum_{w\geq 0}\psi_{u,a}(w)\mathbf{x}_{u,t-w}, 
\end{align*}
where $\psi_{u,a}(0)=1$, and for $w\geq 1$,
\begin{align*}
    \psi_{u,a}(w)=w^{d_{u,a}-1}l_{u,a}(w),\qquad d_{u,a}<1/2.
\end{align*}
Since $l_{u,a}(w)$ converges to a finite constant and $m_u$ is bounded, 
\begin{align*}
    |\psi_{u,a}(w)|\lesssim w^{d_{u,a}-1},\qquad w\geq 1.
\end{align*}
Since the entries of the innovation sequence $\{\mathbf{x}_{u,t}\}$ are independent across coordinates and over time and have zero means and uniformly bounded second moments,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\|\mathbf{u}_t\|^2
    &= \sum_{w\ge 0}\psi_{u,a}(w)^2\mathbbm{E}\big\| \mathbf{x}_{u,t-w} \big\|^2 \\
    &\lesssim p_u\sum_{w\ge0}\psi_{u,a}(w)^2
    \lesssim p_u\left(1+\sum_{w\ge1}w^{2d_{u,a}-2}\right)
    \lesssim 1,
\end{align*}
because $d_{u,a}<1/2$, while $p_u$ and $m_u$ are bounded. Taking the supremum over $t\in[T]$ gives \eqref{eqn:uniform_second_moment_G}.

We next show \eqref{eqn:sample_covariance_G}. Fix $h\in\{0,\dots,T-1\}$, and define
\begin{align*}
    \widetilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}_u(h):=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T-h}\big(\mathbf{u}_t\mathbf{u}_{t+h}'-\mathbbm{E}(\mathbf{u}_t\mathbf{u}_{t+h}')\big).
\end{align*}
Since $p_u$ is bounded, it is enough to bound the variance of each entry. The proof proceeds in three steps. We first control the covariance between observations associated with arbitrary segment pairs. We then bound the covariance between the corresponding products. Finally, we group the pairs of time indices according to their lag.

First, fix an entry $(i,j)$. For $t,s\in[T-h]$, let
\begin{align*}
    k=J_u(t),\qquad r=J_u(t+h),\qquad
    k'=J_u(s),\qquad r'=J_u(s+h),
\end{align*}
where $J_u(t)\in[m_u]$ denotes the segment containing time $t$, and write
$\tau=t-s$. Then
\begin{align}
    \textnormal{Var}\left(\widetilde{\Gamma}_{u,ij}(h)\right)
    =T^{-2}\sum_{t=1}^{T-h}\sum_{s=1}^{T-h}
    \textnormal{Cov}\left(u_{t,i}u_{t+h,j},u_{s,i}u_{s+h,j}\right).
    \label{eqn:sample_covariance_variance_expansion}
\end{align}
Because all coordinates use the same filter within each segment and the innovation coordinates are identically distributed, the covariance between two observations from the same coordinate index depends only on their segment labels and time lag. For $k,k'\in[m_u]$ and $\tau\geq 0$, define
\begin{align}
    \gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)
    :=\sigma_u^2\sum_{w\geq 0}
    \psi_{u,k}(w+\tau)\psi_{u,k'}(w),
    \label{eqn:segment_pair_covariance}
\end{align}
where $\sigma_u^2:=\mathbbm{E}(x_{u,0,1}^2)<\infty$, and, for $\tau<0$, set $\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau):=\gamma_{k',k}(-\tau)$. By the temporal independence of the innovations, whenever $J_u(t)=k$, $J_u(s)=k'$, and $t-s=\tau$, it holds that $\textnormal{Cov}(u_{t,i},u_{s,i})=\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)$ for every $i\in[p_u]$. For each segment $a\in[m_u]$, since each $l_{u,a}(w)$ converges to a finite constant, we have
\begin{align}
    |\psi_{u,a}(w)|
    \lesssim w^{d_{u,a}-1},
    \qquad \quad w\geq1.
    \label{eqn:segment_filter_bound}
\end{align}
Moreover, by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality and the square summability of the filters,
\begin{align*}
    \max_{k,k'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{k,k'}(0)|
    \lesssim \max_{k,k'\in[m_u]}
    \left\{\sum_{w\geq0}\psi_{u,k}(w)^2\right\}^{1/2}
    \left\{\sum_{w\geq0}\psi_{u,k'}(w)^2\right\}^{1/2}
    \lesssim 1.
\end{align*}

Suppose first that $d_u>0$. For $\tau\geq1$, since $d_{u,a}\leq d_u$ for every $a\in[m_u]$,
\begin{align*}
    |\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|
    &\lesssim \tau^{d_u-1}+\sum_{w\geq1}(w+\tau)^{d_u-1}w^{d_u-1} \\
    &\lesssim \tau^{d_u-1}+\tau^{d_u-1}\sum_{w=1}^{\tau}w^{d_u-1}+\sum_{w>\tau}w^{2d_u-2}.
\end{align*}
Since $0<d_u<1/2$, integral comparison gives
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{w=1}^{\tau}w^{d_u-1}
    \leq 1+\int_1^\tau x^{d_u-1}\mathrm{d}x
    =1+\frac{\tau^{d_u}-1}{d_u}
    \lesssim \tau^{d_u},
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{w>\tau}w^{2d_u-2}
    \leq \int_\tau^\infty x^{2d_u-2}\mathrm{d}x
    \lesssim \tau^{2d_u-1}.
\end{align*}
Combining these bounds yields $|\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|\lesssim \tau^{2d_u-1}$ for $\tau\geq 1$. Hence, 
\begin{align}
    \max_{k,k'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|^2
    \lesssim (1+|\tau|)^{4d_u-2},
    \qquad \tau\in\mathbbm{Z}.
    \label{eqn:segment_covariance_long_pointwise}
\end{align}
Therefore, by the standard partial-sum bounds for $p$-series,
\begin{align}
    \sum_{\tau=-2(T-1)}^{2(T-1)}
    \max_{k,k'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|^2
    \lesssim 1+\sum_{\tau=1}^{2(T-1)}\tau^{4d_u-2}
    \lesssim
    \begin{cases}
        1, & 0<d_u<1/4,\\
        \log T, & d_u=1/4,\\
        T^{4d_u-1}, & 1/4<d_u<1/2.
    \end{cases}
    \label{eqn:segment_covariance_long_memory_bound}
\end{align}




If $d_u=0$, then \eqref{eqn:segment_filter_bound}, together with $\psi_{u,a}(0)=1$, gives
\begin{align*}
    |\psi_{u,a}(w)|
    \lesssim (1+w)^{-1},
    \qquad a\in[m_u],\quad w\geq0.
\end{align*}
Hence, for $\tau\geq 1$, splitting the sum in \eqref{eqn:segment_pair_covariance} at $w=\tau$ gives
\begin{align*}
    |\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|
    &\lesssim
    \sum_{w=0}^{\tau}\frac{1}{(1+w)(1+w+\tau)}
    +\sum_{w>\tau}\frac{1}{(1+w)(1+w+\tau)} \\
    &\lesssim
    (1+\tau)^{-1}\sum_{w=0}^{\tau}(1+w)^{-1}+\sum_{w>\tau}(1+w)^{-2} \\
    &\lesssim (1+\tau)^{-1}\log(2+\tau).
\end{align*}
Together with the bound at $\tau=0$ and the symmetry for negative lags, we have
\begin{align}
    \max_{k,k'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|
    \lesssim (1+|\tau|)^{-1}\log(2+|\tau|),
    \qquad \tau\in\mathbbm{Z},
    \label{eqn:segment_covariance_edge_pointwise}
\end{align}
so that
\begin{align}
    \sum_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}
    \max_{k,k'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|^2
    <\infty.
    \label{eqn:segment_covariance_edge_point_bound}
\end{align}

If $d_u<0$, all segment filters are absolutely summable. Extending each filter by zero on negative indices, we have
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}
    |\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|
    \lesssim \sum_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}\sum_{w\in\mathbbm{Z}}
    |\psi_{u,k}(w+\tau)||\psi_{u,k'}(w)|
    = \left\{\sum_{v\in\mathbbm{Z}}|\psi_{u,k}(v)|\right\}
    \left\{\sum_{w\in\mathbbm{Z}}|\psi_{u,k'}(w)|\right\}
    <\infty.
\end{align*}
Hence, it follows that 
\begin{align}
    \sum_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}\max_{k,k'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|<\infty, \qquad
    \sum_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}\max_{k,k'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|^2<\infty.
    \label{eqn:segment_covariance_absolute_sum}
\end{align}
Combining \eqref{eqn:segment_covariance_long_memory_bound}, \eqref{eqn:segment_covariance_edge_point_bound}, and \eqref{eqn:segment_covariance_absolute_sum}, we have
\begin{align}
    B_u(T):=\sum_{\tau=-2(T-1)}^{2(T-1)}
    \max_{k,k'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)|^2
    \lesssim
    \begin{cases}
        1, & d_u<1/4,\\
        \log T, & d_u=1/4,\\
        T^{4d_u-1}, & d_u>1/4,
    \end{cases}
    \label{eqn:segment_covariance_square_sum}
\end{align}
In particular, $T^{-1}B_u(T)=O(R(T,d_u))$.

We next bound the covariance between the products. Recall that $k=J_u(t)$, $r=J_u(t+h)$, $k'=J_u(s)$, $r'=J_u(s+h)$, and $\tau=t-s$. If $i\neq j$, independence across coordinates and the zero-mean property give
\begin{align*}
    \textnormal{Cov}(u_{t,i}u_{t+h,j},u_{s,i}u_{s+h,j})
    =\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)\gamma_{r,r'}(\tau).
\end{align*}
If $i=j$, the fourth-moment expansion gives
\begin{align}
    \textnormal{Cov}(u_{t,i}u_{t+h,i},u_{s,i}u_{s+h,i})
    &=\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)\gamma_{r,r'}(\tau)
    +\gamma_{k,r'}(\tau-h)\gamma_{r,k'}(\tau+h)
    +\mathcal{K}_{k,r,k',r'}(\tau,h),
    \label{eqn:cov_uuuu}
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{K}_{k,r,k',r'}(\tau,h)$ denotes the fourth-cumulant contribution, viz.\ after extending each filter by zero on negative indices,
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{K}_{k,r,k',r'}(\tau,h)
    :=\kappa_{4,u}
    \sum_{\ell\in\mathbbm{Z}}
    \psi_{u,k}(\ell)
    \psi_{u,r}(\ell+h)
    \psi_{u,k'}(\ell-\tau)
    \psi_{u,r'}(\ell+h-\tau),
\end{align*}
where $\kappa_{4,u}:=\mathbbm{E}(x_{u,0,1}^4)-3\{\mathbbm{E}(x_{u,0,1}^2)\}^2$ is finite by the bounded fourth moment assumption. Define
\begin{align*}
    a_q:=\psi_{u,k}(q)\psi_{u,r}(q+h),
    \qquad
    b_q:=\psi_{u,k'}(q)\psi_{u,r'}(q+h).
\end{align*}
Then, $|\mathcal{K}_{k,r,k',r'}(\tau,h)| \lesssim \sum_{q\in\mathbbm{Z}}|a_q|\,|b_{q-\tau}|$. Summing over $\tau$ and applying the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality gives
\begin{align*}
    &\;\quad
    \sum_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}|\mathcal{K}_{k,r,k',r'}(\tau,h)|
    \lesssim \left(\sum_{q\in\mathbbm{Z}}|a_q|\right)
    \left(\sum_{q\in\mathbbm{Z}}|b_q|\right) \\
    &\lesssim
    \left\{\sum_{q\in\mathbbm{Z}}|\psi_{u,k}(q)|^2\right\}^{1/2}
    \left\{\sum_{q\in\mathbbm{Z}}|\psi_{u,r}(q+h)|^2\right\}^{1/2} 
    \left\{\sum_{q\in\mathbbm{Z}}|\psi_{u,k'}(q)|^2\right\}^{1/2}
    \left\{\sum_{q\in\mathbbm{Z}}|\psi_{u,r'}(q+h)|^2\right\}^{1/2} 
    \lesssim 1.
\end{align*}
Therefore, we have
\begin{align}
    \sum_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}\max_{k,r,k',r'\in[m_u]}
    |\mathcal{K}_{k,r,k',r'}(\tau,h)|
    \lesssim 1.
    \label{eqn:cumulant_sum_G}
\end{align}
For the two covariance-product terms in \eqref{eqn:cov_uuuu}, we have
\begin{align}
    \sum_{\tau=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\max_{k,r,k',r'\in[m_u]}
    |\gamma_{k,k'}(\tau)\gamma_{r,r'}(\tau)|
    \leq \sum_{\tau=-(T-1)}^{T-1}
    \left\{\max_{a,b\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{a,b}(\tau)|\right\}^2
    \leq B_u(T),
    \label{eqn:first_covariance_product_G}
\end{align}
and, by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality,
\begin{align}
    &\quad\;\sum_{\tau=-(T-1)}^{T-1}
    \max_{k,r,k',r'\in[m_u]}
    |\gamma_{k,r'}(\tau-h)\gamma_{r,k'}(\tau+h)| \nonumber \\
    &\leq
    \left\{\sum_{\tau=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\max_{k,r'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{k,r'}(\tau-h)|^2\right\}^{1/2}
    \left\{\sum_{\tau=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\max_{r,k'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{r,k'}(\tau+h)|^2\right\}^{1/2}  
    \leq B_u(T),
    \label{eqn:shifted_covariance_product_G}
\end{align}
where the last inequality follows because both shifted ranges $\{\tau-h:|\tau|\leq T-1\}$ and $\{\tau+h:|\tau|\leq T-1\}$ are contained in $\{-2(T-1),\dots,2(T-1)\}$.
For fixed $i,j$ and $h$, define
\begin{align*}
    D_{u,ij,h}(\tau)
    &:=\max_{\substack{t,s\in[T-h]\\t-s=\tau}}
    \left|\textnormal{Cov}\left(u_{t,i}u_{t+h,j},u_{s,i}u_{s+h,j}\right)\right|,
\end{align*}
where the maximum over an empty set is taken to be zero by convention. Combining \eqref{eqn:cumulant_sum_G}, \eqref{eqn:first_covariance_product_G}, and
\eqref{eqn:shifted_covariance_product_G}, we obtain
\begin{align}
    \sum_{\tau=-(T-1)}^{T-1}
    D_{u,ij,h}(\tau)
    \lesssim B_u(T)+1.
    \label{eqn:product_covariance_sum_G}
\end{align}
For each $\tau$, the number of pairs $(t,s)\in[T-h]^2$ satisfying $t-s=\tau$ is $\max\{T-h-|\tau|,0\}$. Therefore, by \eqref{eqn:sample_covariance_variance_expansion},
\begin{align*}
    \textnormal{Var}\left(\widetilde{\Gamma}_{u,ij}(h)\right)
    &\leq T^{-2}\sum_{\tau=-(T-1)}^{T-1}
    \max\{T-h-|\tau|,0\}D_{u,ij,h}(\tau) \\
    &\leq T^{-1}\sum_{\tau=-(T-1)}^{T-1}D_{u,ij,h}(\tau) \\
    &\lesssim T^{-1}(B_u(T)+1).
\end{align*}
Since $T^{-1}B_u(T)=O(R(T,d_u))$ and $T^{-1}=O(R(T,d_u))$, it follows that
\begin{align*}
    \textnormal{Var}\left(\widetilde{\Gamma}_{u,ij}(h)\right)
    =O(R(T,d_u)).
\end{align*}
Since each entry of $\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}_u(h)$ is centered and $p_u$ is bounded,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\big\| \widetilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}_u(h) \big\|_F^2
    =\sum_{i=1}^{p_u}\sum_{j=1}^{p_u}\mathbbm{E}\left(\widetilde{\Gamma}_{u,ij}(h)^2\right)
    =\sum_{i=1}^{p_u}\sum_{j=1}^{p_u}\textnormal{Var}\left(\widetilde{\Gamma}_{u,ij}(h)\right)
    =O(R(T,d_u)).
\end{align*}
Markov's inequality then gives
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widetilde{\boldsymbol{\Gamma}}_u(h) \big\|_F^2=O_P(R(T,d_u)),
\end{align*}
which proves \eqref{eqn:sample_covariance_G}.


To prove \eqref{eqn:sample_cross_moment_G}, define
\begin{align*}
    \widetilde{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{uv}:=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{u}_t\mathbf{v}_t'.
\end{align*}
Fix an entry $(i,j)\in[p_u]\times[p_v]$. Since the two processes $\{\mathbf{u}_t\}$ and $\{\mathbf{v}_t\}$ are independent and have mean zero, we have $\mathbbm{E}(u_{t,i}v_{t,j})=0$ for every $t\in[T]$. Hence,
\begin{align}
    \textnormal{Var}\left(\widetilde{\Sigma}_{uv,ij}\right)
    =T^{-2}\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{s=1}^T
    \textnormal{Cov}(u_{t,i}v_{t,j},u_{s,i}v_{s,j})
    =T^{-2}\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{s=1}^T
    \textnormal{Cov}(u_{t,i},u_{s,i})\textnormal{Cov}(v_{t,j},v_{s,j}),
    \label{eqn:sample_cross_moment_variance}
\end{align}
where the second equality follows from the independence of $\{\mathbf{u}_t\}$ and $\{\mathbf{v}_t\}$.

Let $J_u(t)$ and $J_v(t)$ denote the segment indices of $\mathbf{u}_t$ and $\mathbf{v}_t$, respectively. For $a,a'\in[m_u]$ and $\tau\geq0$, define
\begin{align*}
    \gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)
    &:=\sigma_u^2\sum_{w\geq0}\psi_{u,a}(w+\tau)\psi_{u,a'}(w),
\end{align*}
where $\sigma_u^2:=\mathbbm{E}(x_{u,0,1}^2)<\infty$, and set $\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau):=\gamma_{u,a',a}(-\tau)$ when $\tau<0$. Define $\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)$ analogously for $b,b'\in[m_v]$. By the temporal independence of the innovations, whenever $J_u(t)=a$, $J_u(s)=a'$, and $t-s=\tau$, we have $\textnormal{Cov}(u_{t,i},u_{s,i})=\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)$, and the analogous equality holds for $\{\mathbf{v}_t\}$. In particular,
\begin{align*}
    \max_{\substack{a,a'\in[m_u]\\ b,b'\in[m_v]}}
    \left|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)\right|
    =\left\{\max_{a,a'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)|\right\}
    \left\{\max_{b,b'\in[m_v]}|\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)|\right\}.
\end{align*}

We first consider the case $d_u>0$ and $d_v>0$. Taking square roots in \eqref{eqn:segment_covariance_long_pointwise}, applied separately to $\{\mathbf{u}_t\}$ and $\{\mathbf{v}_t\}$, gives
\begin{align}
    \max_{\substack{a,a'\in[m_u]\\ b,b'\in[m_v]}}
    \left|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)\right|
    \lesssim (1+|\tau|)^{2d_u-1}(1+|\tau|)^{2d_v-1}
    = (1+|\tau|)^{4d_{uv}-2},
    \label{eqn:cross_covariance_decay}
\end{align}
where $d_{uv}:=(d_u+d_v)/2$. 

Next, consider the case where $d_u\leq 0$ or $d_v\leq 0$. We divide it into two disjoint subcases. First, suppose that $\min\{d_u,d_v\}<0$. Without loss of generality, let $d_u<0$. The argument in \eqref{eqn:segment_covariance_absolute_sum} gives 
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}\max_{a,a'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)|<\infty.
\end{align*}
Moreover, by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality and \eqref{eqn:uniform_second_moment_G},
\begin{align*}
    \sup_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}\max_{b,b'\in[m_v]}|\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)|
    \leq \sigma_v^2\max_{b,b'\in[m_v]}
    \left\{\sum_{w\geq 0}\psi_{v,b}(w)^2\right\}^{1/2}
    \left\{\sum_{w\geq 0}\psi_{v,b'}(w)^2\right\}^{1/2}
    \lesssim 1,
\end{align*}
where $\sigma_v^2:=\mathbbm{E}(x_{v,0,1}^2)$. It follows that
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}\max_{\substack{a,a'\in[m_u]\\ b,b'\in[m_v]}}
    \left|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)\right|
    <\infty.
\end{align*}
It remains to consider the case $\min\{d_u,d_v\}=0$. Without loss of generality, let $d_u=0$ and $d_v\geq 0$. The bound in \eqref{eqn:segment_covariance_edge_pointwise} gives
\begin{align*}
    \max_{a,a'\in[m_u]}|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)|
    \lesssim (1+|\tau|)^{-1}\log(2+|\tau|).
\end{align*}
If $d_v>0$, then
\begin{align*}
    \max_{\substack{a,a'\in[m_u]\\ b,b'\in[m_v]}}
    \left|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)\right|
    \lesssim (1+|\tau|)^{2d_v-2}\log(2+|\tau|),
\end{align*}
which is summable because $2d_v-2<-1$. If $d_v=0$, the same bound in \eqref{eqn:segment_covariance_edge_pointwise} gives
\begin{align*}
    \max_{\substack{a,a'\in[m_u]\\ b,b'\in[m_v]}}
    \left|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)\right|
    \lesssim (1+|\tau|)^{-2}\log^2(2+|\tau|),
\end{align*}
which is also summable. Hence, whenever $d_u\leq 0$ or $d_v\leq 0$, we have
\begin{align}
    \sum_{\tau\in\mathbbm{Z}}\max_{\substack{a,a'\in[m_u]\\ b,b'\in[m_v]}}
    \left|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)\right|
    <\infty.
    \label{eqn:cross_covariance_short_sum}
\end{align}
In this case, since $d_u\leq 0$ or $d_v\leq 0$, while both $d_u,d_v<1/2$, we have $d_{uv}<1/4$.

Define
\begin{align*}
    B_{uv}(T)
    &:=\sum_{\tau=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\max_{\substack{a,a'\in[m_u]\\ b,b'\in[m_v]}}
    \left|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)\right|.
\end{align*}
Combining the two cases \eqref{eqn:cross_covariance_decay} and \eqref{eqn:cross_covariance_short_sum}, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    B_{uv}(T)\lesssim
    \begin{cases}
        1, & d_{uv}<1/4,\\
        \log T, & d_{uv}=1/4,\\
        T^{4d_{uv}-1}, & d_{uv}>1/4.
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}
Consequently, $T^{-1}B_{uv}(T)=O(R(T,d_{uv}))$. For each $\tau\in\{-(T-1),\ldots,T-1\}$, the number of pairs $(t,s)\in[T]^2$ satisfying $t-s=\tau$ is $T-|\tau|$. Therefore, grouping the pairs according to their lag gives
\begin{align*}
    \textnormal{Var}\left(\widetilde{\Sigma}_{uv,ij}\right)
    &\le T^{-2}\sum_{\tau=-(T-1)}^{T-1}(T-|\tau|)
    \max_{\substack{a,a'\in[m_u]\\ b,b'\in[m_v]}}
    \left|\gamma_{u,a,a'}(\tau)\gamma_{v,b,b'}(\tau)\right| \\
    &\le T^{-1}B_{uv}(T)=O(R(T,d_{uv})).
\end{align*}
Since $p_u$ and $p_v$ are bounded and $\mathbbm{E}(\widetilde{\Sigma}_{uv,ij})=0$,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\big\| \widetilde{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{uv} \big\|_F^2
    =\sum_{i=1}^{p_u}\sum_{j=1}^{p_v}\textnormal{Var}\left(\widetilde{\Sigma}_{uv,ij}\right)
    =O(R(T,d_{uv})).
\end{align*}
Markov's inequality then gives
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widetilde{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_{uv} \big\|_F^2=O_P(R(T,d_{uv})),
\end{align*}
which proves \eqref{eqn:sample_cross_moment_G} and completes the proof. $\square$

(Auxiliary bounds for error interactions)
\label{lemma:bound_Delta}
    Let Assumptions~(E1) and (E2) hold. Let $\{\mathbf{u}_t\}_{t\in[T]}\in\mathcal{G}_T(\{d_{u,1},\ldots,d_{u,m_u}\}|\{\mathbf{x}_{u,i},i\in\mathbbm{Z}\})$ be a $p_u$-dimensional piecewise-stationary time series, where $p_u$ and $m_u$ are bounded, and assume that $\{\mathbf{u}_t\}_{t\in[T]}$ is independent of $\{\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}:t\in[T],\,m\in[M]\}$. Let $d_u:=\max_{1\le j\le m_u}d_{u,j}$. For each $m,n\in[M]$ with $m\neq n$, the following bounds hold:
    \begin{align}
        \big\| T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{E}_{t}^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\mathbf{u}_t' \big\|_F^2 
        &= O_P(T^{-1}p_mq_m), \label{eqn:E1u} \\
        \big\| T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'} \big\|_F^2  
        &= O_P(T^{-1}p_mp_nq_mq_n), \label{eqn:Em11En} \\
        \big\| T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'} \big\|_F^2
        &= O_P(p_mq_m^2+T^{-1}p_m^2q_m^2). \label{eqn:Em11Em}
	\end{align}
    Moreover, for any deterministic vector $\mathbf{w}=(w_1,\dots,w_T)'$ and for each $m\in[M]$,
    \begin{align}
        \Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T w_t\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\Big\|^2
        =O_P\big(T^{-2}p_mq_m\|\mathbf{w}\|^2\big). \label{eqn:weighted_row_error}
    \end{align}

We first prove \eqref{eqn:E1u}. 
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E} \bigg\| T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{E}_{t}^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\mathbf{u}_t' \bigg\|_F^2 
    &= T^{-2}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\sum_{s=1}^T 
    \mathbbm{E}\left(\text{tr}(\mathbf{E}_{t}^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\mathbf{u}_t'\mathbf{u}_s\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_{s}^{(m)'})\right) \\
    &= T^{-2}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\sum_{s=1}^T 
    \mathbbm{E}(\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_{s}^{(m)'}\mathbf{E}_{t}^{(m)}\1_{q_m})
    \mathbbm{E}(\mathbf{u}_t'\mathbf{u}_s).
\end{align*}
The second equality follows because the whole process $\{\mathbf{u}_t\}_{t\in[T]}$ is independent of $\{\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\}_{t\in[T]}$. Define the admissible index set $I_T(h):=\{t\in[T]: t+h\in[T]\}$. Re-indexing by lag $h:= s-t$ gives
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E} \bigg\| T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{E}_{t}^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\mathbf{u}_t' \bigg\|_F^2 
    &= T^{-2} \sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1} \sum_{t\in I_T(h)}
    \mathbbm{E}\big(\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_{t+h}^{(m)'}\mathbf{E}_{t}^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\big)
    \mathbbm{E}(\mathbf{u}_{t}'\mathbf{u}_{t+h}).
\end{align*}
We bound the two expectations on the r.h.s.\ separately. First, expand
\begin{align*}
    \big|\mathbbm{E}(\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_{t+h}^{(m)'}\mathbf{E}_{t}^{(m)}\1_{q_m})\big|
    = \Big|\mathbbm{E}\big(\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,il}^{(m)}\big)\Big|
    \le \sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}\big|\mathbbm{E}(E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,il}^{(m)})\big|.
\end{align*}
By Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm}, for each fixed $(i,j)$,
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{l=1}^{q_m}\big| \mathbbm{E}(E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,il}^{(m)}) \big|
    \le \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}\big| \mathbbm{E}(E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,kl}^{(m)}) \big|
    \le C\rho(h),
\end{align*}
uniformly over $t,h,m,i,j$. Summing over $i$ and $j$, we obtain
\begin{align}
    \big|\mathbbm{E}(\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_{t+h}^{(m)'}\mathbf{E}_{t}^{(m)}\1_{q_m})\big|
    \le Cp_mq_m\rho(h).
    \label{eqn:E_inner_rho_bound}
\end{align}
Moreover, by \eqref{eqn:uniform_second_moment_G} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:prop_piecewise} and the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality,
\begin{align}
    \sup_{t\in I_T(h)}|\mathbbm{E}(\mathbf{u}_t'\mathbf{u}_{t+h})|
    \le \sup_{t,s\in[T]}\left(\mathbbm{E}\|\mathbf{u}_t\|^2\right)^{1/2}\left(\mathbbm{E}\|\mathbf{u}_s\|^2\right)^{1/2}
    \le C.
    \label{eqn:u_cov_uniform_bound}
\end{align}
Combining \eqref{eqn:E_inner_rho_bound} and \eqref{eqn:u_cov_uniform_bound},
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E} \bigg\| T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{E}_{t}^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\mathbf{u}_t' \bigg\|_F^2 
    &\le CT^{-2}p_mq_m\sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\sum_{t\in I_T(h)}\rho(h) \\
    &\le CT^{-1}p_mq_m\sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(h) \\
    &=O(T^{-1}p_mq_m),
\end{align*}
and Markov's inequality proves \eqref{eqn:E1u}.

We next prove \eqref{eqn:Em11En}. First, for each group $m\in[M]$, define $\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}:=q_m^{-1}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}$. Then
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\bigg\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T 
    \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\bigg\|_F^2 
    = q_m^2q_n^2\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{k=1}^{p_n}
    \mathbbm{E}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\zeta_{t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{t,k}^{(n)}\right)^2 .
\end{align*}
We first show that, for $m\ne n$ and $t,s\in[T]$,
\begin{align}
    \mathbbm{E}\left(\zeta_{t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{s,k}^{(n)}\right)=0.
    \label{eqn:zeta_cross_zero}
\end{align}
Write $\zeta_{t,i}^{(m)}=\zeta_{G,t,i}^{(m)}+\zeta_{F,t,i}^{(m)}+\zeta_{\epsilon,t,i}^{(m)}$, where the three terms are the corresponding row-averaged components induced respectively by
$\mathbf{Q}_{e,m}\mathbf{G}_{e,t}\mathbf{J}_{e,m}'$, $\mathbf{R}_{e,m}\mathbf{F}_{e,t}^{(m)}\mathbf{C}_{e,m}'$, and
$\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\epsilon,m}\circ\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t^{(m)}$ in (E1). By the mean-zero assumptions and the mutual independence of the global error, local error, and idiosyncratic error innovation processes in (E1) and (E2), all cross terms vanish, and hence
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\left(\zeta_{t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{s,k}^{(n)}\right)
    =\mathbbm{E}\left(\zeta_{G,t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{G,s,k}^{(n)}\right)
    +\mathbbm{E}\left(\zeta_{F,t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{F,s,k}^{(n)}\right)
    +\mathbbm{E}\left(\zeta_{\epsilon,t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{\epsilon,s,k}^{(n)}\right).
\end{align*}
The last two terms are zero by the cross-group independence of the group-specific local error processes and the idiosyncratic error processes in (E2). It remains to consider the global-global term. By (E2),
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\left((\mathbf{G}_{e,t})_{ab}(\mathbf{G}_{e,s})_{cd}\right)
    =\mathbbm{1}\{a=c,b=d\}
    \sum_{u,v\ge0}a_{e,u}a_{e,v}\mathbbm{1}\{t-u=s-v\}.
\end{align*}
Therefore,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\{\zeta_{G,t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{G,s,k}^{(n)}\}
    &=(q_mq_n)^{-1}
    \sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_n}
    \mathbbm{E}\left((\mathbf{Q}_{e,m}\mathbf{G}_{e,t}\mathbf{J}_{e,m}')_{ij}
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,n}\mathbf{G}_{e,s}\mathbf{J}_{e,n}')_{kl}\right) \\
    &=(q_mq_n)^{-1}
    \left(\sum_{u,v\ge0}a_{e,u}a_{e,v}\mathbbm{1}\{t-u=s-v\}\right)
    (\mathbf{Q}_{e,m}\mathbf{Q}_{e,n}')_{ik}
    \big(\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{J}_{e,m}\mathbf{J}_{e,n}'\1_{q_n}\big) \\
    &=0,
\end{align*}
where the last equality follows from the cross-group orthogonality $\mathbf{Q}_{e,m}\mathbf{Q}_{e,n}'=\0$ or $\mathbf{J}_{e,m}\mathbf{J}_{e,n}'=\0$ in (E1). This proves \eqref{eqn:zeta_cross_zero}.

By \eqref{eqn:zeta_cross_zero}, for each fixed $i\in[p_m]$ and $k\in[p_n]$,
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\mathbbm{E}\left(
    T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \zeta_{t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{t,k}^{(n)}\right)^2
    =T^{-2}\sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\sum_{t\in I_T(h)}
    \textnormal{Cov}\left(
    \zeta_{t+h,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{t+h,k}^{(n)},
    \zeta_{t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{t,k}^{(n)}\right)  \\
    &\le T^{-2}q_m^{-2}q_n^{-2}
    \sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\sum_{t\in I_T(h)}
    \sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_n}
    \sum_{a=1}^{q_m}\sum_{b=1}^{q_n}
    \left|\textnormal{Cov}\left(
    E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t+h,kl}^{(n)},
    E_{t,ia}^{(m)}E_{t,kb}^{(n)}
    \right)\right|.
\end{align*}
By \eqref{eqn:error_product_cov} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm},
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{a=1}^{q_m}\sum_{b=1}^{q_n}
    \left|\textnormal{Cov}\left(
    E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t+h,kl}^{(n)},
    E_{t,ia}^{(m)}E_{t,kb}^{(n)}
    \right)\right|
    \le C\rho(h),
\end{align*}
uniformly over all admissible indices. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\left(
    T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \zeta_{t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{t,k}^{(n)}\right)^2
    &\le CT^{-2}q_m^{-2}q_n^{-2}
    \sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\sum_{t\in I_T(h)}
    \sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_n}\rho(h) \\
    &\le CT^{-1}q_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}
    \sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(h) \\
    &=O(T^{-1}q_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}).
\end{align*}
Consequently,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\bigg\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T 
    \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\bigg\|_F^2 
    &\le Cq_m^2q_n^2
    \sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{k=1}^{p_n}T^{-1}q_m^{-1}q_n^{-1} \\
    &=O(T^{-1}p_mp_nq_mq_n).
\end{align*}
Markov's inequality proves \eqref{eqn:Em11En}.

We next prove \eqref{eqn:Em11Em}. Using the same notation $\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}=q_m^{-1}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}$, we have
\begin{align*}
    T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'}
    =T^{-1}q_m^2\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}.
\end{align*}
Hence,
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\mathbbm{E}\Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'}\Big\|_F^2  \\
    &= q_m^4\mathbbm{E}\Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}\Big\|_F^2  \\
    &\le 2q_m^4
    \Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \mathbbm{E}\left(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}\right)\Big\|_F^2
    +2q_m^4\mathbbm{E}\Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \Big[\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}-\mathbbm{E}\left(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}\right)\Big]\Big\|_F^2 .
\end{align*}
We first bound the deterministic part. Let $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\zeta,t}^{(m)}:=\mathbbm{E}\left(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}\right)$. For each fixed $t\in[T]$ and $i\in[p_m]$, Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm} with $h=0$ gives
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}
    \big|(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\zeta,t}^{(m)})_{ik}\big|
    &=q_m^{-2}\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}
    \left|\sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
    \mathbbm{E}(E_{t,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,kl}^{(m)})\right| \\
    &\le q_m^{-2}
    \sum_{j=1}^{q_m}
    \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
    \big|\mathbbm{E}(E_{t,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,kl}^{(m)})\big| \\
    &\le Cq_m^{-1}.
\end{align*}
Therefore,
\begin{align*}
    \big\|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\zeta,t}^{(m)}\big\|_F^2
    =\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\big|(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\zeta,t}^{(m)})_{ik}\big|^2
    \le\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\left\{\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}
    \big|(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\zeta,t}^{(m)})_{ik}\big|\right\}^2
    =O(p_mq_m^{-2}),
\end{align*}
uniformly over $t\in[T]$. By Jensen's inequality,
\begin{align*}
    \Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \mathbbm{E}\left(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}\right)\Big\|_F^2
    \le T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \big\|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\zeta,t}^{(m)}\big\|_F^2
    =O(p_mq_m^{-2}).
\end{align*}

We next bound the centered stochastic part. Write
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\mathbbm{E}\Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \Big[\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}
    -\mathbbm{E}\left(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}\right)
    \Big]\Big\|_F^2  \\
    &=T^{-2}\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}
    \sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\sum_{t\in I_T(h)}
    \textnormal{Cov}\left(
    \zeta_{t+h,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{t+h,k}^{(m)},
    \zeta_{t,i}^{(m)}\zeta_{t,k}^{(m)}
    \right).
\end{align*}
Taking absolute values and using $\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}=q_m^{-1}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}$, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\mathbbm{E}\Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \Big[\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}
    -\mathbbm{E}\left(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}\right)
    \Big]\Big\|_F^2  \\
    &\le T^{-2}q_m^{-4}
    \sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}
    \sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\sum_{t\in I_T(h)}
    \sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
    \sum_{a=1}^{q_m}\sum_{b=1}^{q_m}
    \left|\textnormal{Cov}\left(
    E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t+h,kl}^{(m)},
    E_{t,ia}^{(m)}E_{t,kb}^{(m)}
    \right)\right|.
\end{align*}
By \eqref{eqn:error_product_cov} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm}, with the two group indices both equal to $m$,
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{a=1}^{q_m}\sum_{b=1}^{q_m}
    \left|\textnormal{Cov}\left(
    E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t+h,kl}^{(m)},
    E_{t,ia}^{(m)}E_{t,kb}^{(m)}
    \right)\right|
    \le C\rho(h),
\end{align*}
uniformly over all admissible indices. Hence,
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\mathbbm{E}\Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \Big[\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}
    -\mathbbm{E}\left(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}\right)
    \Big]\Big\|_F^2  \\
    &\le CT^{-2}q_m^{-4}
    \sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}
    \sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\sum_{t\in I_T(h)}
    \sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
    \rho(h) \\
    &\le CT^{-1}p_m^2q_m^{-2}
    \sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(h) \\
    &=O(T^{-1}p_m^2q_m^{-2}).
\end{align*}
Combining the deterministic and centered stochastic parts, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'}\Big\|_F^2
    =O(p_mq_m^2+T^{-1}p_m^2q_m^2).
\end{align*}
Markov's inequality proves \eqref{eqn:Em11Em}.

To prove \eqref{eqn:weighted_row_error}, retain the notation $\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}=q_m^{-1}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}$. Then, 
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    w_t\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\Big\|^2
    &=T^{-2}q_m^2\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{s=1}^T w_tw_s
    \mathbbm{E}\left(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_s^{(m)}\right) \\
    &\le T^{-2}q_m^2\sum_{t=1}^T\sum_{s=1}^T
    |w_tw_s|\left|\mathbbm{E}\left(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_s^{(m)}\right)\right|.
\end{align*}
For any $h\in\mathbbm{Z}$ and $t\in I_T(h)$, write
\begin{align*}
    \left|\mathbbm{E}\left(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_{t+h}^{(m)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\boldsymbol{\zeta}_t^{(m)}\right)\right|
    &=q_m^{-2}\left|\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{k=1}^{p_m}
    (\mathbf{M}_{p_m})_{ik}\sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
    \mathbbm{E}\left(E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,kl}^{(m)}\right)\right| \\
    &\le q_m^{-2}\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{j=1}^{q_m}
    \sum_{k=1}^{p_m}\sum_{l=1}^{q_m}
    \left|\mathbbm{E}\left(E_{t+h,ij}^{(m)}E_{t,kl}^{(m)}\right)\right| \\
    &\le Cq_m^{-2}\sum_{i=1}^{p_m}\sum_{j=1}^{q_m}\rho(h) \\
    &=Cp_mq_m^{-1}\rho(h),
\end{align*}
where the first inequality uses $|(\mathbf{M}_{p_m})_{ik}|\le 1$, and the last inequality follows from \eqref{eqn:error_cov_summable} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm}. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    w_t\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\Big\|^2
    \le CT^{-2}p_mq_m
    \sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\sum_{t\in I_T(h)}|w_tw_{t+h}|\rho(h).
\end{align*}
Recall the $p=1,q=2,r=2$ case of the discrete Young inequality: if $\sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}|a_h|<\infty$ and $\sum_{t\in\mathbbm{Z}}b_t^2<\infty$, then
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{s\in\mathbbm{Z}}
    \left(\sum_{t\in\mathbbm{Z}}|a_{s-t}||b_t|\right)^2
    \le\left(\sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}|a_h|\right)^2
    \sum_{t\in\mathbbm{Z}}b_t^2 .
\end{align*}
Extending $w_t$ by zero outside $[T]$, and taking $a_h=\rho(h)$ and $b_t=w_t$ in the preceding inequality, we have
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;\sum_{h=-(T-1)}^{T-1}\sum_{t\in I_T(h)}
    |w_tw_{t+h}|\rho(h)
    =\sum_{s\in\mathbbm{Z}}|w_s|\sum_{t\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(s-t)|w_t| \\
    &\le\left(\sum_{s\in\mathbbm{Z}}w_s^2\right)^{1/2}
    \left\{\sum_{s\in\mathbbm{Z}}
    \left(\sum_{t\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(s-t)|w_t|\right)^2
    \right\}^{1/2} 
    \le\left(\sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(h)\right)\sum_{t\in\mathbbm{Z}}w_t^2 
    \le C\|\mathbf{w}\|^2,
\end{align*}
where the second inequality follows from the discrete Young inequality, and the last inequality follows from $\sum_{h\in\mathbbm{Z}}\rho(h)<\infty$ in Lemma~\ref{lemma:correlation_Etm}. Consequently,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbbm{E}\Big\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    w_t\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\Big\|^2
    \le CT^{-2}p_mq_m\|\mathbf{w}\|^2.
\end{align*}
Markov's inequality proves \eqref{eqn:weighted_row_error}. This completes the proof. $\square$

(Asymptotic orthogonality of projected rotation)
\label{lemma:rotation_orthogonality}
    Let $\mathbf{U},\widehat{\mathbf{U}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}$ satisfy $\mathbf{U}'\mathbf{U}=\mathbf{I}_r$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{U}}'\widehat{\mathbf{U}}=\mathbf{I}_r$. Define $\mathbf{P}_U:=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{U}'$, $\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_U:=\widehat{\mathbf{U}}\widehat{\mathbf{U}}'$, and $\mathbf{H}_U:=\mathbf{U}'\widehat{\mathbf{U}}$. Then, 
    \begin{align}
        \widehat{\mathbf{U}}-\mathbf{U}\mathbf{H}_U=(\mathbf{I}_d-\mathbf{P}_U)\widehat{\mathbf{U}}.
    \end{align}
    Moreover, if $\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{U}}-\mathbf{U}\mathbf{H}_U \big\|_F=o_P(1)$, then
    \begin{align}
        \mathbf{H}_U'\mathbf{H}_U=\mathbf{I}_r+o_P(1),
        \qquad \mathbf{H}_U\mathbf{H}_U'=\mathbf{I}_r+o_P(1),
    \end{align}
    and $\mathbf{H}_U$ is invertible with probability tending to one. In addition,
    \begin{align}
        \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_U-\mathbf{P}_U \big\|_F^2
        = 2\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{U}}-\mathbf{U}\mathbf{H}_U \big\|_F^2.
    \end{align}

By the definition of $\mathbf{H}_U$, $\mathbf{U}\mathbf{H}_U=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{U}'\widehat{\mathbf{U}}=\mathbf{P}_U\widehat{\mathbf{U}}$. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
    \widehat{\mathbf{U}}-\mathbf{U}\mathbf{H}_U
    =\widehat{\mathbf{U}}-\mathbf{P}_U\widehat{\mathbf{U}}
    =(\mathbf{I}_d-\mathbf{P}_U)\widehat{\mathbf{U}}.
\end{align*}
Let $\mathbf{E}_U:=\widehat{\mathbf{U}}-\mathbf{U}\mathbf{H}_U$. Since $\mathbf{U}'\mathbf{E}_U=0$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{I}_r
    =\widehat{\mathbf{U}}'\widehat{\mathbf{U}}
    =(\mathbf{U}\mathbf{H}_U+\mathbf{E}_U)'(\mathbf{U}\mathbf{H}_U+\mathbf{E}_U)
    =\mathbf{H}_U'\mathbf{H}_U+\mathbf{E}_U'\mathbf{E}_U.
\end{align*}
Hence
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \mathbf{H}_U'\mathbf{H}_U-\mathbf{I}_r \big\|
    =\big\| \mathbf{E}_U \big\|^2
    \le\big\| \mathbf{E}_U \big\|_F^2
    =o_P(1).
\end{align*}
Since $\mathbf{H}_U$ is square, $\mathbf{H}_U'\mathbf{H}_U$ and $\mathbf{H}_U\mathbf{H}_U'$ have the same eigenvalues, given by the squared singular values of $\mathbf{H}_U$. Hence
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \mathbf{H}_U\mathbf{H}_U'-\mathbf{I}_r \big\|
    =\max_{1\le j\le r}|\sigma_j^2(\mathbf{H}_U)-1|
    =\big\| \mathbf{H}_U'\mathbf{H}_U-\mathbf{I}_r \big\|
    =o_P(1).
\end{align*}
Also, since $\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{H}_U'\mathbf{H}_U)=1+o_P(1)$, $\mathbf{H}_U$ is invertible with probability tending to one.

Finally, write
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_U-\mathbf{P}_U \big\|_F^2
    &=\text{tr}(\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_U)+\text{tr}(\mathbf{P}_U)
    -2\text{tr}(\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_U\mathbf{P}_U)  \\
    &=2r-2\text{tr}(\widehat{\mathbf{U}}'\mathbf{U}\mathbf{U}'\widehat{\mathbf{U}})  \\
    &=2r-2\text{tr}(\mathbf{H}_U'\mathbf{H}_U).
\end{align*}
On the other hand, from 
$\mathbf{I}_r=\mathbf{H}_U'\mathbf{H}_U+\mathbf{E}_U'\mathbf{E}_U$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \mathbf{E}_U \big\|_F^2
    =\text{tr}(\mathbf{E}_U'\mathbf{E}_U)
    =r-\text{tr}(\mathbf{H}_U'\mathbf{H}_U).
\end{align*}
Therefore,
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_U-\mathbf{P}_U \big\|_F^2
    =2\big\| \mathbf{E}_U \big\|_F^2
    =2\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{U}}-\mathbf{U}\mathbf{H}_U \big\|_F^2.
\end{align*}
This completes the proof.


\begin{lemma}(Bounds for $\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}$ and $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}$)\label{lemma:bound_S_A1_Delta_A1}
    For any $m,n\in[M]$ with $m\neq n$, let $\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_1,mn}$ be defined in \eqref{eqn:S_A1} and \eqref{eqn:S_A1_hat}, respectively, and define $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}:=\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_1,mn}-\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}$. Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m},
    \begin{align}
        \big\| \mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2
        &= O(p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}), \label{eqn:S_A1_bound} \\
        \big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2
        &= o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}). \label{eqn:Delta_A1_oP}
    \end{align}
    Furthermore, defining $\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}:=\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'$, we have
    \begin{align}
        \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2
        = O_P\Big(R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}
        +T^{-1}p_mp_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}}q_m^{-1}\Big).
        \label{eqn:Delta_A1_projected_bound}
    \end{align}
\end{lemma}

(Bounds for $\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}$ and $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}$)\label{lemma:bound_S_A1_Delta_A1}
    For any $m,n\in[M]$ with $m\neq n$, let $\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_1,mn}$ be defined in \eqref{eqn:S_A1} and \eqref{eqn:S_A1_hat}, respectively, and define $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn}:=\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_1,mn}-\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}$. Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m},
    \begin{align}
        \big\| \mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2
        &= O(p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}), \label{eqn:S_A1_bound} \\
        \big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2
        &= o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}). \label{eqn:Delta_A1_oP}
    \end{align}
    Furthermore, defining $\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}:=\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{1m}'$, we have
    \begin{align}
        \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2
        = O_P\Big(R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}
        +T^{-1}p_mp_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}}q_m^{-1}\Big).
        \label{eqn:Delta_A1_projected_bound}
    \end{align}

Recall from \eqref{eqn:S_A1} and \eqref{eqn:S_A1_hat} that
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}
    &:=T^{-1}q_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}
    \sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}\left(\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{Y}_t^{(n)'}\right)\mathbf{M}_{p_n}, \\
    \widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_1,mn}
    &:=T^{-1}q_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}
    \sum_{t=1}^T\left(\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{Y}_t^{(n)'}\right)\mathbf{M}_{p_n}.
\end{align*}

We first prove \eqref{eqn:S_A1_bound}. By \eqref{eqn:uniform_second_moment_G} in
Lemma~\ref{lemma:prop_piecewise},
\begin{align*}
    \left\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t')\right\|_F
    \leq T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}\|\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t'\|_F
    = T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}\|\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\|^2
    = O(1).
\end{align*}
By \eqref{eqn: Omega_population}, we have $\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}=\mathbf{A}_{1m}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t')\right)\mathbf{A}_{1n}'$. Hence, by (ME1), 
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2
    \leq \big\| \mathbf{A}_{1m} \big\|_F^2
    \left\|T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t')\right\|_F^2
    \big\| \mathbf{A}_{1n} \big\|_F^2
    =O\bigl(p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}\bigr),
\end{align*}
which proves \eqref{eqn:S_A1_bound}.

We next prove \eqref{eqn:Delta_A1_oP} and \eqref{eqn:Delta_A1_projected_bound}. By (IC1), for each $m\in[M]$, 
\begin{align*}
    q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{Y}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}
    =\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}+q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}.
\end{align*}
Substituting these identities into the definitions of $\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_1,mn}$ gives 
\begin{align*}
    \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn} = \widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_1,mn} - \mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn} 
    = \mathcal{I}_{A_1,1}+\mathcal{I}_{A_1,2}+\mathcal{I}_{A_1,3}+\mathcal{I}_{A_1,4}, 
\end{align*}
where
\begin{alignat*}{2}
    \mathcal{I}_{A_1,1} &:= T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{a}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{a}_t^{(n)'} - \mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}, \qquad
    &&\mathcal{I}_{A_1,2} := (Tq_n)^{-1} \sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}, \\
    \mathcal{I}_{A_1,3} &:= (Tq_m)^{-1} \sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\mathbf{a}_t^{(n)'}, \qquad
    &&\mathcal{I}_{A_1,4} := (T q_m q_n)^{-1} \sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}.
\end{alignat*}
We bound the four components separately. Throughout the remainder of the proof, we repeatedly use the contraction properties \eqref{eqn:projector_contraction} in Proposition~\ref{prop:projector_prop} to remove orthogonal projectors from upper bounds without further mention. By \eqref{eqn: Omega_population}, since $\mathbf{S}_{A_1,mn}=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbbm{E}\left(\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(n)'}\right)$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{I}_{A_1,1}
    =T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \left\{\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(n)'}-\mathbbm{E}\left(\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(n)'}\right)\right\}.
\end{align*}
Using $\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}=\mathbf{A}_{1m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t+\mathbf{A}_{2m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}$ and $\mathbf{a}_t^{(n)}=\mathbf{A}_{1n}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t+\mathbf{A}_{2n}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(n)}$, together with the sample orthogonality and cross-group uncorrelatedness conditions in (IC2), we obtain
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{I}_{A_1,1}=
    \mathbf{A}_{1m}\left\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \left(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t'-\mathbbm{E}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t')\right)\right\}\mathbf{A}_{1n}'
    +\mathbf{A}_{2m}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(n)'}\right)\mathbf{A}_{2n}'.
\end{align*}
It follows from \eqref{eqn:sample_covariance_G} and \eqref{eqn:sample_cross_moment_G} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:prop_piecewise}, together with (ME1) and (ME2), that
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_1,1} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(R(T,d_{r,\star})\left(p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}
    +p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}\right)\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A1_1_full}
\end{align}
Moreover, since $(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\mathbf{A}_{1m}=\0$,
\begin{align*}
    (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_1,1}
    =(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\mathbf{A}_{2m}
    \left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(n)'}\right)\mathbf{A}_{2n}'.
\end{align*}
Thus,
\begin{align}
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_1,1} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A1_1_projected}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{I}_{A_1,2}$, write
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{I}_{A_1,2}
    =\mathbf{A}_{1m}\left\{(Tq_n)^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}\right\}
    +\mathbf{A}_{2m}\left\{(Tq_n)^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}\right\}.
\end{align*}
Applying \eqref{eqn:E1u} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_Delta} to $\{\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\}$ and $\{\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\}$, together with (ME1),
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_1,2} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(T^{-1}\left(p_m^{\gamma_r}
    +p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}\right)p_nq_n^{-1}\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A1_2_full}
\end{align}
Since left-multiplication by $(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})$ eliminates the term involving $\mathbf{A}_{1m}$,
\begin{align*}
    (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_1,2}
    =(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\mathbf{A}_{2m}
    \left\{(Tq_n)^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_n}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(n)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_n}\right\}.
\end{align*}
Consequently,
\begin{align}
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_1,2} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_nq_n^{-1}\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A1_2_projected}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{I}_{A_1,3}$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{I}_{A_1,3}
    =q_m^{-1}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t'\right)\mathbf{A}_{1n}'
    +q_m^{-1}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(n)'}\right)\mathbf{A}_{2n}'.
\end{align*}
Applying \eqref{eqn:E1u} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_Delta} and (ME1) gives
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_1,3} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(T^{-1}p_mq_m^{-1}\left(p_n^{\gamma_r}+p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}\right)\right)
    =O_P\left(T^{-1}p_mp_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}}q_m^{-1}\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A1_3_full}
\end{align}
Consequently,
\begin{align}
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_1,3} \big\|_F^2
    \leq \big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_1,3} \big\|_F^2
    = O_P\Big(T^{-1}p_mp_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}}q_m^{-1}\Big).
    \label{eqn:I_A1_3_projected}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{I}_{A_1,4}$, by \eqref{eqn:Em11En} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_Delta},
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_1,4} \big\|_F^2
    &=O_P\Big(T^{-1}p_mp_nq_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}\Big),
    \label{eqn:I_A1_4_full} \\
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_1,4} \big\|_F^2
    &=O_P\Big(T^{-1}p_mp_nq_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}\Big).
    \label{eqn:I_A1_4_projected}
\end{align}
Using $\big\| \sum_{j=1}^4\mathcal{I}_{A_1,j} \big\|_F^2\lesssim\sum_{j=1}^4\big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_1,j} \big\|_F^2$, together with \eqref{eqn:I_A1_1_full}, \eqref{eqn:I_A1_2_full}, \eqref{eqn:I_A1_3_full}, and \eqref{eqn:I_A1_4_full}, we obtain
\begin{align}
    \big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Bigg(&R(T,d_{r,\star})\left(p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}
    +p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}\right)
    +T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}}p_nq_n^{-1} \nonumber \\
    &+T^{-1}p_mp_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}}q_m^{-1}
    +T^{-1}p_mp_nq_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}\Bigg).
    \label{eqn:Delta_A1_bound}
\end{align}

To deduce \eqref{eqn:Delta_A1_oP}, first note that $T^{-1}=O(R(T,d_{r,\star}))$. Moreover, by (R1), $p_s^{1-\gamma_r}q_s^{-1}$ and $p_s^{1-\gamma_r^{(s)}}q_s^{-1}$ are both $o(1)$ uniformly in $s\in[M]$. Since $p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}} \leq p_m^{\gamma_r}+p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}$, we have
\begin{align*}
    T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}}p_nq_n^{-1}
    &\lesssim R(T,d_{r,\star})
    \Big\{p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}p_n^{1-\gamma_r}q_n^{-1}
    +p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}p_n^{1-\gamma_r^{(n)}}q_n^{-1}\Big\} \\
    &=o\left(R(T,d_{r,\star})
    \big\{p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}
    +p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}\big\}
    \right).
\end{align*}
The term $T^{-1}p_mp_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}}q_m^{-1}$ follows symmetrically, while the term $T^{-1}p_mp_nq_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}$ is of still smaller order since $p_m^{1-\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}=o(1)$. Hence, \eqref{eqn:Delta_A1_bound} reduces to
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(R(T,d_{r,\star})
    \big\{p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}
    +p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}\big\}\right).
\end{align*}
After division by $p_m^{\gamma_r}p_n^{\gamma_r}$, the r.h.s.\ is 
$O_P\left(R(T,d_{r,\star})\{1+\nu_{r,m}\nu_{r,n}\}\right)$. For $m\ne n$,
\begin{align*}
    1+\nu_{r,m}\nu_{r,n}\leq 2(\nu_{r,m}\vee\nu_{r,m}^{-1})(1\vee\nu_{r,n}),
\end{align*}
so (R1) gives $R(T,d_{r,\star})\{1+\nu_{r,m}\nu_{r,n}\}=o(1)$ uniformly over $m\ne n$. This proves \eqref{eqn:Delta_A1_oP}.

Finally, by $T^{-1}=O(R(T,d_{r,\star}))$ and (R1), we have $T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_nq_n^{-1}=o\left(R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}\right)$, while $T^{-1}p_mp_nq_m^{-1}q_n^{-1}=o\left(T^{-1}p_mp_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}}q_m^{-1}\right)$. Combining \eqref{eqn:I_A1_1_projected}, \eqref{eqn:I_A1_2_projected}, \eqref{eqn:I_A1_3_projected}, and \eqref{eqn:I_A1_4_projected}, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m})\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_1,mn} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(
    R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}}
    +T^{-1}p_mp_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}}q_m^{-1}
    \Big),
\end{align*}
which proves \eqref{eqn:Delta_A1_projected_bound}. This completes the proof. $\square$

(Bounding $\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_1,m}^{-1}$)\label{lemma:bound_D_A1}
Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m}, define $\omega_{A_1,m}:=p_m^{\gamma_r}\sum_{n\ne m}p_n^{\gamma_r}$, and let $\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_1,m}$ be defined in \eqref{eqn:Omega_A1_A1_D1}. Then, for each $m\in[M]$,
\begin{align}
    \lambda_{r_1}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_1,m})\asymp \omega_{A_1,m},\qquad
    \lambda_{r_1}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_1,m})\asymp_P \omega_{A_1,m},\qquad
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_1,m}^{-1} \big\|_F^2=O_P(\omega_{A_1,m}^{-2}).
    \label{eqn:D_A1_inv}
\end{align}

(Bounds for $\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}$ and $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}$)\label{lemma:bound_S_A2_Delta_A2}
    For any $m\in[M]$, let $\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_2,m}$ be defined in \eqref{eqn:S_A2}, and define $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}:=\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_2,m}-\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}$. Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:A2m_alphatm}, 
    \begin{align}
        \big\| \mathbf{S}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
        &=O_P(p_m^{2\gamma_r^{(m)}}), 
        \label{eqn:S_A2_bound} \\
        \big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
        &=o_P(p_m^{2\gamma_r^{(m)}}).
        \label{eqn:Delta_A2_oP}
    \end{align}
    Furthermore, defining $\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m}:=\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}\widecheck{\mathbf{A}}_{2m}'$, we have
    \begin{align}
        \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
        =O_P\Big(&R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r+\gamma_r^{(m)}}
        \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r} \nonumber \\
        &+T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_r+1}q_m^{-1}
        \max_{n\in[M]}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
        +p_mq_m^{-2}\Big).
        \label{eqn:Delta_A2_projected_bound}
    \end{align}

Recall from \eqref{eqn:S_A2} that
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}
    :=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}, \qquad
    \widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_2,m}
    :=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)'}\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp},
\end{align*}
where $\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}=\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}$ and $\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}=\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}$. Consequently,
\begin{align}
    \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}
    =T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)'}
    \left(\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\right)
    +T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \left(\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)'}-\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)'}\right)
    \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}.
    \label{eqn:Delta_A2_decomposition}
\end{align}

We first prove \eqref{eqn:S_A2_bound}. By \eqref{eqn:uniform_second_moment_G} and \eqref{eqn:sample_covariance_G} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:prop_piecewise}, and (IC2), it follows that
\begin{align}
    T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T
    \mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)'}
    =\mathbf{A}_{1m}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t'\right)\mathbf{A}_{1m}'
    +\mathbf{A}_{2m}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)'}\right)\mathbf{A}_{2m}'.
    \label{eqn:sample_cov_a_m}
\end{align}
Since $\mathbf{A}_{1m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}=\0$,
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}
    =\mathbf{A}_{2m}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)'}\right)\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}.
    \label{eqn:S_A2_rep}
\end{align}
Therefore, by (ME1), Proposition~\ref{prop:projector_prop}, and Lemma~\ref{lemma:prop_piecewise},
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \mathbf{S}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
    \leq\big\| \mathbf{A}_{2m} \big\|_F^4
    \big\| T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)'} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P(p_m^{2\gamma_r^{(m)}}),
\end{align*}
which proves \eqref{eqn:S_A2_bound}.

We next prove \eqref{eqn:Delta_A2_oP} and \eqref{eqn:Delta_A2_projected_bound}. By (IC1), $\widehat{\mathbf{a}}_t^{(m)}-\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}=q_m^{-1}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}$. Using \eqref{eqn:Delta_A2_decomposition}, write
\begin{align*}
    \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}
    =\mathcal{I}_{A_2,1}+\mathcal{I}_{A_2,2}+\mathcal{I}_{A_2,3}+\mathcal{I}_{A_2,4},
\end{align*}
where
\begin{alignat*}{2}
    \mathcal{I}_{A_2,1}
    &:=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)'}
    \left(\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\right), \;\;
    &&\mathcal{I}_{A_2,2}
    :=(Tq_m)^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}, \\
    \mathcal{I}_{A_2,3}
    &:=(Tq_m)^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\mathbf{a}_t^{(m)'}\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}, \;\;
    &&\mathcal{I}_{A_2,4}
    :=(Tq_m^2)^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}.
\end{alignat*}
We bound these four terms separately.


By Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m} and \eqref{eqn:A1_projector_rate},
\begin{align}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(&R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}-\gamma_r}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r} \nonumber \\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{1-\gamma_r}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\Big).
    \label{eqn:A1_projector_explicit_rate}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{I}_{A_2,1}$, since $\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}=\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}-\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}$, together with \eqref{eqn:sample_cov_a_m} and (ME1), we have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_2,1} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left((p_m^{2\gamma_r}+p_m^{2\gamma_r^{(m)}})\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A2_1_full}
\end{align}
Furthermore, since
$(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\mathbf{A}_{2m}=\0$,
\begin{align*}
    (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_2,1}
    =(\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\mathbf{A}_{1m}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t'\right)\mathbf{A}_{1m}'(\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}-\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}).
\end{align*}
Consequently,
\begin{align}
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_2,1} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(p_m^{2\gamma_r}\big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A2_1_projected}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{I}_{A_2,2}$, write
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{I}_{A_2,2}
    &=\mathbf{A}_{1m}\left\{(Tq_m)^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\right\}\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\\
    &\quad\;+\mathbf{A}_{2m}\left\{(Tq_m)^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}'\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)'}\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\right\}\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}.
\end{align*}
Applying \eqref{eqn:E1u} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_Delta}, together with (ME1) and Proposition~\ref{prop:projector_prop}, gives
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_2,2} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(T^{-1}p_mq_m^{-1}(p_m^{\gamma_r}+p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}})\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A2_2_full}
\end{align}
Since left-multiplication by $\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m}$ eliminates the term involving
$\mathbf{A}_{2m}$,
\begin{align}
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_2,2} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_r+1}q_m^{-1}\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A2_2_projected}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{I}_{A_2,3}$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{I}_{A_2,3}
    &=q_m^{-1}\left\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t'\right\}
    \mathbf{A}_{1m}'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\\
    &\quad\;+q_m^{-1}\left\{T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbf{M}_{p_m}\mathbf{E}_t^{(m)}\1_{q_m}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)'}\right\}
    \mathbf{A}_{2m}'\widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}.
\end{align*}
By \eqref{eqn:E1u} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_Delta}, (ME1), and Proposition~\ref{prop:projector_prop},
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_2,3} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(T^{-1}p_mq_m^{-1}(p_m^{\gamma_r}+p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}})\right)
    =O_P\left(T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}+1}q_m^{-1}\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A2_3_full}
\end{align}
The same bound remains valid after left-multiplication by $\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m}$, and hence
\begin{align}
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_2,3} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\left(T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}+1}q_m^{-1}\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A2_3_projected}
\end{align}

For $\mathcal{I}_{A_2,4}$, by \eqref{eqn:Em11Em} in Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_Delta} and Proposition~\ref{prop:projector_prop},
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_2,4} \big\|_F^2
    &=O_P\left(p_mq_m^{-2}+T^{-1}p_m^2q_m^{-2}\right),
    \label{eqn:I_A2_4_full}\\
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\mathcal{I}_{A_2,4} \big\|_F^2
    &=O_P\left(p_mq_m^{-2}+T^{-1}p_m^2q_m^{-2}\right).
    \label{eqn:I_A2_4_projected}
\end{align}
Using $\big\| \sum_{j=1}^4\mathcal{I}_{A_2,j} \big\|_F^2\lesssim\sum_{j=1}^4\big\| \mathcal{I}_{A_2,j} \big\|_F^2$, together with \eqref{eqn:I_A2_1_full}, \eqref{eqn:I_A2_2_full}, \eqref{eqn:I_A2_3_full}, and \eqref{eqn:I_A2_4_full}, we obtain
\begin{align}
    \big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(
    &(p_m^{2\gamma_r}+p_m^{2\gamma_r^{(m)}})
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2
    \nonumber\\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}+1}q_m^{-1}
    +p_mq_m^{-2}+T^{-1}p_m^2q_m^{-2}\Big).
    \label{eqn:Delta_A2_bound}
\end{align}

To deduce \eqref{eqn:Delta_A2_oP}, Theorem~\ref{thm:initial_A1m} and \eqref{eqn:A1_projector_rate} give
\begin{align*}
    \quad\;\big(1+\nu_{r,m}^{-2}\big)
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{P}}_{A_1,m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Bigg(&R(T,d_{r,\star})(\nu_{r,m}+\nu_{r,m}^{-1})
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{r,n} \\
    &+\frac{1}{T}\left\{\frac{p_m^{1-\gamma_r}}{q_m}
    +\frac{p_m^{1-\gamma_r^{(m)}}}{q_m}\nu_{r,m}^{-1}\right\}
    \left(1\vee\max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}\nu_{r,n}\right)\Bigg)
    =o_P(1),
\end{align*}
where the last equality follows from (R1) and $T^{-1}=O(R(T,d_{r,\star}))$. Moreover, 
\begin{align*}
    T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(m)}+1-2\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}
    =T^{-1}p_m^{1-\gamma_r^{(m)}}q_m^{-1}(1\vee\nu_{r,m}^{-1})=o(1),
\end{align*}
while the remaining two terms in \eqref{eqn:Delta_A2_bound}, after division by $p_m^{2\gamma_r^{(m)}}$, are also $o(1)$ by (R1). Hence \eqref{eqn:Delta_A2_oP} follows.

Finally, combining \eqref{eqn:I_A2_1_projected}, \eqref{eqn:I_A2_2_projected}, \eqref{eqn:I_A2_3_projected}, and \eqref{eqn:I_A2_4_projected} with \eqref{eqn:A1_projector_explicit_rate}, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    \big\| (\mathbf{I}_{p_m}-\mathbf{P}_{A_2,m})\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
    =O_P\Big(&R(T,d_{r,\star})p_m^{\gamma_r+\gamma_r^{(m)}}
    \max_{n\in[M]\setminus\{m\}}p_n^{\gamma_r^{(n)}-\gamma_r}\\
    &+T^{-1}p_m^{\gamma_r+1}q_m^{-1}
    \max_{n\in[M]}p_n^{\gamma_{r,\max}^{(n)}-\gamma_r}
    +p_mq_m^{-2}\Big),
\end{align*}
where $T^{-1}p_m^2q_m^{-2}$ is absorbed into the second term under
(R1). This proves \eqref{eqn:Delta_A2_projected_bound}, and completes the proof. $\square$

(Bounding $\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_2,m}^{-1}$)\label{lemma:bound_D_A2}
    Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:A2m_alphatm}, define $\omega_{A_2,m}:=p_m^{2\gamma_r^{(m)}}$, and let $\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_2,m}$ be defined in \eqref{eqn:Omega_A2_A2_D2}. Then, for each $m\in[M]$,
    \begin{align}
        \lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m})\asymp_P \omega_{A_2,m},\qquad
        \lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m})\asymp_P \omega_{A_2,m},\qquad
        \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_2,m}^{-1} \big\|_F^2=O_P(\omega_{A_2,m}^{-2}).
        \label{eqn:D_A2_inv}
    \end{align}

By Lemma~\ref{lemma:prop_piecewise}, (S1), and Weyl's inequality,
\begin{align*}
    \lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)'}\right)\asymp_P\lambda_1\left(T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^T\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)}\boldsymbol{\alpha}_t^{(m)'}\right)\asymp_P 1.
\end{align*}
Since $\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}=\mathbf{A}_{1m}^{\perp}\mathbf{A}_{1m}^{\perp'}$,
\begin{align*}
    &\quad\;(\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{A}_{2m})^{-1/2}\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\mathbf{A}_{2m}(\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{A}_{2m})^{-1/2} \\
    &=\left\{\mathbf{A}_{1m}^{\perp'}\mathbf{A}_{2m}(\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{A}_{2m})^{-1/2}\right\}'
    \left\{\mathbf{A}_{1m}^{\perp'}\mathbf{A}_{2m}(\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{A}_{2m})^{-1/2}\right\}.
\end{align*}
By (S1), (ME1), and Proposition~\ref{prop:projector_prop}, this gives
\begin{align*}
    \lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}(\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\mathbf{A}_{2m})
    \asymp\lambda_1(\mathbf{A}_{2m}'\mathbf{P}_{A_1,m}^{\perp}\mathbf{A}_{2m})
    \asymp p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, (ME1) gives
\begin{align*}
    \lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m})
    \asymp_P\lambda_1(\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m})
    \asymp_P p_m^{2\gamma_r^{(m)}}
    =\omega_{A_2,m}.
\end{align*}
Let $\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}:=\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_2,m}-\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}$. Since $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}=\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_2,m}\widehat{\mathbf{S}}_{A_2,m}'$ and $\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m}=\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}'$,
\begin{align*}
    \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m}
    =\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}'+\mathbf{S}_{A_2,m}\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}'+\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m}'.
\end{align*}
Lemma~\ref{lemma:bound_S_A2_Delta_A2} gives $\big\| \mathbf{S}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F=O_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}})$ and $\big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F=o_P(p_m^{\gamma_r^{(m)}})$. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F
    \lesssim\big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F\big\| \mathbf{S}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F+\big\| \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F^2
    =o_P(p_m^{2\gamma_r^{(m)}})
    =o_P(\omega_{A_2,m}).
\end{align*}
By Weyl's inequality,
\begin{align*}
    \big|\lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m})-\lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m})\big|\leq\big\| \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m}-\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{A_2,m} \big\|_F
    =o_P(\omega_{A_2,m}),
\end{align*}
so that $\lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m})\asymp_P\omega_{A_2,m}$. Thus, $\widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_2,m}$ is invertible with probability tending to one and, by \eqref{eqn:Omega_A2_A2_D2},
\begin{align*}
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{D}}_{A_2,m}^{-1} \big\|_F^2
    =\sum_{i=1}^{r_2^{(m)}}\lambda_i^{-2}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m})
    \leq r_2^{(m)}\lambda_{r_2^{(m)}}^{-2}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{A_2,m})
    =O_P(\omega_{A_2,m}^{-2}),
\end{align*}
which completes the proof. $\square$

By construction, we may decompose
\begin{align}
    \widehat{\mathbf{G}}_{t,m}
    &=
    \mathbf{H}_{Q,m}' \widecheck{\mathbf{Q}}_m' \mathbf{Q}_m \mathbf{G}_t \mathbf{J}_m' \widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m \mathbf{H}_{J,m}
    + \mathcal{R}_{Q,m}
    + \mathcal{R}_{J,m}
    + \mathcal{R}_{E,m} + \mathcal{R}_{\text{local},m},
    \label{eqn: projected_core_decomp} 
\end{align}
where
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{R}_{Q,m} &:=
    \big( \widehat{\mathbf{Q}}_m - \widecheck{\mathbf{Q}}_m \mathbf{H}_{Q,m} \big)'
    \big( \mathbf{Q}_m \mathbf{G}_t\mathbf{J}_m' + \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{M}_{q_m} 
    + \mathbf{R}_m\mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m'
    - \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m'
    \big) \widehat{\mathbf{J}}_m , \notag 
    \\
    \mathcal{R}_{J,m} &:=
    \mathbf{H}_{Q,m}' \widecheck{\mathbf{Q}}_m' \big( \mathbf{Q}_m \mathbf{G}_t \mathbf{J}_m'
    + \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{M}_{q_m}
    + \mathbf{R}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m'
    - \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m' \big)
    \big( \widehat{\mathbf{J}}_m - \widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m \mathbf{H}_{J,m} \big) , \notag
    \\
    \mathcal{R}_{E,m} &:=
    \mathbf{H}_{Q,m}' \widecheck{\mathbf{Q}}_m' \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{M}_{q_m} \widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m \mathbf{H}_{J,m} , \notag
    \\
    \mathcal{R}_{\text{local},m} &:=
    \mathbf{H}_{Q,m}' \widecheck{\mathbf{Q}}_m'
    \big( \mathbf{R}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m'
    - \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m' \big)
    \widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m \mathbf{H}_{J,m} . \notag
\end{align*}


Note that $\widecheck{\mathbf{Q}}_m$ and $\widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m$ have orthonormal columns, $\mathbf{H}_{Q,m}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{J,m}$ are asymptotically orthogonal, and $\widehat{\mathbf{J}}_m$ has orthonormal columns.
Hence we have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \widecheck{\mathbf{Q}}_m \big\| =
    \big\| \widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m \big\| =
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{J}}_m \big\| = 1,
    \quad
    \big\| \mathbf{H}_{Q,m} \big\| =
    \big\| \mathbf{H}_{J,m} \big\| = O_P(1).
    \label{eqn: aligned_core_norm_properties}
\end{align}
Then consider $\mathcal{R}_{E,m}$. As the columns of $\widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m\mathbf{H}_{J,m}$ are asymptotically orthonormal, by \eqref{eqn: unit_vector_error_bound}, it holds that
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{R}_{E,m} \big\|_F^2
    &\leq
    \big\| \mathbf{H}_{Q,m}' \widecheck{\mathbf{Q}}_m' \big\|^2
    \cdot  \big\| \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{M}_{q_m} \widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m \mathbf{H}_{J,m} \big\|_F^2
    \lesssim_P
    \min(p_m,q_m) ,
    \label{eqn: aligned_core_RE_rate}
\end{align}
where the equality follows from \eqref{eqn: aligned_core_norm_properties} and the fact that $\big\| \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{M}_{q_m} \widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m \mathbf{H}_{J,m} \big\|_F^2  = O_P(p_m)$ as in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init}, and a symmetrical argument which also gives the rate in $O_P(q_m)$.


Next, consider $\mathcal{R}_{\text{local},m}$.
By Theorem~\ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init}, we have
\begin{align}
    &\;\quad
    \big\| \mathbf{R}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m' - \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m' \big\|_F^2  \notag \\
    &\lesssim 
    \big\| \mathbf{R}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m' -
    \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m  \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{H}_{C,m}  \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m' \big\|_F^2  
    + \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big( \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big) \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m' \big\|_F^2  \notag \\
    &=
    \big\| \mathbf{R}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m' -
    \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m  \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \big( \mathbf{R}_m'\mathbf{R}_m \big)^{1/2} \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \big( \mathbf{C}_m'\mathbf{C}_m \big)^{1/2} \mathbf{H}_{C,m}  \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m' \big\|_F^2  \notag \\
    &\;\quad
    + \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big( \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big) \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m' \big\|_F^2  \notag \\
    &\lesssim_P
    \big\| \mathbf{R}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m' -
    \mathbf{R}_m (\mathbf{R}_m'\mathbf{R}_m)^{-1/2} \big( \mathbf{R}_m'\mathbf{R}_m \big)^{1/2} \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \big( \mathbf{C}_m'\mathbf{C}_m \big)^{1/2} \big( \mathbf{C}_m'\mathbf{C}_m \big)^{-1/2}  \mathbf{C}_m' \big\|_F^2  \notag \\
    &\;\quad
    + \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big( \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big) \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m' \big\|_F^2  \notag \\
    &\lesssim_P
    \big\| \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \big( \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} - \mathbf{H}_{R,m}^{-1} \widecheck{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{H}_{C,m} \big) \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m' \big\|_F^2  
    \lesssim_P
    p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \big( \Delta_{R,m} + \Delta_{C,m} + \Delta_{F,m} \big) ,
    \label{eqn: aligned_core_local_error_bound}
\end{align}
where $\Delta_{R,m}$, $\Delta_{C,m}$ and $\Delta_{F,m}$ are defined in the statement of Theorem~\ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init}.
Combining \eqref{eqn: aligned_core_norm_properties} and \eqref{eqn: aligned_core_local_error_bound},
we thus have
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{R}_{\text{local},m} \big\|_F^2
    &\leq
    \big\| \mathbf{H}_{Q,m}' \widecheck{\mathbf{Q}}_m' \big\|^2 
    \cdot \big\| \mathbf{R}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m'
    - \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m'
    \big\|_F^2 
    \cdot \big\| \widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m \mathbf{H}_{J,m} \big\|^2 \notag \\
    &\lesssim_P
    p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \big( \Delta_{R,m} + \Delta_{C,m} + \Delta_{F,m} \big) .
    \label{eqn: aligned_core_Rlocal_rate}
\end{align}


It remains to consider $\mathcal{R}_{Q,m}$ and $\mathcal{R}_{J,m}$.
For $\mathcal{R}_{Q,m}$, by Assumptions~(F1), (L1), \eqref{eqn:Em11Em}, and \eqref{eqn: aligned_core_local_error_bound}, 
we have
\begin{align*}
    &\;\quad
    \big\| \mathbf{Q}_m \mathbf{G}_t \mathbf{J}_m'
    + \mathbf{M}_{p_m} \mathbf{E}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{M}_{q_m}
    + \mathbf{R}_m \mathbf{F}_t^{(m)} \mathbf{C}_m'
    - \widehat{\mathbf{R}}_m \widehat{\mathbf{F}}_t^{(m)} \widehat{\mathbf{C}}_m' \big\|_F^2 \notag \\
    &\lesssim_P
    p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}
    + p_m q_m
    + p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \big( \Delta_{R,m} + \Delta_{C,m} + \Delta_{F,m} \big) ,
\end{align*}
which, combined with Lemma~\ref{lemma: common_comp_global_local_init}
and \eqref{eqn: aligned_core_norm_properties},
give
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{R}_{Q,m} \big\|_F^2
    &\lesssim_P
    \eta_{Q,m} \,
    \Big\{
    p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}
    + p_m q_m
    + p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \big( \Delta_{R,m} + \Delta_{C,m} + \Delta_{F,m} \big)
    \Big\} .
    \label{eqn: aligned_core_RQ_rate}
\end{align}
Similarly, $\mathcal{R}_{J,m}$ satisfies that
\begin{align}
    \big\| \mathcal{R}_{J,m} \big\|_F^2
    &\lesssim_P
    \eta_{J,m} \,
    \Big\{
    p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}
    + p_m q_m
    + p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}} q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \big( \Delta_{R,m} + \Delta_{C,m} + \Delta_{F,m} \big) \Big\} .
    \label{eqn: aligned_core_RJ_rate}
\end{align}


Finally, combining
\eqref{eqn: projected_core_decomp},
\eqref{eqn: aligned_core_RE_rate}, 
\eqref{eqn: aligned_core_Rlocal_rate}, 
\eqref{eqn: aligned_core_RQ_rate}, and \eqref{eqn: aligned_core_RJ_rate}, we conclude that
\begin{align*}
    &\;\quad
    \Big\| \widehat{\mathbf{G}}_{t,m} - \mathbf{H}_{Q,m}' \widecheck{\mathbf{Q}}_m' \mathbf{Q}_m \mathbf{G}_t \mathbf{J}_m' \widecheck{\mathbf{J}}_m \mathbf{H}_{J,m} \Big\|_F^2 \\
    &\lesssim_P
    \min(p_m, q_m)
    + p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \big( \Delta_{R,m} + \Delta_{C,m} +\Delta_{F,m} \big) \\
    &\;\quad
    + \big( \eta_{Q,m} + \eta_{J,m} \big) \Big\{
    p_m^{\delta_{q,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{j,1}^{(m)}}
    + p_m q_m
    + p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \big( \Delta_{R,m} + \Delta_{C,m} + \Delta_{F,m} \big)
    \Big\} \\
    &\lesssim_P
    \min(p_m, q_m)
    + p_m^{\delta_{r,1}^{(m)}}
    q_m^{\delta_{c,1}^{(m)}}
    \big( \Delta_{R,m} + \Delta_{C,m} +\Delta_{F,m} \big) 
    + \big( \eta_{Q,m} + \eta_{J,m} \big) p_m q_m ,
\end{align*}
which completes the proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma: aligned_projected_cores}.
$\square$

Recall from Step~3 of Section~\ref{subsec:estimation} the definition of the notation $\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{W})$ for a block matrix $\mathbf{W} = (\mathbf{W}_{ij})_{i\in[k_c], j\in[k_c]}$, with each sub-matrix $\mathbf{W}_{ij}$ of size $k_r\times k_r$, such that
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{R}(\mathbf{W}) = (\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{W}_{11}),\ldots,\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{W}_{k_c1}),\ldots,\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{W}_{1k_c}),\ldots,\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\mathbf{W}_{k_ck_c})) .
\end{align*}
Then note that each sub-matrix in $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Xi}}_{J,m} \otimes \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Xi}}_{Q,m}$ is exactly the corresponding element in $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Xi}}_{J,m}$ multiplied by the matrix $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Xi}}_{Q,m}$;
hence the vectorization of such sub-matrix would be the corresponding element in $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Xi}}_{J,m}$ multiplied by $\textnormal{\textbf{vec}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Xi}}_{Q,m}) = \widehat{\sigma}_{\Xi,m}^{1/2} \widehat{\mathbf{u}}_{\Xi,m}$ by construction.
Together with the construction of $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Xi}}_{J,m}$, we complete the proof of this lemma.
$\square$

\label{lemma: aligned_projected_cores_rot_rate}
Let all assumptions in Lemma~\ref{lemma: common_comp_global_local_init} hold,
and recall the matrices $\mathbf{H}_{Q,m}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{J,m}$ within.
Then 
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{H}_{Q,m}' \mathbf{H}_{Q,m} &=
    \mathbf{I}_{k_r}
    + O_P\big( \eta_{Q,m}^{1/2} \big) , \quad
    \mathbf{H}_{J,m}' \mathbf{H}_{J,m} =
    \mathbf{I}_{k_c}
    + O_P\big( \eta_{J,m}^{1/2} \big)  ,
\end{align*}
where $\eta_{Q,m}$ and $\eta_{J,m}$ are defined in Theorem~\ref{thm:common_comp_global_local_init}.
